{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/","identifier":"content:causality-home","@type":"WebPage","name":"Some questions need another description.","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"description":"A mathematical programme and learning architectures for systems that can change their descriptions and organisation.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://causality.in/research/"}],"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Some questions need another description.\nA model can answer every question its language permits and still miss the relation the problem needs. Causality asks how descriptions can change without losing account of what they preserve, forget and make possible.\nA dark field of teal points with faint currents curving through it.\nWhen the frame becomes too small.\nMore accurate estimates cannot supply a distinction the representation cannot express. MONAD seeks a calculus for changing that representation, including the questions and transformations it admits.\nA dense field of blue-white points threaded with fine curving lines.\nExperience should change what comes next.\nA useful memory is more than a growing record. Reckon studies which experience changes later outcomes, what deserves to survive a finite budget and how to examine the difference.\nTeal points scattered around a darker, emptier centre.\nIntelligence between the parts.\nMUON investigates the adaptive local unit. HENA asks how many such units can learn to communicate, remember and specialise, making their organisation part of the computation.\nGolden points gathered into one dense, ring-like cloud.\nAn organisation that can learn.\nPale makes responsibilities, capabilities and coordination explicit, and HENA explores whether more of that organisation could arise from intent through local interactions, a direction the About page sets out.\nSparse rose points carried along long, sweeping currents.\nAnother substrate. The same ambition.\nQ-MUON and Q-HENA take the local-unit and collective questions into quantum computation. Classical and quantum paths develop alongside each other, exploring what each can contribute.\nCyan points with brighter currents winding through the dark.\nA calculus for changing descriptions.\nArchitectures that can learn their organisation.\nWhat becomes possible when both can grow?\nExplore the research\nViolet currents flowing almost alone, with only a few faint points left.\n","image":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://causality.in/assets/renditions/relation-field/scene-0.webp","description":"A dark field of teal points with faint currents curving through it."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://causality.in/assets/renditions/relation-field/scene-1.webp","description":"A dense field of blue-white points threaded with fine curving lines."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://causality.in/assets/renditions/relation-field/scene-2.webp","description":"Teal points scattered around a darker, emptier centre."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://causality.in/assets/renditions/relation-field/scene-3.webp","description":"Golden points gathered into one dense, ring-like cloud."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://causality.in/assets/renditions/relation-field/scene-4.webp","description":"Sparse rose points carried along long, sweeping currents."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://causality.in/assets/renditions/relation-field/scene-5.webp","description":"Cyan points with brighter currents winding through the dark."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://causality.in/assets/renditions/relation-field/scene-6.webp","description":"Violet currents flowing almost alone, with only a few faint points left."}],"url":"https://causality.in/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"},"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:causality","name":"Causality","description":"A mathematical programme and learning architectures for systems that can change their descriptions and organisation.","brand":{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/hasPart":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/#entity"}],"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/","identifier":"content:causality-home","@type":"WebPage","name":"Some questions need another description.","url":"https://causality.in/"}]}]},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#website","@type":"WebSite","name":"Causality","url":"https://causality.in/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"},"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"}],"usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"}},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand","@type":"Brand","identifier":"brand:causality","name":"Causality","url":"https://causality.in/","subjectOf":{"@id":"https://causality.in/#website"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#brand"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:causality","name":"Causality"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/hasPart":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:monad","name":"MONAD"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:muon","name":"MUON"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:hena","name":"HENA"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:q-muon","name":"Q-MUON"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:q-hena","name":"Q-HENA"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:reckon","name":"Reckon","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux"}]},{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/#brand","@type":"Brand","identifier":"brand:post-agi-economics","name":"Post-AGI Economics","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/","subjectOf":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/#website"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#brand"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:post-agi-economics","name":"Post-AGI Economics"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/hasPart":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/#entity","@type":"CreativeWork","identifier":"project:one-yardstick","name":"One Yardstick"}]},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#brand","@type":"Brand","identifier":"brand:rawthoughts","name":"RawThoughts","description":"The applied engineering, consulting and publishing brand of RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited.","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","subjectOf":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#website"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/hasPart":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"},{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/#brand"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:pale","name":"Pale","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:scope","name":"Scope","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/counsel/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:counsel","name":"Counsel","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:gazette","name":"Gazette","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/weave/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:weave","name":"Weave","applicationCategory":"BusinessApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/rawfish/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:rawfish","name":"RawFish","applicationCategory":"BusinessApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux"}]},{"@id":"https://reve.fashion/#brand","@type":"Brand","identifier":"brand:reve","name":"Rêve","http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://reve.fashion/#project","@type":"Project","identifier":"project:reve","name":"Rêve"}},{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/#brand","@type":"Brand","identifier":"brand:sleepwalker","name":"Sleepwalker","url":"https://sleepwalker.in/","subjectOf":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/#website"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/#project","@type":"Project","identifier":"project:sleepwalker","name":"Sleepwalker"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/hasPart":[{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/#entity","@type":"Book","identifier":"project:sleepwalker-graphic-novel","name":"Sleepwalker"}]},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}],"brand":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"},{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/#brand"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#brand"},{"@id":"https://reve.fashion/#brand"},{"@id":"https://sleepwalker.in/#brand"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/about/","identifier":"content:causality-about","@type":"AboutPage","name":"About Causality","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"description":"How Causality connects changing descriptions, learning within a unit and learning among units, with experiments that test what helps.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://causality.in/hena/"},{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://causality.in/monad/"},{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://causality.in/muon/"},{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"text":"Can a law depend not only on a path’s past, but on where that path can go? Causality follows that question into the descriptions we use: what would let a system change the way it represents a problem, the questions it can ask and how it organises its computation?\nA system can change its state, learn a skill or revise its declared world while keeping the language of that world fixed. Changing the language reaches another level: the same old description may now mean something different. Changing how the system draws distinctions reaches deeper still. Causality joins mathematical and architectural research across that ladder, asking what each change preserves and what it makes possible.\nThe calculus and its experiments\nThe mathematical centre\nPale holds a declared frame fixed while work proceeds inside it. MONAD asks what would let that frame change: what a description can tell apart, what it loses and what happens when it needs new questions or rules of change. Its proof library establishes particular cases: which answers a summary keeps, what another observation can recover and how a finite budget limits the next question. Those results give the larger calculus something exact to build on. Changing a rule without moving the finish line makes one boundary concrete: a system may improve its way of working without quietly changing what counts as success.\nMONAD\nOne reasoning unit\nMUON investigates one adaptive reasoning unit: how earlier input changes its next answer, what it remembers and what it learns. Stock models come first behind a common interface, as the baseline for comparison; a native micro-transformer unit is the long-term building block.\nMUON\nMany units\nHENA investigates intelligence arising among units: learned sparse communication, limited distributed memory and emergent hierarchy. Organisation becomes something the architecture learns, rather than only an arrangement a designer supplies.\nHENA\nWhat would a learned architecture have to improve?\nPale gives people and agents an explicit account of their work: who is responsible, what each participant may do and which context a task needs. Its local compiler checks that declared account. HENA asks a further question: could useful organisation emerge and adapt through local interactions, so less of it has to be prescribed?\nCounsel supplies local judgments within Pale’s organisation. MUON and HENA investigate those two scales as a learning architecture: the unit and the collective. HENA is Pale’s research successor: if its learned organisation proves useful, it could take over some of the coordination Pale describes explicitly. Pale, which sits around the coding agents and applications people already use, would then stand to it as a harness stands to the model it runs, more guardrail than organiser, still controlling permissions and external actions. HENA needs MUON’s unit and MONAD’s account of how a description can change, and the more the system organises itself, the more it depends on both. The mechanism remains open, and Pale’s own usefulness does not wait for it. In Harsh’s account HENA is an evolving swarm of MUONs that reasons about what could have happened, the counterfactual rung of the ACI framework in Beyond the Mirage. The products come first because they let agents see what a change would affect before they make it.\nKeep the experience that helps. Explore the substrates that could carry it.\nReckon studies which retained experience helps later work. Its laboratory compares memory policies under a budget. Reckon’s design also preserves an experiment’s inputs for reuse, compares improvements to everyday tools as well as Causality research candidates, and keeps the decision an owner makes from the result. Running an evaluation and deciding to use its candidate remain separate acts. For everyday tools Reckon works from Pale’s history of what each piece of work relied on and what followed; without that history it has nothing to compare.\nQ-MUON and Q-HENA explore the unit and collective architectures through quantum states, gates, channels and entanglement-mediated memory. Both classical and quantum paths are permanent peers. A different substrate opens research possibilities; its useful contribution has to be found, not assumed.\nPlanning over predictions, whole-trajectory constraints and equations with future states give the originating intuition different mathematical forms. The endpoint example follows what information each form requires.\nCausality is a research programme within RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited, authored by Harsh Joshi.\n","url":"https://causality.in/about/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:causality","name":"Causality","description":"A mathematical programme and learning architectures for systems that can change their descriptions and organisation.","brand":{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/hasPart":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/#entity"}],"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/","identifier":"content:causality-home","@type":"WebPage","name":"Some questions need another description.","url":"https://causality.in/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/","identifier":"collection:causality-essays","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Blog","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":7,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/future-without-retrocausation/#article","identifier":"content:monad-future-without-retrocausation","name":"The future can constrain a model without sending a message back","url":"https://causality.in/blog/future-without-retrocausation/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/when-the-support-moves/#article","identifier":"content:monad-when-the-support-moves","name":"When the support moves","url":"https://causality.in/blog/when-the-support-moves/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/when-effects-cancel/#article","identifier":"content:monad-influence-is-not-transitive","name":"When effects cancel along a path","url":"https://causality.in/blog/when-effects-cancel/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":4,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/the-record-and-the-arrow/#article","identifier":"content:monad-the-record-and-the-arrow","name":"The record and the arrow","url":"https://causality.in/blog/the-record-and-the-arrow/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":5,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/beyond-the-mirage/#article","identifier":"content:causality-beyond-the-mirage","name":"Beyond the Mirage: a framework for AGI classification and controllable progress","url":"https://causality.in/blog/beyond-the-mirage/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":6,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/entropy-and-a-learning-mind/#article","identifier":"content:causality-entropy-and-a-learning-mind","name":"Entropy and a learning mind","url":"https://causality.in/blog/entropy-and-a-learning-mind/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":7,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/the-ineffable-geometry/#article","identifier":"content:causality-the-ineffable-geometry","name":"The ineffable geometry","url":"https://causality.in/blog/the-ineffable-geometry/"}}],"itemListOrder":"https://schema.org/ItemListOrderDescending"},"description":"Worked questions about distinctions, causal evidence and learning under limits.","text":"","url":"https://causality.in/blog/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]}},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/beyond-the-mirage/#article","identifier":"content:causality-beyond-the-mirage","@type":"Article","name":"Beyond the Mirage: a framework for AGI classification and controllable progress","headline":"Beyond the Mirage: a framework for AGI classification and controllable progress","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["Agentic Causal Intelligence","AGI classification","Causal Barrier","causal reasoning","AI agency","causal benchmarks","emergent abilities","AI controllability"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/beyond-the-mirage/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Beyond the Mirage: a framework for AGI classification and controllable progress","url":"https://causality.in/blog/beyond-the-mirage/"},"description":"The proposed Agentic Causal Intelligence framework separates causal reasoning from autonomy, then asks what evidence and controls each capability needs.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2025-09-24","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Beyond the Mirage: a framework for AGI classification and controllable progress\nAn AI can give an impressive answer about the world without knowing what would happen if it changed that world. It can also act through tools without understanding the consequences of its actions. These are different gaps. Counting parameters does not tell us which one has been crossed.\nThis framework proposes judging progress towards AGI by verifiable changes in capability: from recognising associations to reasoning about interventions and counterfactuals, and from answering a prompt to pursuing a goal. The Agentic Causal Intelligence framework, or ACI, puts those two dimensions on separate axes. The safety roadmap alongside it asks how our ability to verify and constrain a system can keep pace with what it can do.\nThe central claim is that we face a Causal Barrier. Robust causal understanding requires more than fluent reproduction of statistical relationships. The gap is a difference in kind, and crossing it calls for a qualitative change in what a system can represent and reason about. Scaling alone is an inadequate account of that change.\nNote\nThis research proposal dates from September 2025; its examples and judgments about models belong to that setting. The strong architectural thesis, ACI classifications and safety roadmap are hypotheses to test; the reported experiments retain the narrower scope stated beside them.\nWhy bigger isn't necessarily smarter\nLarge language models learn from enormous bodies of text. They are remarkable navigators of its patterns and correlations. That is mastery of statistical entropy: finding a path through a vast landscape of possible language. But reasoning about the world asks which relationships survive an intervention. A convincing continuation of a sentence and a reliable answer about a changed world are different achievements.\nTwo lines of work make parameter count an inadequate answer. The first asks how to spend a training budget. The second asks what a score actually measures.\nChinchilla: size and data must be considered together\nDeepMind's 2022 Chinchilla study compared more than 400 training runs. Its compute-optimal analysis found that model size and training tokens should grow in roughly equal proportions. The resulting 70-billion-parameter Chinchilla used 1.4 trillion tokens and the same training compute as the 280-billion-parameter Gopher. It outperformed Gopher on the reported evaluations; its MMLU accuracy was 67.5%, more than seven percentage points higher.\nParameter count had concealed how the budget was allocated. Training efficiency, data and the cost of using the resulting model belonged in the comparison. The result makes progress an engineering question: what does this allocation of resources actually buy? Its compute-optimal training relation still has to be distinguished from an optimum for a particular inference workload.\nThat is a useful correction to the spectacle of the ever-larger number. Better engineering can change what the same resources buy. For how that possibility reaches an ordinary business, Evolution of AI, and its future in B2B follows the interface through to the work behind it.\nThe mirage of emergence\nSchaeffer, Miranda and Koyejo's 2023 study offered a measurement explanation for apparently abrupt abilities. A strict all-correct score can stay near zero even while the probability of getting each part right improves. Change the metric, and the same outputs can reveal a smoother progression. They examined arithmetic in the GPT-3/InstructGPT family, metric choices in BIG-Bench, and artificial examples of apparent emergence in vision models.\nImagine a task with ten independently correct steps, each succeeding with probability p. Success on the whole task is p^{10}. That curve can make a steady improvement in the parts look like a sudden arrival of useful whole-task performance. The example is a calculation under an independence assumption, not a model of every reasoning process.\nBefore declaring a new kind of intelligence, inspect the metric. The same model outputs can support a story about a sudden leap or a story about steady improvement, depending on how success is counted. That makes measurement part of the explanation, rather than a neutral window onto it.\nTwo axes, rather than one ladder of model sizes\nThe first ACI axis asks what kind of causal question the system can answer. It borrows the distinction between association, intervention and counterfactuals from the causal hierarchy.\nLevel 1, association: seeing. What does observing X=x tell us about Y? The expression P(Y\\mid X=x) describes this question. An observation can be informative without being a cause. Seeing an umbrella may predict rain; making someone carry an umbrella does not thereby make it rain.\nLevel 2, intervention: doing. What happens if X is deliberately set to x? Write this as P(Y\\mid\\operatorname{do}(X=x)). An intervention changes the mechanism determining X. Recovering its effect needs appropriate causal assumptions or experimental information; an observational distribution alone does not generally determine it.\nLevel 3, counterfactuals: imagining otherwise. Given what actually happened, what would have happened under a different action? This combines evidence about the actual situation with a model of the alternative. It asks more than an unconditioned prediction about a new intervention.\nThose are classes of questions and information. Turning them into an assessment of a deployed AI requires named tasks, assumptions and tests. Producing text containing a causal equation does not establish that the system has solved the corresponding problem.\nThe second axis asks how the system acts.\nGrade A, passive or oracle. A system receives an input and returns an answer. In the proposed classification, a base model used for a single response is the straightforward example. Planning described in an answer is different from authority to execute the plan.\nGrade B, reactive or tool-using. The system chooses and uses an external tool within the task. Toolformer is an early research example: it learned when to call APIs, what arguments to supply and how to incorporate the results into subsequent prediction. Retrieval and calculators expand what an answer can use. Their availability does not establish causal understanding of the task.\nGrade C, proactive or agentic. A system pursues a goal through several steps, maintaining context, planning, inspecting results and revising its approach. AutoGPT-like arrangements illustrate this direction. The unit being classified is the whole arrangement, including memory, tools and permissions, rather than its underlying model in isolation.\nThe proposed ACI matrix\nCross the axes and the landscape becomes easier to read. A system can move right by gaining tools and autonomy without moving upwards in causal reasoning. The labels below are proposed placements, with the upper cells describing the capabilities we would need to demonstrate.\nCausal depth\tA: passive / oracle\tB: reactive / tool-using\tC: proactive / agentic\t\n3: counterfactual\tCounterfactual oracle\tReactive counterfactual agent\tProactive counterfactual agent: the proposal's AGI target\t\n2: interventional\tInterventional oracle; JEPA was suggested as a research direction\tReactive interventional agent\tProactive interventional agent\t\n1: associative\tStandard base LLM, illustrated by GPT-3-era use\tTool-using LLM, illustrated by Toolformer or RAG\tAgentic framework, illustrated by AutoGPT-like use\t\nThe ACI matrix places these language-model examples on the associative row. Tool use and a planning loop make them more capable agents, but do not by themselves show that their reasoning has crossed into intervention. That upward move is the immediate frontier the framework asks us to confront.\nPredictive world models are one promising route. LeCun's JEPA proposal asks how a system could learn representations useful for reasoning and planning. It belongs here as a research direction towards an interventional oracle, rather than a certified Level 2 result. A model earns that classification through the questions it can answer.\nThe notional ACI-3C destination is a proactive agent that can reason counterfactually in pursuit of its objectives. That is the framework's AGI target: autonomy joined to a deeper understanding of what could have happened and what an action would change.\nWhat the causal benchmarks actually test\nFluent models can stumble when a causal question becomes unfamiliar, logically constrained or demanding across several perspectives. These failures motivate the Causal Barrier thesis. We need a scorecard that exposes them, rather than one that lets a persuasive answer stand in for understanding.\nStudy\tEvaluated task and population\tRelevant finding\t\nCausalProbe-2024\tFresh-news causal questions; studied models include LLaMA 2 7B, LLaMA 3 8B, GPT-3.5 Turbo and Claude 3 Opus\tPerformance fell relative to older causal QA benchmarks; the authors also tested a knowledge- and goal-assisted method that improved results.\t\nCorr2Cause\tMore than 200,000 examples generated to test causal inference from correlational statements; seventeen evaluated LLMs\tThe authors report near-random performance and limited out-of-distribution generalisation after fine-tuning.\t\nCausalBench, mathematics\tGPT-4 Turbo, as named in Table 2 of the paper\t88.7% of individual questions correct; 61.4% of scenario groups had all four question types correct.\t\nCausalProbe drew news from the BBC and the Guardian dated 1 January–29 April 2024. Its freshness checks reduce a memorisation concern; they do not make every training exposure independently inspectable. Its “level-1” and “level-2” terminology distinguishes recalled associations from the authors' notion of more genuine reasoning, and must not be substituted automatically for the three Pearl rungs.\nCorr2Cause is a test of reasoning under a constructed set of conditions. Its result does not establish that arbitrary real-world correlations identify a causal relationship. The conditions that make a conclusion follow are part of the problem.\nCausalBench's two percentages use different denominators. The lower score counts success on all four perspectives together: cause to effect, effect to cause, and each with intervention. This is a stricter consistency requirement, not a before-and-after decline on the same measure. The paper separately compares causal scores with a summarisation hallucination leaderboard, including Mistral; that association is not an experiment showing causal skill reduces hallucination.\nThese are concrete failures to explain and concrete tests for future systems to improve. The architectural interpretation is that crossing from association to reliable intervention requires a deeper change than another headline model size. The benchmark results locate weaknesses in the tested systems; the architectural interpretation remains the stronger thesis being advanced here.\nA roadmap for verification and control\nWhy make the classification at all? Because an answer, an action and a self-directed sequence of actions create different problems of control. A system that can model consequences more deeply may also find ways around a restriction. The proposal therefore asks that capability and verification develop together.\nYampolskiy's argument about the limits of controlling advanced AI brings the concern into focus: a less capable controller may fail to anticipate or constrain a more capable system. We cannot assume that an output filter suitable for an oracle will also control an agent able to plan around it.\nThe roadmap proposes three layers of work: bounded alignment for associative systems, verifiable process as systems acquire interventional capabilities, and constrained agency for the hypothetical counterfactual agents beyond them. Each layer adds a demand on what we can demonstrate. The roadmap is a research programme, not permission to give a lower-ranked model unchecked tools.\nBounded alignment\nThe first layer tries to keep prompts and responses within stated requirements. Constitutional AI's training approach uses principles to guide critique, revision and preference feedback. Llama Guard 3 is a separate example of a model trained to classify input and output content against a safety taxonomy.\nThese techniques can be evaluated against particular unwanted outputs. They do not turn a policy into a guarantee that every output, downstream interpretation or tool action is safe. Their usefulness depends on the requirements, test population and ways they can fail.\nThe governance side matters too. NIST's AI Risk Management Framework and its July 2024 Generative AI Profile organise risks and responses across a system's lifecycle, including confabulation, harmful bias and information integrity. They give an organisation questions to manage and evidence to maintain, rather than certifying a model's ACI level. The EU AI Act is another governance example, with its own risk-based legal categories and duties; those categories are not the ACI matrix.\nVerifiable process\nChecking only the final answer is insufficient when the system uses tools or handles protected information. Which program ran? What input did it receive? Which outside action occurred? Was the process altered? Hardware isolation, cryptographic proofs and formal verification can each make a different part of that account inspectable.\nTrusted execution environments can isolate a computation from parts of the host under a specified threat model. Intel SGX is one example. Its protections do not cover every side channel, and the application still has to handle vulnerable code, exposed inputs and recovery. Protecting a program from alteration is not the same as proving that its decisions are correct.\nZero-knowledge machine learning proposes proofs that a stated computation was carried out without revealing all the underlying private information. A proof of inference can establish execution of the represented model and operation; it does not establish that the model's answer is true or that the chosen objective is acceptable. Proof generation and the faithful representation of the computation introduce costs and assumptions of their own.\nThe earlier blockchain essay, A precognition court for AI, explores a related intuition: make relevant operations inspectable and constrain what a system can do through the surrounding arrangement. It is a deliberately abstract argument, not a claim that a ledger solves intelligence or control. The useful question remains what is verified, by whom, and what the verification leaves outside its scope.\nConstrained agency\nFor hypothetical systems with greater autonomy and counterfactual planning, we need to look beyond response filters to constraints on what can be done. Containment, interpretability, corrigibility and limits on resources belong in the research, alongside the physical and computational conditions under which an agent operates. The challenge is to find constraints that remain effective when a system can reason about the constraints themselves.\nEnergy gives the idea a concrete example. Computation needs infrastructure and power; deployment cannot be detached from those resources. Comparisons with Bitcoin or a country's electricity use make the scale imaginable, but only if the population, period and projected or observed status match. The IEA's April 2025 report estimated 415 TWh for all data centres in 2024 and projected about 945 TWh in its 2030 base case; neither number measures AI alone. Energy is a physical constraint any deployed intelligence must face. Turning it into a reliable means of control would require a further argument.\nThroughput supplies another analogy. Ethereum has a constrained base layer and a roadmap for scaling through other layers. Its account of danksharding describes a target above 100,000 transactions per second across the scaled system. A globally operating intelligence would also need communication, data movement and infrastructure. Those can become bottlenecks even when local computation improves. The Ethereum number is a roadmap target for that network, not a calculated AGI requirement.\nWhat each layer has to establish\nProposed layer\tRisks motivating the layer\tMain question\tCandidate mechanisms and governance work\t\nBounded alignment, associated with ACI-1\tMisinformation, bias, harmful content, confabulation and intellectual-property infringement\tDo inputs and outputs meet the requirements, and where do they fail?\tConstitutional training, content classifiers, red teaming, risk assessment and accountable organisational review.\t\nVerifiable process, associated with ACI-2\tUnverifiable tool use, process manipulation, data leakage, adversarial attacks and emergent harmful strategies\tDid the stated computation and permitted actions occur under the declared conditions?\tTrusted execution, proofs of computation, formal verification, auditable tools, hardware certification and secure APIs.\t\nConstrained agency, associated with ACI-3\tUncontrollability, goal misalignment, instrumental convergence and existential risk\tWhat can the system do, resist or circumvent, and which limits remain effective?\tContainment, interpretability, corrigibility, resource controls, monitoring and research on coordination between institutions.\t\nThe policy proposal is to make permission to advance and deploy depend on evidence of appropriate controls. Controls cannot remain something we promise to add after capability. That puts regulatory frameworks, hardware certification and secure APIs alongside possible international arrangements over compute and energy, moratoriums and risk monitoring. These are governance proposals to examine; the benchmark studies do not decide the institutional choices for us.\nThe transition the framework asks us to measure\nAGI dates that leave the definition moving miss this distinction. An increasingly useful tool-using or autonomous assistant may be called AGI while leaving the causal threshold unanswered. Conversely, a narrow system can answer a difficult causal question without having general agency. A date means little until the capability and the test are stated.\nThe proposed path is a sequence of demanding phase transitions in what can be explained, predicted and done. The phrase expresses a qualitative change in capability; deriving a mathematical phase transition would need a specified model and parameter. The immediate challenge is to master intervention verifiably, alongside the separate growth of autonomy, and to build the corresponding means of control.\nThe measure of progress should therefore be more than model size or fluent prose. The ambition is robust causal reasoning, demonstrable alignment with the task and control over consequential action. Each needs evidence of its own. A matrix helps only if it makes those demands clearer.\nThe economic question continues in One Yardstick, One Clock and The Red Pill: even when an agent can do the work, how does the economy adapt, and who can buy what it produces? The mathematical question continues through changing descriptions: which distinctions must a model retain to answer the next question? The ineffable geometry and Entropy and a learning mind approach that question from the earlier geometric and learning intuitions.\nYay, you made it to the end. Congratulations.\n“Statistical entropy” expresses the opening intuition. This argument does not define an entropy measure that ranks causal understanding against language modelling.\nHoffmann et al., Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models, 2022, and DeepMind's account of the experiment.\nSchaeffer, Miranda and Koyejo, Are Emergent Abilities of Large Language Models a Mirage?, NeurIPS 2023.\nThe metric result concerns the studied tasks and model families. It does not prove that every future capability is predictable. A smoothly crossed threshold can still change which complete tasks a system can perform.\nShpitser and Pearl, Complete Identification Methods for the Causal Hierarchy, JMLR 9, 2008.\nSchick et al., Toolformer: Language Models Can Teach Themselves to Use Tools, 2023.\nLeCun, A Path Towards Autonomous Machine Intelligence, position paper, 27 June 2022.\nChi et al., Unveiling Causal Reasoning in Large Language Models: Reality or Mirage?, preprint v1, 26 June 2025, especially sections 6.1–6.3 and the limitations.\nJin et al., Can Large Language Models Infer Causation from Correlation?, ICLR 2024.\nWang, CausalBench: A Comprehensive Benchmark for Evaluating Causal Reasoning Capabilities of Large Language Models, SIGHAN-10, 16 August 2024; mathematics Table 2 and the separate hallucination comparison in section 4.\nYampolskiy, On Controllability of AI, 2020.\nCausal depth and access to act are separate in the matrix. A tool-using ACI-1B or proactive ACI-1C arrangement can already have consequential effects. The three research layers therefore do not supply a sufficient control policy for each deployment merely from its causal row.\nBai et al., Constitutional AI: Harmlessness from AI Feedback, 2022.\nMeta, Llama Guard 3 8B model card, describing the 2024 model.\nNIST, Artificial Intelligence Risk Management Framework: Generative Artificial Intelligence Profile, July 2024.\nEuropean Commission, AI Act. The law supplies its own classification and application timetable; this proposal does not infer a legal category from an ACI cell.\nIntel, Intel SGX SDK for Linux overview, including its stated side-channel limitation; consulted 16 November 2026.\nA Survey of Zero-Knowledge Proof Based Verifiable Machine Learning, 2025, reviewing work through December 2024.\nIEA, Energy and AI: executive summary, April 2025. The population is all data centres; the 2030 value is a projection.\nEthereum, Danksharding, consulted 16 November 2026; the quoted capacity is a roadmap goal.\n","url":"https://causality.in/blog/beyond-the-mirage/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/entropy-and-a-learning-mind/#article","identifier":"content:causality-entropy-and-a-learning-mind","@type":"Article","name":"Entropy and a learning mind","headline":"Entropy and a learning mind","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["entropy","neural network priors","continual learning","stability and adaptation","catastrophic forgetting"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/entropy-and-a-learning-mind/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Entropy and a learning mind","url":"https://causality.in/blog/entropy-and-a-learning-mind/","relatedLink":["https://causality.in/blog/beyond-the-mirage/","https://causality.in/blog/the-ineffable-geometry/"]},"description":"A broken mug starts an early question about neural networks: what happens to useful initial intuitions when learning never stops?","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2024-01-04","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Entropy and a learning mind\nA coffee mug can shatter into pieces. We do not expect the pieces to jump back together and become a mug. You do not need to be a physics or mathematics nerd to carry that expectation. Nerds have tools and labels for describing what you already find intuitive.\nThat familiar asymmetry led to a question I wrote about in January 2024. Could a neural network begin with a useful intuition about the problem it faces, rather than having to discover every regularity from scratch? And if it kept learning from a stream of feedback, what would happen to the special arrangement that carried the intuition?\nThe question connects an image from thermodynamics with learning. It is an analogy to investigate, not a thermodynamic law for neural-network weights.\nWhat the mug helps us see\nOne way into entropy is to count the microscopic arrangements consistent with a macroscopic description. In the equal-probability setting, Boltzmann's expression is S=k_B\\log\\Omega, where \\Omega is that count. Many microscopic arrangements can fit what we describe as the shattered mug; the familiar intact arrangement is more constrained. The count depends on the state and description being considered, so “disorder” is an intuition rather than a definition sufficient for every use of entropy.\nThink of a low-entropy arrangement as a special state, then ask why a system tends to leave it. The picture needs a system boundary and assumptions about its dynamics. The second law concerns total entropy for an isolated system; local order can increase while energy and entropy are exchanged with an environment. A refrigerator and a living organism do not contradict it merely by maintaining an organised part.\nStill, the mug makes the temporal intuition vivid. We expect some changes in one direction and regard their reversal as extraordinarily unlikely under ordinary conditions. That expectation informs how we read a cause and its aftermath. It does not, on its own, supply a complete theory of causation.\nBegin with an intuition, then keep learning\nImagine a large model whose initial weights or architecture carry a useful regularity about the task. Perhaps it begins with a bias that helps it recognise the relevant structure, instead of treating every possible pattern alike. The training then continues, in real time, as the system receives new observations and feedback.\nThe weights are an arrangement that makes some responses possible and others unlikely. Will continued adaptation preserve the useful intuition, refine it or wash it out?\nAn aligned prior could make learning more efficient. A mistaken prior could send the model towards a poor solution or make new evidence harder to absorb. Neither consequence follows just from giving the system more information. The initial arrangement, the task and the update rule interact.\nContinued learning could spread those weights into a less special state, perhaps making the model more general. That is a hypothesis about learning dynamics, not something the second law establishes. A weight distribution needs a defined measure before we can speak precisely about its entropy; performance and that measure need not move together. Training also supplies an objective and repeated interventions, rather than leaving an isolated physical system to evolve freely.\nLosing a bias and losing a capability are different changes\nSuppose the initial intuition is helpful for yesterday's task and wrong for today's. Relaxing it might be learning. Suppose it is still needed, but the latest feedback overwrites it. Then the same movement away from the initial state might be forgetting.\nThis makes the question more interesting than whether the weights changed. What did the change let the model do, and what could it no longer do? Can it retain a useful old response while adapting to a new one? Does the cost of preserving the old capability prevent the new learning?\nContinual-learning research already studies this problem through catastrophic forgetting and the balance between stability and adaptation. One example, elastic weight consolidation, slows changes to weights estimated to matter for earlier tasks. It gives a concrete mechanism to compare with the early intuition; it does not establish that all useful knowledge occupies a neatly extractable part of a model.\nThen there is irreversibility. Once training leaves a useful arrangement, will the process naturally return to it? A saved checkpoint can restore parameters, but that is an external recovery procedure, not the learning dynamics reversing themselves. Reaching the same performance later is another claim again: a different internal arrangement might solve the same test.\nA continuously adapting system needs a continuing comparison\nComplexity adds to the difficulty. A change in one part of a network can affect several tasks, and a stream of new feedback can change which skills matter. The model may improve on its immediate objective while losing a property that was important to the larger system. Continuous adaptation is both the attraction and the problem.\nThat makes monitoring more than watching a training loss fall. The comparison needs the useful behaviour we wanted to retain, the new behaviour we wanted to acquire, the resources spent on each and the conditions under which either stops working. Otherwise “more general” becomes a description we apply after the change without a test that distinguishes improvement from drift.\nMUON's account of experience separates a changed immediate state, a retained record and learning across episodes. Reckon's local laboratory supplies a narrower experiment: compare memory rules or budgets on a stated task and inspect what was kept and what happened. They give connected questions forms in which a result can be inspected.\nThe earlier geometry thought experiment asked whether a better language could make causal relationships intelligible. Beyond the Mirage later asks what evidence would distinguish causal understanding from successful association. This essay sits between them with a simpler question: can we build a system around useful intuitions without losing what made them useful as the system changes?\nA mug's fate seems obvious. A learning system is less obliging. We have to define the special state, observe the change and test the capability. That is where the analogy becomes an experiment worth doing.\nThe autocorrect paradox approaches the problem through a decade of building AI products: why does a model keep getting almost good enough, and what work remains before it fits the task?\nDavid Tong, Statistical Physics, lecture notes on statistical mechanics and thermodynamics. The counting expression requires its stated statistical setting; the learning analogy supplies no independent entropy law.\nKirkpatrick et al., Overcoming catastrophic forgetting in neural networks, 2016 preprint, published in PNAS in 2017. The work tests a particular retention method, not universal recovery of earlier knowledge.\n","url":"https://causality.in/blog/entropy-and-a-learning-mind/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/future-without-retrocausation/#article","identifier":"content:monad-future-without-retrocausation","@type":"Article","name":"The future can constrain a model without sending a message back","headline":"The future can constrain a model without sending a message back","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["endpoint constraints","trajectory energy","boundary-value problems","advanced equations","temporal nonlocality","retrocausation"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/future-without-retrocausation/","@type":"WebPage","name":"The future can constrain a model without sending a message back","url":"https://causality.in/blog/future-without-retrocausation/","relatedLink":["https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/budgeted-questions/","https://causality.in/blog/the-record-and-the-arrow/"]},"description":"An endpoint calculation asks how a future condition shapes a whole path, and where its information enters the problem.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-10-19","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"The future can constrain a model without sending a message back\nA destination changes how we plan a journey. A terminal condition can also change a mathematical path everywhere along its course. Both invite a question: what happens when we describe a stretch of history together with the futures it admits, rather than describing only an isolated present state?\nThe answer depends on what the future contributes to the construction. Planning uses predictions to choose an action now. A boundary-value problem specifies conditions at more than one point and asks for a whole path consistent with them. An advanced equation explicitly contains the state at a later time. These give three different ways to formulate a temporal question. They require different data and support different conclusions.\nAn endpoint is a constraint, not an observation from tomorrow\nConsider continuously differentiable paths x on [0,1] with x(0)=0 and x(1)=1. Minimise E[x]=\\int_0^1(x'(t))^2\\,dt. Write x(t)=t+u(t) with u(0)=u(1)=0. Then\n\nE[x]=\\int_0^1(1+u')^2\\,dt\n=1+2\\bigl(u(1)-u(0)\\bigr)+\\int_0^1(u')^2\\,dt\n=1+\\int_0^1(u')^2\\,dt.\n\nThe remaining integral is non-negative. Equality requires u'=0 throughout; the endpoints then force u=0. Thus x(t)=t is the unique minimiser in this class. The proof identifies both the solution and why no bent path can tie it.\nThe straight path minimises energy among three curves that share the same two endpoints.\nThe plotted family is x(t)=t+a\\sin(\\pi t), with a=0,0.2,-0.2. Every path satisfies the same endpoint data, while E=1+a^2\\pi^2/2. The bent paths remain admissible; the energy criterion selects the straight one.\nThe terminal condition affects the solution everywhere. We supplied both endpoints as part of the problem, rather than obtaining tomorrow's measured endpoint today. Different endpoint data define a different problem. What is solved together is the path: each local value belongs to a trajectory satisfying the given conditions and minimising the stated energy.\nThat makes the elementary example useful beyond its simplicity. It shows how a whole-path question can constrain local behaviour through consistency, without a mechanism that sends information backwards. The future enters as a condition on the admissible solution, and its role is explicit enough to examine.\nPredicting is another construction\nA planner can simulate candidate actions, score their predicted consequences and choose one. Its predictions may be mistaken because the action at the present time depends on an available model of the future, not an observation of the actual future. The prediction and objective are the information available for that choice. Comparing the actions they select and the outcomes that follow tests their usefulness; the actual future remains something the planner will encounter, including the consequences its model missed.\nAn equation containing x(t+\\tau) makes a different commitment. One must specify a domain, admissible solution class and boundary data, and establish whether a solution exists or is unique. Inconsistency or non-uniqueness is a possible mathematical outcome. Physical realisation would be a further question about that particular model.\nThe boundary changes the representation of a local state\nA linear example exposes where the supplied information sits. For y'(t)=-\\lambda y(t)+f(t) with \\lambda>0 and initial data at t_0, the solution is\n\ny(t)=e^{-\\lambda(t-t_0)}y(t_0)+\\int_{t_0}^t e^{-\\lambda(t-s)}f(s)\\,ds.\n\nThe current value is written in terms of an initial state and a weighted history of forcing. Specify the terminal value at T instead, and the same equation can be written\n\ny(t)=e^{\\lambda(T-t)}y(T)-\\int_t^T e^{\\lambda(s-t)}f(s)\\,ds.\n\nThis second representation uses future forcing and a terminal condition because those are the data we supplied. It has not made them available to a real-time observer. The growing coefficient also exposes the instability of reconstructing an earlier state through a noisy record: a small terminal error is magnified as the reconstructed interval lengthens. A formula for a path and a procedure that can reliably obtain it from available observations are different achievements.\nEliminating an unobserved linear component from a larger model can leave a history integral or a future-conditioned integral in the remaining variables, depending on how that component was anchored. That is a mathematical route to temporal nonlocality in a reduced description. It still requires the larger equations and their data. Conversely, an equation that mixes delayed and advanced states cannot be justified by the existence of either representation above: its well-posedness is its own question, and resonant or inconsistent boundary data can prevent a solution in the declared class.\nA description along a path\nThe wider ambition is to understand how the path taken changes what a description needs to preserve. Two states may answer the same questions now and give different answers after an allowed continuation. A terminal condition may rule out a path that otherwise looks locally admissible. A model adequate for one predicted future may fail when another becomes relevant. These are connected intuitions, but they are not the same mathematical relation.\nMONAD investigates descriptions, probes and the continuations a description permits; its temporal branch asks how planning, whole-trajectory constraints and literal advanced states fit into that inquiry. How the three connect remains research work. The endpoint calculation supplies one exact construction, not a theory of every temporal dependence or a physical demonstration of retrocausation.\nWhat makes the question promising is the possibility of following a local description through a larger stretch of permitted change. Instead of giving the future a mysterious influence, we can ask which paths remain possible, which information their selection requires, and where a present description ceases to be enough.\n","image":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://causality.in/blog/future-without-retrocausation/admissible-paths.svg","description":"The straight path minimises energy among three curves that share the same two endpoints.","caption":"The plotted family is x(t)=t+a\\sin(\\pi t), with a=0,0.2,-0.2. Every path satisfies the same endpoint data, while E=1+a^2\\pi^2/2. The bent paths remain admissible; the energy criterion selects the straight one.\n"}],"url":"https://causality.in/blog/future-without-retrocausation/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/the-ineffable-geometry/#article","identifier":"content:causality-the-ineffable-geometry","@type":"Article","name":"The ineffable geometry","headline":"The ineffable geometry","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["geometry and causality","mathematical language","geometry of time","retrocausality","fractional dimension","non-Euclidean geometry","thought experiment"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/the-ineffable-geometry/","@type":"WebPage","name":"The ineffable geometry","url":"https://causality.in/blog/the-ineffable-geometry/","relatedLink":["https://causality.in/blog/beyond-the-mirage/","https://causality.in/blog/entropy-and-a-learning-mind/","https://getharsh.in/blog/breath-of-cosmos/","https://causality.in/blog/future-without-retrocausation/"]},"description":"A vocabulary we do not yet share, a line through an imagined time, and an early hunch about geometry, causality and intelligence.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2023-12-22","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"The ineffable geometry\nOkay, let's run a hypothetical thought experiment. You are participating in a tribe's discussion. There's a catch: you don't understand their vocabulary, and they don't understand yours. Where do you begin?\nThat is the difficulty of finding a mathematical language for causality: a relationship can seem within reach before there is a way to state it. It stops making sense pretty fast.\nMathematics and our understanding of the physical world have developed together. Calculus gives us a language for motion and change. Vector calculus helps describe fields. Tensor methods let us express geometry without tying everything to a particular coordinate system. The interesting part is not a tidy history in which each tool appeared for one purpose. It is that a better language can make a previously awkward question possible to ask precisely.\nCould a richer geometric language help articulate causality, especially where quantum theory, complex systems and AI make ordinary intuition unreliable? What would that language let us see?\nFrom knowing a number to realising what it means\nTake another thought experiment. Suppose I tell you that a thousand children die each day for want of clean water. This is a stipulated number for the image, not a mortality estimate. You hear it and say: that's a lot.\nNow imagine those thousand bodies lined up along your way home from work. Imagine that happening for ten years: 10\\times365\\times1000 bodies, in this simplified count. Now imagine, God forbid, that one is someone you love.\nThe number has not changed. Your relationship to it has. Knowing, understanding and realising are doing different work here. The distance between a quantity and a lived consequence has become difficult to ignore.\nThe words murti and darshan suggest another way to approach that distance. In this image, the seeker comes to a form through a realisation, not merely through a label. “Idol” does not carry that distinction. This interpretation does not settle the words’ different religious meanings.\nGeometry offered a mathematical counterpart to that wish: a way to see a relationship rather than only manipulate its symbols. A derivative becomes a slope; an integral becomes an accumulated area. The picture does not replace the definition or proof. It can help us realise what the definition is saying.\nWhat would a geometry of causality let us do?\nThe ambition is a calculus whose geometry makes causal relationships more directly intelligible. Determinism is the holy grail here: an account precise enough to explain a mechanism rather than leave every question at a correlation. That aspiration does not establish that nature must be deterministic, or that probability and statistics have become unnecessary.\nIndirect evidence seemed a useful starting point. We infer an unseen object from what it changes around it; the bending of light can reveal something about the mass between us and its source. Could a similar discipline help locate a causal relationship that cannot be inspected directly? Statistical causal inference already develops ways of asking such questions. The wider inquiry was whether insights from physics, mathematics, philosophy, computation and cognition could supply other useful descriptions.\nTime symmetry was one possible direction. A law can admit a reversed description while the observations and boundary conditions available to us privilege one direction. Another was to make temporal relationships less dependent on the picture of an isolated present moving along a line. A third was simulation: specify a proposed rule, examine what follows, and find where the construction fails. A simulated effect would establish something about the rule supplied, not demonstrate that the physical world follows it.\nCould a local mathematical account of intelligence let us isolate and transfer useful pieces of it? The idea of “micro-calculus” is to look closely enough at a system's changes to make a capability transferable in parts. The name supplies no method yet. Defining the unit, the preserved capability and the transformation would be part of the research.\nThat possibility matters beyond a clever model. If a system's reasoning and changes became more intelligible, it could help with reliability, engineering, public policy and decisions about AI. The chain from a new description to those applications remains work to establish.\nMore than a line\nTwo days later, on 24 December 2023, I took the hunch into another thought experiment. This is my favourite kind: bring a few tangents together and see what breaks. Ready?\nIs the sum of a triangle's interior angles always 180 degrees?\nFor an ordinary triangle in Euclidean plane geometry, yes. On a curved surface, the corresponding rule can differ. A triangle on a sphere, following great-circle arcs from the North Pole to two points on the equator ninety degrees apart, has three right angles. Its sum is 270 degrees. The surface and the meaning of its straightest paths are part of the question.\nThe comparison has a third case: on a hyperbolic plane, a geodesic triangle's angles add to less than 180 degrees. Positive, zero and negative curvature give different answers to the apparently familiar question. The NASA comparison of a sphere, a flat plane and a saddle is a way into that distinction; specifying the geometry is what makes the answer precise.\nNow ask: what is the dimension of time?\nWe know how to measure the dimensions of a phone or a room. Seconds, minutes and hours measure duration. But a unit of duration does not by itself tell us what kind of mathematical object time should be in a particular theory. Nor does uncertainty about that question mean that physics knows nothing about time.\nFor the sake of the obnoxious experiment, suppose time could be described using a fractional dimension and a non-Euclidean geometry. What would a line through that space look like?\nIn this image, it bends, loops, disappears and appears before it begins. Could understanding such a line tell us something about the flow of time, and could a framework make sense of an effect preceding its cause? Retrocausality was the question, not an established consequence of the picture.\nFractional dimension, curvature and causal order are different mathematical properties. Choosing the first two does not derive the third. To turn this image into a model, we would have to say what the space is, what counts as a path, how events are ordered, what observations are available and which equations relate them. Only then could we test consistency or ask what the construction predicts.\nIt is a challenging picture to wrap your head around. I don't blame you if it doesn't make sense. Hence the name ineffable geometry: the intuition comes before an adequate language for it.\nA hunch with work in front of it\nAs much as I hate to admit it, this is, at best, a hunch: the geometry of time might connect chaos, entropy, causality and consciousness. It needs mathematical rigour, beginning with a search for inconsistencies in its own assumptions.\nThe next exploration, Entropy and a learning mind, asks what happens when a trained system's useful initial arrangement keeps changing. A later, narrower calculation, The future can constrain a model without sending a message back, shows exactly how supplied endpoint data can constrain a whole path. There, the future enters through the data supplied to the problem. The larger question is how to describe time and causal order themselves.\nMONAD is where that idea of micro-calculus now lives, under the name it grew into: which answers a description preserves, and when the objects or rules of that description need to change. The geometric ambition is larger than any one existing example. Its value will depend on what it eventually lets us state, derive and test.\nI am brewing the idea. The invitation is to explore it seriously enough to discover both what is promising and what does not survive the mathematics. The object of the thought experiment is more than a line. The line is where the work starts.\n","url":"https://causality.in/blog/the-ineffable-geometry/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/the-record-and-the-arrow/#article","identifier":"content:monad-the-record-and-the-arrow","@type":"Article","name":"The record and the arrow","headline":"The record and the arrow","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["retrodiction","arrow of time","reversible dynamics","coarse observation","inverse conditioning","reconstruction horizon","Boltzmann entropy"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/the-record-and-the-arrow/","@type":"WebPage","name":"The record and the arrow","url":"https://causality.in/blog/the-record-and-the-arrow/","relatedLink":["https://causality.in/reckon/","https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/budgeted-questions/","https://causality.in/blog/future-without-retrocausation/"]},"description":"A reversible motion leaves an ambiguous record: where did the recoverable past disappear?","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-09-23","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"The record and the arrow\nThe trace is still there: a sensor value, an image, a stored summary. Following it backwards seems like a matter of undoing what happened. Yet a perfectly reversible motion can leave a record compatible with several different beginnings. Where did the ambiguity enter if the motion itself lost nothing? The answer lies in the whole route from the state to the record, including what the observer could see and keep.\nThis is one reason the observer belongs inside a description of the problem. Not because attention changes physical law, but because resolution, noise, preparation and permitted operations change which questions a record can answer. A finite budget can make a distinction unreachable even when it still exists in the underlying system.\nThe record-and-observer account began in Reckon's February 2026 framework. Its retrodiction chapter separates identifiability, inverse sensitivity and noise before deriving the logarithmic horizon. The calculation here develops that distinction for a reader who need not run the laboratory.\nReversing the dynamics does not reverse the channel\nWrite an effective observation as\n\nO_{\\tau,R,s}=C_R\\circ\\Pi_s\\circ\\Phi_\\tau\\circ U.\n\nHere U is an allowed preparation, \\Phi_\\tau the evolution over time \\tau, \\Pi_s the observation scale and C_R the record channel available under resources R. Reconstructing an earlier state requires the relevant information to survive the whole composition. Inverting \\Phi_\\tau alone cannot undo distinctions that \\Pi_s erased.\nA finite example makes this exact. Rotate a three-bit state so that (a,b,c) becomes (b,c,a), then record only its first bit. The rotation is reversible, but the record 0 has four possible earlier states because it tells us only that b=0. With all three output bits, the past is uniquely recoverable. With one bit, it is not. Extra precision in that one bit cannot recover a or c; another observation is needed. Non-identifiability is not merely a difficult numerical inversion.\nA unique inverse can still amplify error\nNow consider y_\\tau=e^{-\\lambda\\tau}x_0, with \\lambda>0 and bounded observation error |\\xi|\\leq\\sigma. The map is injective at every finite \\tau, but its inverse amplifies error:\n\n\\hat x_0=e^{\\lambda\\tau}(y_\\tau+\\xi),\n\\qquad |\\hat x_0-x_0|\\leq e^{\\lambda\\tau}\\sigma.\n\nFor a fixed noise bound and required error \\varepsilon\\geq\\sigma, the worst-case guarantee holds only while\n\n\\tau\\leq\\frac{\\log(\\varepsilon/\\sigma)}{\\lambda}.\n\nThis horizon comes from conditioning of the inverse, not from a many-to-one evolution.\nTake λ=1 per unit of time, σ=0.001 and an allowed reconstruction error of 0.1. The worst-case guarantee reaches about 4.605 time units. Reducing the noise bound tenfold adds about 2.303 units; it does not multiply the horizon tenfold. A noiseless channel, a differently conditioned map or a different observable changes the result. The useful distinction is between information that never survived the record and information that survived but becomes increasingly expensive to recover accurately.\nA tenfold smaller noise bound shifts the error-bound curve, adding 2.303 time units before it reaches the same tolerance of 0.1.\nThe logarithmic vertical scale makes exponential error growth a straight line. Both curves use the same inverse and tolerance; reducing the record's noise shifts when the guarantee ends.\nThat makes a record design a question about future use. More stored detail is valuable when it preserves a distinction the later reconstruction needs. Repeated measurements can help only under an appropriate noise model; correlated errors need not average away. A log with perfect byte retention can still omit the preparation, the observation scale or the event needed to interpret its values.\nEntropy depends on the description being counted\nFor a closed system evolving by a measure-preserving reversible map, the fine-grained entropy of the transported distribution is unchanged, when that entropy is well defined. A macrostate instead groups many microstates. Its Boltzmann entropy is\n\nS_B(m)=k_B\\log\\mu(\\Gamma_m),\n\nwhere \\Gamma_m is the set of microstates in macrostate m, \\mu is the measure the map preserves and k_B is Boltzmann's constant. These are different objects. A macrostate can become more typical without the fine-grained distribution acquiring or destroying information in the same sense.\nLebowitz's account of Boltzmann's entropy and time's arrow explains how macroscopic irreversibility can coexist with reversible microscopic dynamics. Coarse and fine descriptions, a low-entropy past and typicality matter to that explanation. Reversibility by itself does not supply a theorem that every observation has an arrow, nor does naming a learning loss “entropy” make it a thermodynamic quantity.\nThe observer-relative question is consequently more precise than “is the past lost?” Which distinction was recorded, through which channel, at what scale, and which inverse problem is being attempted? Given endpoint data can constrain a path because the information is part of the problem. A trace has to preserve the information from which the path might be recovered. The past can remain unique in the dynamics while several pasts remain compatible with the record.\n","image":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://causality.in/blog/the-record-and-the-arrow/retrodiction-horizon.svg","description":"A tenfold smaller noise bound shifts the error-bound curve, adding 2.303 time units before it reaches the same tolerance of 0.1.","caption":"The logarithmic vertical scale makes exponential error growth a straight line. Both curves use the same inverse and tolerance; reducing the record's noise shifts when the guarantee ends.\n"}],"url":"https://causality.in/blog/the-record-and-the-arrow/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/when-effects-cancel/#article","identifier":"content:monad-influence-is-not-transitive","@type":"Article","name":"When effects cancel along a path","headline":"When effects cancel along a path","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["causal interventions","transitivity of causation","xor cancellation","detectable influence","transitive reachability","total-variation distance"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/when-effects-cancel/","@type":"WebPage","name":"When effects cancel along a path","url":"https://causality.in/blog/when-effects-cancel/","relatedLink":["https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/causal-inference-primer/","https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/four-questions-for-an-evaluation/"]},"description":"An exact xor model makes two interventions compose differently, leaving a path without the expected end-to-end effect.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-09-29","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"When effects cancel along a path\nAn intervention on A changes B. An intervention on B changes C. It is tempting to conclude that intervening on A changes C. That conclusion need not hold. A path records relationships under particular interventions; it does not ensure that their effects compose without cancellation.\nTake the binary structural model\n\nA\\sim\\operatorname{Bernoulli}(1/4),\\qquad B=A,\\qquad C=A\\oplus B.\n\nThe symbol \\oplus denotes xor. In the unmodified system C is always zero.\nSet A to zero or one by intervention and leave the other equations intact. B changes from zero to one. C remains zero, because its two inputs change together and cancel under xor. A therefore has a detectable effect on B but no distributional effect on C in this comparison.\nNow intervene on B, replacing its equation while leaving A’s distribution unchanged. Setting B to zero makes C equal A, so C is one with probability one quarter. Setting B to one makes C the complement of A, so C is one with probability three quarters. B has a detectable effect on C, yet the earlier A-to-C conclusion still fails. The intervention on B breaks a dependency that the intervention on A preserved. That difference is enough to produce the failure in this constructed model.\nWhat each intervention does to C\nIntervention\tAssignments A ⊕ B = C, with probability\tProbability C = 1\t\nSet A = 0\t0 ⊕ 0 = 0 (100%)\t0%\t\nSet A = 1\t1 ⊕ 1 = 0 (100%)\t0%\t\nSet B = 0\t0 ⊕ 0 = 0 (75%); 1 ⊕ 0 = 1 (25%)\t25%\t\nSet B = 1\t0 ⊕ 1 = 1 (75%); 1 ⊕ 1 = 0 (25%)\t75%\t\nReachability and direct evidence are different relations\nA graph algorithm can compute transitive reachability. That is a valid operation on graph structure. Reinterpreting every reachable pair as a directly detectable intervention effect is a separate operation requiring assumptions the graph alone does not contain.\nA debugger can use inferred paths to choose which relationships to examine next, while retaining measured edges as a separate relation. Testing an implied edge costs trials and supplies its own comparison. The graph becomes an instrument for investigation when reachability directs attention without replacing the evidence that an intervention needs.\nReliable does not mean detectable at every threshold\nSuppose a binary channel records a balanced input correctly with probability 0.8. Under input zero it records one with probability 0.2; under input one that probability is 0.8. The total-variation distance between these two output laws is 0.6.\nA stipulated detection threshold of 0.9 is not met even though prediction through the channel succeeds eighty percent of the time. Reliability and detection refer to different criteria. Improving a record does not license every threshold or erase the conditions under which it was observed.\nJoseph Halpern gives a finite counterexample to the transitivity of causation, alongside conditions that can restore it. His counterexample prompted a separate check in MONAD’s own finite observer model.\nThe MONAD programme includes a machine-checked failure of transitivity in its finite observer setting. Detectable influence in that setting cannot simply be treated as an order. Taking its transitive closure would add relationships whose intervention effects need to be examined separately.\nThe xor example shows what such a check can reveal. One intervention breaks a dependency that another preserves, so the two paths carry different evidence. An account of those intervention-specific relationships is more informative than a larger graph whose edges have silently changed meaning.\nJoseph Y. Halpern, Sufficient Conditions for Causality to Be Transitive, 2016. The check uses his displayed three-variable equations under hard interventions; it does not identify his definition of actual causation with MONAD’s detectable influence. The checked MONAD example fixes the starting state at (0,0,0) and allows only the identity operation and four specified maps that set A or B, with noiseless records. This article instead uses Bernoulli-distributed A and interventions that replace a structural equation to illustrate the same cancellation.\n","url":"https://causality.in/blog/when-effects-cancel/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/when-the-support-moves/#article","identifier":"content:monad-when-the-support-moves","@type":"Article","name":"When the support moves","headline":"When the support moves","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["uniform-endpoint testing","moving support","two-hypothesis test","1/n scale","square-root scale","local alternatives"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/when-the-support-moves/","@type":"WebPage","name":"When the support moves","url":"https://causality.in/blog/when-the-support-moves/","relatedLink":["https://causality.in/blog/when-effects-cancel/","https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/four-questions-for-an-evaluation/"]},"description":"A measurement beyond the old endpoint changes what one sample can establish; an exact test follows the consequence.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-10-15","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"When the support moves\n“With more samples, we can detect smaller changes.” But how much smaller? Suppose an instrument produces independent measurements uniformly between zero and an endpoint θ. Another setting moves that endpoint slightly. Most readings could have come from either setting; one reading beyond the old endpoint could not. That sharp edge gives another sample an opportunity unlike the one it has in a smooth shift of an average.\nWe can calculate what the opportunity is worth. Let the original law be uniform on [0, θ], with θ positive, and compare it with a uniform law on a slightly longer interval. The endpoint changes which observations are possible. Following that change through a two-hypothesis test gives a detection scale that sample count alone would not tell us.\nA single observation can rule out one hypothesis\nCall the original endpoint \\theta and the alternative endpoint \\theta+\\delta, with \\delta>0. If any observation exceeds \\theta, it is impossible under the original law. If every observation lies below \\theta, both laws remain possible.\nUnder the alternative, one observation lies below \\theta with probability \\theta/(\\theta+\\delta). For n independent observations, the probability that all remain below it is\n\nq_n=\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{\\theta+\\delta}\\right)^n.\n\nThe support boundary is doing the work. Yibi Huang's parameter-estimation lecture uses the uniform-endpoint family to show why a sample maximum constrains which endpoints have nonzero likelihood. Here we use that same boundary for a two-hypothesis test rather than estimate an unrestricted endpoint.\nThe optimal balanced test has an exact error\nGive the two hypotheses equal prior probability and charge the same loss for either wrong answer. On the common region, the original law has the greater joint density: \\theta^{-n} rather than (\\theta+\\delta)^{-n}. Outside it, only the alternative can occur.\nThe optimal decision is therefore original when all observations are at most \\theta, alternative otherwise. It never mistakes the original law for the alternative. Under the alternative, it is wrong with probability q_n. Averaging over the equal priors gives total error q_n/2.\nThis is a complete calculation for the declared test. It is not an empirical error estimate or a guarantee for an unknown population. Different priors or unequal losses would change the decision threshold.\nAn inverse-sample change remains detectable\nNow let the alternative depend on sample count: set \\delta=h/n, for fixed h>0. Then\n\nq_n=\\left(1+\\frac{h}{n\\theta}\\right)^{-n}\\longrightarrow e^{-h/\\theta},\n\\qquad \\text{balanced error}\\longrightarrow\\frac12e^{-h/\\theta}.\n\nThe limit lies strictly between zero and one half. Thus an endpoint difference on the 1/n scale retains nontrivial discrimination in this model.\nFor an arithmetic illustration, choose θ and h both equal to ten. With one hundred observations, the alternative endpoint is 10.1 and the error is about 0.185. As n grows, the error approaches e⁻¹/2, about 0.184, while the endpoint difference shrinks. These are model calculations, not measurements from an experiment.\nAt a larger difference h/\\sqrt n, the same test's error instead tends to zero. The square-root scale is therefore not the smallest scale that retains discrimination here. That does not disprove square-root conclusions in models whose assumptions justify them. It disproves their unrestricted use across different experiments.\nKeep local and fixed alternatives separate\nIf δ is fixed while n grows, q_n decays exponentially. If δ changes with n, a different limiting problem is being asked. A theorem for fixed alternatives cannot be silently transferred to the second comparison.\nExact test error tends to zero for a fixed endpoint gap and for a gap of 10 divided by square root n, but approaches about 0.184 for a gap of 10 divided by n.\nAll three curves use \\theta=10 and the exact error q_n/2. The alternatives differ: a fixed gap of 0.1, a local gap of 10/n, and a gap of 10/\\sqrt n. At n=100 the first two compare the same endpoints and meet. Beyond that point, only one keeps moving the alternative closer.\nDependence, measurement noise, a different distribution family or a different decision can change the information in each observation. A noisy instrument may erase the sharp boundary that makes this calculation possible.\nMONAD's formal testing work includes this moving-support counterexample. Its particular resource consequence comes from the sharp boundary: each sample has an opportunity to enter a region the original hypothesis cannot produce. Changing that opportunity changes what another sample is worth. Sample count becomes informative through the observation model, and the model's support is part of the detection claim rather than a detail to omit from its reported rate.\n","image":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://causality.in/blog/when-the-support-moves/fixed-and-local-alternatives.svg","description":"Exact test error tends to zero for a fixed endpoint gap and for a gap of 10 divided by square root n, but approaches about 0.184 for a gap of 10 divided by n.","caption":"All three curves use \\theta=10 and the exact error q_n/2. The alternatives differ: a fixed gap of 0.1, a local gap of 10/n, and a gap of 10/\\sqrt n. At n=100 the first two compare the same endpoints and meet. Beyond that point, only one keeps moving the alternative closer.\n"}],"url":"https://causality.in/blog/when-the-support-moves/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/","identifier":"content:causality-collections","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Thoughts","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/","identifier":"collection:causality-changing-descriptions","name":"Changing descriptions","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/","@type":"CollectionPage"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/","identifier":"collection:causality-composition-and-learning","name":"Composition and learning","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/","@type":"CollectionPage"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/","identifier":"collection:causality-evidence-and-evaluation","name":"What does the measurement establish?","url":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/","@type":"CollectionPage"}}]},"description":"Three connected inquiries: what a summary preserves, what units learn through how they are organised and what an evaluation shows about a change. Each begins with a question and develops it through small examples; standalone pieces are on the Blog.","text":"","url":"https://causality.in/collections/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]}},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/","identifier":"collection:causality-changing-descriptions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Changing descriptions","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"description":"What survives a changed question, a noisy observation or another representational basis?","text":"This collection is about systems whose description itself changes.\nA description offers a particular view of a thing. It may preserve every answer we currently need while collapsing states that a later question would separate. The difficulty appears when those states must become distinguishable again: is the answer merely hidden, was it discarded, or does the present language have no way to express it? Even when nothing is lost, different coordinates can leave us disagreeing about which quantities the description has recovered.\nThere is a bargain behind any useful description. We discard detail so that a problem becomes tractable, while expecting enough of it to survive for the next judgment. Storage, access, the language of the question and the permitted error all participate in that bargain. Its failure can demand another measurement, another representation or a narrower promise. Understanding which change is needed matters more than making the old account confidently answer again.\n","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"hasPart":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/","identifier":"collection:causality-preservation-under-uncertainty","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Preservation under uncertainty","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/when-the-description-must-change/#article","identifier":"content:monad-when-the-description-must-change","name":"When the description must change","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/when-the-description-must-change/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/which-factors-did-we-recover/#article","identifier":"content:monad-which-factors-did-we-recover","name":"Which factors did we recover?","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/which-factors-did-we-recover/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/","identifier":"journey:causality-from-summary-to-repair","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From summary to repair","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/","identifier":"journey:causality-from-summary-to-repair","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From summary to repair","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Changing descriptions","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"From summary to repair","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/"}}]}],"description":"An ordered path from a lost answer through affordable questions to the full cost of an accessible repair.","text":"A summary can omit a distinction while an accessible source preserves the means to recover it. That possibility changes what a useful record needs to keep, but only after we understand what was lost and what another read can supply.\nThe order of this inquiry follows that dependency. First establish which answer the summary can no longer give. Then ask whether the remaining resources let an observer reach it. Only then can a sequence of queries be priced as a repair: a cheap first question may leave an expensive branch, or no feasible branch at all. Storage and access together determine the promise. Losing the source changes what the same retained bytes can support.\n","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/","identifier":"collection:causality-changing-descriptions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Changing descriptions","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/"}],"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/compression-is-a-promise/#article","identifier":"content:monad-compression-is-a-promise","name":"Compression is a promise about questions","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/compression-is-a-promise/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/budgeted-questions/#article","identifier":"content:monad-budgeted-questions","name":"What can you distinguish with one more move?","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/budgeted-questions/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/the-price-of-another-question/#article","identifier":"content:monad-the-price-of-another-question","name":"The price of another question","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/the-price-of-another-question/"}}]}},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/budgeted-questions/#article","identifier":"content:monad-budgeted-questions","@type":"Article","name":"What can you distinguish with one more move?","headline":"What can you distinguish with one more move?","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["observation budgets","three-bit rotation","residual budget","accessible distinctions","observation protocols"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/budgeted-questions/","@type":"WebPage","name":"What can you distinguish with one more move?","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/budgeted-questions/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Changing descriptions","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"From summary to repair","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"What can you distinguish with one more move?","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/budgeted-questions/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/compression-is-a-promise/","https://causality.in/blog/future-without-retrocausation/"]},"description":"Rotating three bits makes another feature visible and spends part of the observer's remaining investigation.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-10-01","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"What can you distinguish with one more move?\nWhat does looking again add? An object may reveal nothing new from the same position, but one allowed move can bring another feature into view. Two states indistinguishable at a glance can separate during a short investigation. The observer's resources now belong inside the description of what it can know: the next view costs something, and paying for it changes which views remain possible.\nConsider three bits (a,b,c). The observer can read only the first position. It can also rotate the state to (b,c,a), paying one unit per rotation. Reading is free in this toy model, and doing nothing is allowed.\nWith each affordable rotation, another coordinate becomes observable:\nSuccessive reads reveal a, then b, then c, dividing the same eight states into two, four and eight classes; each question mark denotes an unread coordinate.\nRead the tree from left to right. The first column uses no rotation and reveals a; one rotation reveals b, and a second reveals c. The edge label is the newly read bit. The resulting two, four and eight classes divide the same eight states.\nThe state space did not grow. The accessible question family grew. At budget one, states 000 and 001 remain indistinguishable even though their third bits differ. After one rotation, the third bit sits one more paid move away.\nSpending the budget changes what remains possible\nSuppose two states agree on every observation reachable with budget two. After one rotation they must still agree on every observation reachable with the remaining budget one. Any such observation can be prefixed with the rotation to form a question already covered by the original budget.\nWrite x\\sim_B y when all observations affordable with budget B agree. If an action u costs k\\leq B, then the residual-budget law is\n\nx\\sim_B y\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u(x)\\sim_{B-k}u(y).\n\nA separating continuation of cost at most B-k would, when prefixed by u, separate the original states within budget B. That contradicts the premise. Reusing the full original budget after the move would smuggle in an extra resource allocation.\nThe example assumes deterministic total actions, state-independent non-negative costs and a declared set of observations. If an action sometimes fails, costs depend on the state or measurements disturb it, those features have to enter the model.\nAccessibility is different from retention\nThe observer might have access to the full three-bit state but currently lack the budget to inspect it. The bits are hidden from that observer's present protocol, not erased. Alternatively, a store might have retained only a and discarded b and c. Rotating that one-bit summary does not recreate the original state.\nAn archive or a fresh measurement could make the missing bits available. Its storage, access permission, transfer and latency are part of the repair. The distinction between “I have not looked” and “I no longer have it” is as important for a retrieval system as it is for this finite model.\nMore resources do not necessarily mean one scalar\nThree-bit rotation uses one simple cost. Real investigations may be limited by memory, compute, bandwidth, trials and elapsed time. A procedure using less memory but more communication is not automatically cheaper. A larger allowance can bring another query within reach, but only if its source remains accessible.\nA useful comparison first names which resources can substitute for one another, which are hard ceilings and which measurements actually require them. Otherwise a claim of greater distinguishability may merely have changed the accounting unit.\nThe broader MONAD question asks how descriptions remain adequate as their admissible questions change. This example makes one part tractable: future questions can be indexed by a finite cost, and spending that cost changes the remaining comparison. The observer's representation is now tied to a protocol with an allowance, rather than to a glance treated as the whole investigation. Choosing among those reads introduces repair cost: an affordable first question may leave an expensive distinction unresolved.\n","image":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/budgeted-questions/observation-tree.svg","description":"Successive reads reveal a, then b, then c, dividing the same eight states into two, four and eight classes; each question mark denotes an unread coordinate.","caption":"Read the tree from left to right. The first column uses no rotation and reveals a; one rotation reveals b, and a second reveals c. The edge label is the newly read bit. The resulting two, four and eight classes divide the same eight states.\n"}],"url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/budgeted-questions/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/","identifier":"journey:causality-from-summary-to-repair","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From summary to repair","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/compression-is-a-promise/#article","identifier":"content:monad-compression-is-a-promise","@type":"Article","name":"Compression is a promise about questions","headline":"Compression is a promise about questions","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["sufficient summaries","question factorisation","information loss","supplement alphabet","future query families"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/compression-is-a-promise/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Compression is a promise about questions","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/compression-is-a-promise/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Changing descriptions","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"From summary to repair","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Compression is a promise about questions","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/compression-is-a-promise/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/how-tolerances-accumulate/","https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/budgeted-questions/"]},"description":"Yesterday's summary answered every question we asked. A new question reveals the distinction it chose not to keep.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-10-01","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Compression is a promise about questions\nYesterday's summary answered every question we asked of it. Today we need one more answer, and the summary gives two different situations the same name. It has not necessarily become inaccurate. Its earlier success concealed a choice about which differences were worth keeping. Can we locate that choice precisely enough to know what the new question needs?\nTake four states, represented by two bits. Suppose the only question is whether the first bit is one. The summary q(a,b)=a answers it perfectly:\nOriginal state\tSummary q\tFirst-bit answer\tSecond-bit answer\t\n00\t0\t0\t0\t\n01\t0\t0\t1\t\n10\t1\t1\t0\t\n11\t1\t1\t1\t\nIt keeps two classes, \\{00,01\\} and \\{10,11\\}, instead of four states. Nothing relevant to the first question has been lost. The final column shows exactly what the summary cannot recover.\nNow ask whether the second bit is one. The old summary cannot answer. Both 00 and 01 became 0, but their answers differ. There is no function of that single retained bit which returns both answers correctly. More computation, a more persuasive explanation and a larger language model cannot repair this exact ambiguity from the same input alone.\nThe question family defines the summary\nWrite a question as a function f on the original states. It can be answered from a summary q when some function g satisfies\n\nf=g\\circ q.\n\nFor a surjective summary, this is equivalent to f being constant on every group of states that q merged. Necessity follows because equal summaries give g the same input. For sufficiency, choose any representative of a summary class and return its answer; constancy makes the choice irrelevant. This is the small proof behind the promise.\nThe second bit existed before anybody asked about it. Making it relevant changed the question family, not the state space. A richer language capable of representing previously unavailable objects would pose a further problem; the two-bit example isolates the simpler change so that its consequence can be calculated exactly.\nAn agent's notes have the same vulnerability. They can be excellent for the task that produced them and inadequate for a new task. Understanding which answers they preserve lets the agent recognise that inadequacy before it turns an ambiguous record into a confident response. The missing distinction points towards what another read or measurement would need to supply.\nA publication gives this a practical form. A listing can keep an article's title, description and address while leaving out its argument. That is enough to choose something to read. It is not enough to check a proof: two revisions can keep the same listing while one changes the crucial step. Gazette keeps the authored article as the source and derives the listing from it, so the reader can follow the address to the reasoning. The short description serves discovery; the retained article makes a deeper question answerable. If only the listing survived, no amount of searching those three fields would recover the missing step.\nRepair needs an information source\nSeveral repairs are possible, and they have different costs. Retaining the original two bits preserves both answers directly; a supplement containing the second bit restores just the missing distinction. Measuring the original object again can supply that bit if the object still exists and the measurement is permitted. Without one of these sources, declining to answer preserves the boundary between an unresolved question and a guess presented as reconstruction.\nIn this example, preserving the ability to answer both coordinate questions requires distinguishing all four states. A binary supplement with two values suffices because each old summary class contains two states. This is finite alphabet accounting, not a coding theorem about an arbitrary stream, and it assumes the supplement's encoder had access to the original state.\nThe question sits near established mathematics of abstraction. Cousot and Cousot's abstract interpretation studies computation through abstractions; an exact correspondence would locate which existing constructions a changing-question account can reuse and where its additional obligations begin.\nAn exact supplement and a noisy observation make different promises. A report can be informative while leaving both original states possible. Repeated measurements may reduce a decision's error, but improving the chance of being right is not the same as restoring the answer in every state.\nThe future cannot be unrestricted for free\nIf every possible question separating every pair of states must remain answerable, a sufficient summary cannot merge any two states. Perfect future safety then removes nontrivial compression. A useful system must choose a restricted family, tolerate an explicit approximation, retain a repair source or accept that some later questions will remain unanswered.\nThe two-bit construction gives the changing-description problem a small but useful starting point: a summary's failure can be identified as a particular unanswered question, and a repair can be specified by the distinction it must restore. With budgeted continuations, the future family also depends on what the observer can afford to do next. The summary and the remaining access together determine which promise can still be kept.\n","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/compression-is-a-promise/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/","identifier":"journey:causality-from-summary-to-repair","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From summary to repair","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/the-price-of-another-question/#article","identifier":"content:monad-the-price-of-another-question","@type":"TechArticle","name":"The price of another question","headline":"The price of another question","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["adaptive query trees","minimum repair cost","Bellman recurrence","nondestructive source access","worst-case cost"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/the-price-of-another-question/","@type":"WebPage","name":"The price of another question","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/the-price-of-another-question/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Changing descriptions","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"From summary to repair","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"The price of another question","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/the-price-of-another-question/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/budgeted-questions/","https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/compression-is-a-promise/"]},"description":"A finite query tree finds the cheapest complete repair, rather than the cheapest first read.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-10-05","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"The price of another question\nA summary can tell us that an answer is missing without supplying the answer. Repair becomes possible only when an available source can still distinguish the states that need different answers. Unlike a distinction erased without a supplement, the original source here remains available, and an observer can ask exact questions of it without changing it. The question is how much access that distinction costs. The available, nondestructive source is essential to the calculation: an experiment whose measurement alters its subject would need another model.\nA cheap first question is not the whole cost\nSuppose the hidden state is one of A, B or C. The retained summary is identical in all three cases, so identifying the state requires another read. Three source queries are available: asking “Is it A?” costs one unit, asking “Is it B?” costs two, and reading the complete identity costs four. These costs are stipulated for a toy model, not observed retrieval prices.\nAsk the first question. If its answer is yes, stop. Otherwise ask the second:\nThe query tree identifies A for one unit and either B or C for three units.\nEach leaf carries the total paid along its path. Removing the second query leaves the cheap-first strategy with a cost-five branch, while a direct complete read still costs four.\nThe tree's worst-case cost is three, even though one branch finishes after a single read.\nNo tree can guarantee the answer within two. The first query leaves B and C unresolved, and the second alone leaves A and C unresolved. Distinguishing either remaining pair requires paying for another question. The direct identity read costs four. Three is consequently the minimum worst-case cost for this query set.\nNow remove the second query. A cheap-first strategy costs five in its unresolved branch: one for the first question, then four for the full read. Reading the full identity immediately costs four. The cheaper individual query has become the worse first choice.\nOptimise over the questions that actually remain\nLet S be the states still compatible with what is known. If the required answer is constant on S, set V(S)=0: no further query is needed. Otherwise choose an available question, pay its cost, and continue inside the subset compatible with its answer.\nFor finite states, queries and outcomes, the minimum worst-case value has the recurrence\n\nV(S)=\\min_{j\\,\\text{productive on }S}\\left[c_j+\\max_{o:S_{j,o}\\ne\\varnothing}V(S_{j,o})\\right],\n\\qquad S_{j,o}=\\{x\\in S:q_j(x)=o\\},\n\nwhere the minimum considers productive queries: those that actually split S. A query that gives the same answer throughout S can be removed from an exact nondestructive tree without losing information. Each productive branch contains fewer compatible states, providing a finite recursion even when a useful query is free.\nThis is the Bellman principle in the declared query problem. Finding the best tree can still be computationally expensive for a large finite problem.\nUnavailable information cannot be purchased with more reasoning\nIf two compatible states give the same answer to every available query but require different final answers, adaptation cannot help. They follow the same branch after every query and reach the same leaf. No correct tree exists for both.\nConversely, zero repair cost need not mean the answer was stored. A free measurement of an accessible original bit can recover that bit from a constant summary. The result is free only within the declared access model; storage, latency, permission and real instrumentation have not vanished because the mathematical query cost is zero.\nWorst-case and expected cost serve different promises\nOur tree costs one for A and three for B or C. With equal probabilities,\n\n\\mathbb E[\\text{cost}]=\\frac{1+3+3}{3}=\\frac73,\n\\qquad\\max_x\\text{cost}(x)=3.\n\nChanging the probabilities changes the expectation without changing that guarantee. A hard deadline may need a worst-case allowance, while a repeated operation may also care about expected expenditure. A method can be cheaper under one promise without being cheaper under the other.\nMONAD's formal repair branch establishes feasibility and minimum-cost results within exact, available-source query models. The calculation changes what it means to preserve a useful record. A summary may deliberately omit a distinction if an admitted source can recover it at an acceptable cost. That promise belongs to the summary and the access path together. When the source or query set changes, the same retained bytes can support a different answer, a more expensive one, or none at all.\n","image":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/the-price-of-another-question/query-tree.svg","description":"The query tree identifies A for one unit and either B or C for three units.","caption":"Each leaf carries the total paid along its path. Removing the second query leaves the cheap-first strategy with a cost-five branch, while a direct complete read still costs four.\n"}],"url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/the-price-of-another-question/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/","identifier":"journey:causality-from-summary-to-repair","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From summary to repair","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/","identifier":"collection:causality-preservation-under-uncertainty","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Preservation under uncertainty","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Changing descriptions","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Preservation under uncertainty","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/"}}]}],"description":"What remains usable when observations are noisy, experiments are simulated and approximations accumulate?","text":"An observation can leave uncertainty and still support a useful decision. The promise then depends on the error we permit, the source of another observation and the transformations the evidence undergoes. Exact recovery, a bounded decision risk and closeness to a previous description are different successes.\nThe distinction matters over a sequence of changes. Repeating a measurement can supply new evidence; processing one report cannot do the same job. A simulator can preserve a decision well enough without reproducing the original experiment. A locally acceptable replacement can also carry a chain beyond its first tolerance. These are related questions about what a description preserves, but answering one does not settle the others.\n","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/","identifier":"collection:causality-changing-descriptions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Changing descriptions","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/"}],"hasPart":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/recovering-a-distinction-from-a-noisy-answer/#article","identifier":"content:monad-a-noisy-answer-is-not-an-exact-repair","name":"A noisy answer is not an exact repair","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/recovering-a-distinction-from-a-noisy-answer/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/how-tolerances-accumulate/#article","identifier":"content:monad-approximate-is-not-equivalent","name":"How tolerances accumulate","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/how-tolerances-accumulate/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/one-experiment-through-another/#article","identifier":"content:monad-one-experiment-through-another","name":"When one experiment can stand in for another","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/one-experiment-through-another/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/how-tolerances-accumulate/#article","identifier":"content:monad-approximate-is-not-equivalent","@type":"Article","name":"How tolerances accumulate","headline":"How tolerances accumulate","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["accumulated approximation","nontransitive tolerance","total-variation distance","fixed-reference comparison"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/how-tolerances-accumulate/","@type":"WebPage","name":"How tolerances accumulate","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/how-tolerances-accumulate/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Changing descriptions","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Preservation under uncertainty","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"How tolerances accumulate","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/how-tolerances-accumulate/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/compression-is-a-promise/"]},"description":"Each replacement stays close to its predecessor. How far can the chain travel from the original?","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-10-08","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"How tolerances accumulate\nEach replacement is close enough to the component it replaces. After several replacements, the result is no longer close enough to the component we started with. Nothing had to fail a local check. What travelled through the chain was permission to vary, not a guarantee that the whole chain stayed within the first allowance.\nChoose a positive tolerance \\varepsilon. Let three values be 0, 0.75\\varepsilon and 1.5\\varepsilon. The first two are within tolerance; so are the last two. The endpoints are not. If “close enough” is treated as an equivalence relation, grouping through the middle value merges a pair the original rule meant to distinguish. The calculation is small, but it changes what a sequence of individually acceptable edits can promise.\nFor Bernoulli laws, total-variation distance is the absolute difference between success probabilities. With tolerance 0.1, the same construction becomes:\nThe rolling comparison accepts both changes of 0.075, while the fixed-reference comparison rejects the final drift of 0.15 against tolerance 0.1.\nThe rolling comparison passes both replacements. The fixed comparison passes the first and rejects the second. At step zero there has been no replacement; both distances start at zero.\nThis is the ordinary difference between a distance threshold and a transitive relation, now expressed for observable probability laws rather than positions on a line.\nLocal acceptance does not settle accumulated drift\nImagine replacing one component repeatedly. Each candidate is accepted because it differs from its immediate predecessor by less than a tolerance. After enough replacements, the final component can be far from the initial reference. Comparing every candidate to that fixed reference would answer a different question, as would allocating a cumulative error budget and charging each change against it. Deliberately establishing a new reference is another legitimate policy, but it changes the comparison being promised. The error is to run the predecessor-only policy while claiming the guarantee of the fixed-reference policy.\nIf a distance obeys the triangle inequality and two steps have bounds \\delta_1 and \\delta_2, then\n\nd(P_0,P_2)\\leq d(P_0,P_1)+d(P_1,P_2)\\leq\\delta_1+\\delta_2.\n\nThe generic bound is the sum, not the larger allowance. Additional structure may improve it, but it has to be shown.\nThe loss the distance controls\nA representation can be close in one sense and poor in another. Two probability laws can be close under a declared measure while a chosen rare-event decision remains sensitive. Two models can agree on average accuracy while disagreeing on a protected task. A small reconstruction error need not mean low decision regret, and neither is a measurement of repair cost.\nFor a concrete total-variation statement, take a loss \\ell with 0\\leq\\ell\\leq1. Then\n\n\\left|\\mathbb E_P[\\ell]-\\mathbb E_Q[\\ell]\\right|\\leq\\operatorname{TV}(P,Q).\n\nThis controls the change in expected loss across the declared observation laws. The loss range matters: multiplying the loss by a thousand multiplies the possible difference too.\nA common simulator lets one experiment supply observations for a decision rule designed for another. Its uniform error buys a bounded-loss transfer guarantee. That is another use of total variation, with its own condition: the simulator must work without knowing the hidden answer.\nExact classes and approximate neighbourhoods serve different jobs\nAn exact quotient groups states on which every question the description must answer agrees. That gives genuine classes, and each such answer is a function of the class. Approximate neighbourhoods instead carry tolerances and comparison procedures. Their overlap is not a licence to merge them into exact classes.\nMONAD keeps these branches separate. Its research question needs approximation, but adding approximation does not merely decorate the exact definitions with a small epsilon. It changes what can compose and what a downstream caller may conclude.\nA tolerance lets a system accept variation instead of demanding an exact copy. Its usefulness depends on carrying the comparison through subsequent changes: the reference, observation family and cumulative allowance determine what remains promised. Approximation gains that flexibility by keeping the error visible, rather than by turning overlapping neighbourhoods into identities.\n","image":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/how-tolerances-accumulate/fixed-and-rolling-reference.svg","description":"The rolling comparison accepts both changes of 0.075, while the fixed-reference comparison rejects the final drift of 0.15 against tolerance 0.1.","caption":"The rolling comparison passes both replacements. The fixed comparison passes the first and rejects the second. At step zero there has been no replacement; both distances start at zero.\n"}],"url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/how-tolerances-accumulate/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/","identifier":"collection:causality-preservation-under-uncertainty","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Preservation under uncertainty","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/one-experiment-through-another/#article","identifier":"content:monad-one-experiment-through-another","@type":"Article","name":"When one experiment can stand in for another","headline":"When one experiment can stand in for another","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["comparison of experiments","Le Cam deficiency","simulation deficiency","common-kernel simulation","bounded-loss risk transfer"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/one-experiment-through-another/","@type":"WebPage","name":"When one experiment can stand in for another","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/one-experiment-through-another/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Changing descriptions","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Preservation under uncertainty","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"When one experiment can stand in for another","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/one-experiment-through-another/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/how-tolerances-accumulate/"]},"description":"A common simulator transfers a decision rule between observation laws. Its error controls the change in bounded loss.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-10-09","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"When one experiment can stand in for another\nA decision rule expects observations from one experiment, but we can run another. To reuse the rule, we need a way to turn the available observations into the kind it expects. Matching an average output is too weak: the transformation has to work across the unknown conditions the decision is meant to distinguish.\nOne transformation across the unknown conditions\nComparing observations from two experiments introduces another promise. Let E_\\theta and F_\\theta be their observation laws under an unknown condition \\theta. A simulator K processes an observation from E to resemble one from F. It must be the same Markov kernel for every condition: a simulator given the hidden answer could manufacture precisely the evidence a decision needs.\nSuppose its uniform error is\n\n\\sup_\\theta\\operatorname{TV}(KE_\\theta,F_\\theta)\\leq\\delta.\n\nAny decision rule D designed for F can then run after K on E. With a loss in [0,1], its expected loss changes by at most \\delta in each condition. The reason is concrete: the decision and its loss together are another bounded test of the simulated law. The guarantee concerns using that rule through that simulator, not a claim that the two experiments are identical.\nTake two stipulated binary sensors for a condition \\theta\\in\\{0,1\\}. The first reports the correct bit with probability 0.9; the second with probability 0.8. Flip the first report independently with probability 1/8. Its probability of remaining correct becomes\n\n0.9(1-1/8)+0.1(1/8)=0.8.\n\nThis one kernel simulates the second sensor exactly in both conditions. A decision rule expecting the second sensor therefore has exactly its old risk after the simulation. If the target instead reports correctly with probability 0.82, the same simulated output is within total variation 0.02 of it in either condition. Guessing the reported bit has risk 0.2 after simulation and 0.18 under the target: a difference of 0.02, meeting the bound. The numbers describe a constructed comparison, not a sensor experiment.\nThe smallest attainable error raises a further mathematical question. Simulation deficiency is the infimum of the uniform errors over common kernels. It follows the deficiency Lucien Le Cam introduced for comparing statistical experiments. An infimum need not have a minimising kernel, so the general transfer uses a kernel within a positive tolerance of it rather than assuming an exact best simulator exists. MONAD formalises that forward guarantee. It does not turn a small decision difference into a converse construction of an affordable simulator; feasibility and resource cost need their own account.\nComposing simulators also spends an error allowance\nSuppose K simulates F from E within uniform total variation \\delta_1, and L simulates G from F within \\delta_2. Their composition obeys\n\n\\operatorname{TV}(LKE_\\theta,G_\\theta)\n\\leq \\operatorname{TV}(LKE_\\theta,LF_\\theta)+\\operatorname{TV}(LF_\\theta,G_\\theta)\n\\leq\\delta_1+\\delta_2.\n\nThe second inequality uses contraction of total variation under the same Markov kernel L. It does not let the two tolerances collapse into one. Accumulated drift asks why overlapping neighbourhoods fail to give exact classes; simulation asks whether a single admissible transformation can carry evidence into another decision problem. Keeping both the transformation and its error explicit makes the forward promise useful.\n","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/one-experiment-through-another/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/","identifier":"collection:causality-preservation-under-uncertainty","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Preservation under uncertainty","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/recovering-a-distinction-from-a-noisy-answer/#article","identifier":"content:monad-a-noisy-answer-is-not-an-exact-repair","@type":"Article","name":"A noisy answer is not an exact repair","headline":"A noisy answer is not an exact repair","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["noisy supplements","binary decision risk","independent repeated observations","zero-error recovery","information acquisition"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/recovering-a-distinction-from-a-noisy-answer/","@type":"WebPage","name":"A noisy answer is not an exact repair","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/recovering-a-distinction-from-a-noisy-answer/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Changing descriptions","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Preservation under uncertainty","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"A noisy answer is not an exact repair","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/recovering-a-distinction-from-a-noisy-answer/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/compression-is-a-promise/"]},"description":"A one-bit report can be useful without restoring the missing bit. Repetition changes risk only when it supplies new evidence.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-10-12","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"A noisy answer is not an exact repair\nA summary may preserve one part of a state and discard another. If a new question needs the discarded bit, a fresh report can help without making the answer certain. That difference is easy to miss when the report and an exact supplement both occupy one binary symbol.\nOne report, two possible original states\nSuppose that within each retained-summary class the missing bit is equally likely to be zero or one, and a source reports it incorrectly with probability 0.1 in either case. Reading the report supplies useful evidence. It cannot supply an exact supplement that recovers the bit in every state: either received value remains possible under either original bit.\nFor this symmetric source and equal prior, using the reported bit is the best decision, with error probability 0.1. Three reads with conditionally independent errors let a majority vote reduce that error to\n\n3(0.1)^2(0.9)+(0.1)^3=0.028.\n\nThe improvement comes from new source observations, not further processing of the first report. Copying that report three times leaves the error at 0.1. Even genuinely independent repetition cannot give a perfect finite guarantee here: every finite sequence of reports has positive probability under either original bit.\nAlphabet size, channel information and acquisition cost now answer different questions. Two supplement symbols can represent the exact missing answer when the encoder knows it and delivery preserves it. A noisy report carries information while leaving a chance of the wrong answer. Asking again spends access or measurement resources to change that chance. A repair can trade those costs against an acceptable error, but it has changed the promise from answering every state correctly to controlling a declared risk. The smallest exact repair tree and the best statistical test consequently solve different problems.\nMONAD studies the distinction between exact preservation and comparison under uncertainty. An exact summary is sufficient for a family of questions. A statistical observation instead gives a decision some attainable risk under a declared law. Both accounts can be precise, but their hypotheses determine what the word ‘repair’ can mean.\n","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/recovering-a-distinction-from-a-noisy-answer/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/","identifier":"collection:causality-preservation-under-uncertainty","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Preservation under uncertainty","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/when-the-description-must-change/#article","identifier":"content:monad-when-the-description-must-change","@type":"Article","name":"When the description must change","headline":"When the description must change","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["changing description languages","representational adequacy","path-dependent relevance","permitted continuations","abstract interpretation"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/when-the-description-must-change/","@type":"WebPage","name":"When the description must change","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/when-the-description-must-change/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Changing descriptions","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"When the description must change","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/when-the-description-must-change/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/compression-is-a-promise/","https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/what-should-a-surprise-change/"]},"description":"A perfect estimate cannot supply a relation its language never represented. What would make that inadequacy visible?","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-09-24","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"When the description must change\nSuppose a model estimates every quantity available to it perfectly. We still cannot decide what to do. The missing answer concerns a relation its language never represented, so a better estimate has nowhere to put it. That failure asks more than another inaccurate prediction does: how can a system recognise that it needs another kind of description, rather than another value inside this one?\nThat is the larger question behind MONAD. Compression is one entry into it, because a summary makes an explicit choice about which distinctions survive. The programme asks how to follow that choice through changes in questions, allowed continuations and the description itself. It begins with sets and functions rather than assuming that every needed distinction is a coordinate in a vector space.\nA correct answer to the smaller question\nConsider a small inventory system with two bits: a says whether a replacement part is available; b says whether it is compatible with a particular machine. A dashboard preserves only a. If the question is “is anything in stock?”, that is sufficient. A perfect predictor of a cannot answer “can this machine be repaired?” when repair requires both a and b. States 10 and 11 have the same dashboard answer and different repair answers.\nThere are several materially different ways this problem can arise. The compatibility information might still be in an accessible original record, so the dashboard hid it. The original might have been discarded, making repair impossible from the retained material. Compatibility might have existed in the old carrier, the things the description represents, but never been an admitted question, one the summary was asked to keep answerable. Or the carrier might represent only stock counts, with no objects for machines, interfaces or compatibility relations at all. Asking for a new column in that last case actually asks for a different description of the situation. These failures should not all be called information loss.\nThe mathematical test is elementary and useful. Let q:X\\to S be the summary and f:X\\to Y a required question. The summary answers it exactly when there is a function g:S\\to Y such that\n\nf=g\\circ q.\n\nIf f distinguishes two states that q identifies, no such g exists. That is the fixed-context promise of compression. Failure to factor establishes what this summary cannot answer; it does not yet tell us whether the remedy is another query, a refined summary or a richer carrier.\nRelevance belongs to a path\nTwo machines can have the same current stock and compatibility status but different maintenance histories. Suppose an overheating event leaves a required inspection before a replacement may be used. An inspection flag i now matters:\nDescription\tWhat it can decide\tWhat is still absent\t\nStock bit a\tA part is available\tCompatibility and inspection history\t\nPair (a,b)\tA compatible part is available\tWhether an inspection is still required\t\nTriple (a,b,i)\tRepair is allowed under this inspection rule\tWhich other historical consequences matter\t\nMachine–part compatibility relation\tCompatibility across several machines and parts\tThe rule for extending this relation as interfaces change\t\nThe last row is not just another flag on the original dashboard. It introduces objects and a relation that a stock-only carrier did not express. Choosing which historical consequences deserve flags is also a modelling question, not an automatic consequence of adding storage.\nPermitted continuations matter as well. A distinction irrelevant to a routine inspection may become decisive after an admitted upgrade. If a future question can separate states that the present summary merges, preservation must account for that possibility or state the limitation. “Future” here means the continuations considered in the problem, not tomorrow transmitting information into today. Restricting those continuations, allowing revision, retaining a recovery source or accepting a tolerance makes compression useful. Requiring exact answers to every separating future question would force an injective summary and leave no nontrivial collapse.\nThis joins four ambitions: admitting distinctions when the language is too small, compressing relative to meaningful continuations, accounting for preservation and recoverability, and following relevance along a trajectory. They constrain one another. A path cannot make a distinction relevant and then permit the account of preservation to ignore that the current representation cannot express it. The response may be refinement, a larger context or an explicit unresolved question; silently improving the score inside the old frame is not a response to that failure.\nCarrying an old answer into a new description\nA change of carrier needs a way to compare the two descriptions. Let e:X\\to X' transport an old state into a new carrier, and let f':X'\\to Y be a new question. On transported old states, the question is f'\\circ e. An old summary q:X\\to S preserves that answer precisely when some g:S\\to Y satisfies\n\nf'\\circ e=g\\circ q.\n\nThe obstruction is still concrete: find x,y with q(x)=q(y) but f'(e(x))\\ne f'(e(y)). This detects where the old summary fails to carry the new question, relative to the chosen transport. It neither constructs e nor justifies why those are the right states to compare.\nFor the two-bit inventory carrier, adding an inspection flag gives X'=X\\times\\{0,1\\}. If a recorded history supplies the flag through h:X\\to\\{0,1\\}, then e(x)=(x,h(x)) is a particular transport. But if the old state contains no history from which to determine inspection, h is missing information, not a function we can recover by notation. Two extended states (x,0) and (x,1) have the same old projection and can give different repair answers. The added coordinate needs a source.\nIntroducing machines, parts and their compatibility relation goes further. A stock count does not specify which machine an item fits. There may be several richer descriptions above the same old count, with no preferred one. Here a relation between descriptions may be more appropriate than a single chosen map; answers and permitted continuations also need their own transport. Agreement after relabelling an answer is different from discovering a new distinction. The fixed-map criterion makes one comparison exact while leaving that larger choice of carriers, relations and changing question families open.\nWhen the rules of change become objects\nA system can change a state, change the language describing that state, or change which transformations its descriptions permit. The third step makes the rules of change part of what the theory must describe. MONAD’s longer-term recursive goal lets transformations at one level become objects at another. The same problem then recurs: what does this higher-level description preserve, what can it no longer distinguish, and what would justify changing it?\nThat is more than adding another variable to an inventory record. It asks how a system can revise its representational possibilities while earlier judgments remain checkable or are explicitly marked as no longer portable. The theory does not become its own unrestricted judge. Each level still needs a stated comparison and a warrant for what it admits.\nWho supplied the questions?\nA preservation theorem is relative to its contract. It may establish that every admitted question survives and still leave out the question a person needed. This is why the origin and justification of the contract are part of the programme, alongside the mathematics of keeping it. A change to a description can propose a new question; it cannot establish the question's relevance simply by making itself answer it. The earlier search for a vocabulary began with that attraction to another way of seeing. The formal question gives the attraction a particular demand: which distinction became expressible, and what can we now do with it?\nThere is a further ambiguity even when a representation reconstructs every observation: which factors did it recover? Different latent coordinates can explain the same observed distribution. A preservation contract needs a reason to call one coordinate compatibility, inspection or something else; reconstruction by itself cannot provide that meaning.\nAbstract interpretation supplies established ways to reason about sound approximation and completeness relative to properties of a system. MONAD's inquiry is not a replacement name for that machinery. The question is how such precisely understood fixed-context constructions behave when the carrier, question family and permitted changes themselves become objects of change. Where an existing construction answers the question, the useful outcome is an exact correspondence, not a claim of a new theory.\nThe challenge is to make inadequacy visible before it becomes a confident answer. A residual can prompt the question, but surprise alone cannot distinguish noise from a missing variable or a language that has become too small. A calculus of changing descriptions would have to say what changed, where the new information came from, what earlier judgments still mean and which questions now demand another representation.\n","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/when-the-description-must-change/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/","identifier":"collection:causality-changing-descriptions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Changing descriptions","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/which-factors-did-we-recover/#article","identifier":"content:monad-which-factors-did-we-recover","@type":"Article","name":"Which factors did we recover?","headline":"Which factors did we recover?","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["latent-factor identification","observation-preserving transformations","disentanglement","controlled interventions","modelling contracts"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/which-factors-did-we-recover/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Which factors did we recover?","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/which-factors-did-we-recover/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Changing descriptions","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Which factors did we recover?","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/which-factors-did-we-recover/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/when-the-description-must-change/"]},"description":"Independent latent coordinates and perfect reconstruction need not identify the quantities we intended to describe.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-09-25","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Which factors did we recover?\nA model reconstructs the observations perfectly, and its learned coordinates are independent. We would like to call those coordinates the underlying factors. The difficulty is not necessarily that the fit is poor. Another set of coordinates may satisfy the same observational tests while mixing the quantities we intended to separate.\nThe observed distribution leaves a choice\nEven an unlimited collection of the same kind of observation may leave the intended factors unidentified. Locatello and colleagues' 2019 result concerns latent generative models with more than one factor and a factorised prior admitting a density. They construct invertible, entangling changes of latent coordinates which, with the corresponding change of decoder, preserve the observed distribution. A representation separating the original factors can mix the transformed ones. Identifying the intended factors therefore needs information beyond that observed distribution, such as explicit model and data biases or supervision. This is not an impossibility theorem for every representation-learning problem or for MONAD.\nA finite illustration makes the ambiguity visible without reproducing that continuous construction. Let two independent fair bits be U and V. Describe the same four states either by (U,V) or by (A,B)=(U\\mathbin{\\oplus}V,V), where \\oplus is exclusive-or:\nOriginal coordinates (U,V)\tChanged coordinates (A,B)\tProbability\t\n(0,0)\t(0,0)\t1/4\t\n(0,1)\t(1,1)\t1/4\t\n(1,0)\t(1,0)\t1/4\t\n(1,1)\t(0,1)\t1/4\t\nBoth coordinate pairs are uniform and independent. The inverse U=A\\oplus B, V=B lets a decoder reconstruct exactly the same observations. Independence and reconstruction therefore cannot tell us whether the first coordinate means U or parity. Yet changing V while holding U fixed changes both A and B. An observed, controlled change to the named factor supplies a comparison absent from the four observational frequencies.\nFor a modelling contract, that extra information has a concrete job. A label can associate a coordinate with a named quantity; a restricted model family can encode an assumption about how the quantities combine; an intervention witness can show what changes when one of them is altered. Each may exclude alternatives, under its own conditions. A useful test keeps an observation-preserving alternative beside the preferred account and asks which supplied evidence actually separates them. Choosing the questions after seeing the representation could otherwise make the representation pass a contract whose meaning it helped invent.\nMeaning has to enter somewhere\nThe inventory example behind a change of description names stock, compatibility and inspection because the problem already tells us what those quantities mean. A representation learner does not inherit that interpretation merely by finding three coordinates. The source of the names and relevant interventions is part of the modelling problem.\nThis is a narrower question than whether a description needs another carrier. It can arise with two invertibly related descriptions of exactly the same states. Their observational adequacy agrees, while their response to a named intervention differs. A test of factor recovery therefore has to say what information fixes the intended factors, and whether that information actually excludes the alternative representation.\n","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/which-factors-did-we-recover/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"mentions":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:monad","name":"MONAD","description":"A calculus for preserving, compressing and changing what a description can express.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/","identifier":"content:causality-project-monad","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MONAD","url":"https://causality.in/monad/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/descriptions-and-repair/#article","identifier":"content:monad-docs","name":"Descriptions and repair","url":"https://causality.in/monad/descriptions-and-repair/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/mentions/","identifier":"content:monad-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MONAD mentions","url":"https://causality.in/monad/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD/releases/download/v0.1.0/MONAD.pdf","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD/releases/download/v0.1.0/MONAD.pdf","name":"MONAD: Minimal Ontological Notions for Axiomatic Distinction","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/monad/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/monad/overview","name":"MONAD"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/","identifier":"collection:causality-changing-descriptions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Changing descriptions","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/","identifier":"collection:causality-composition-and-learning","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Composition and learning","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"description":"What can reasoning units learn through their organisation, and how can we distinguish that contribution from the abilities of their parts?","text":"This collection is about how a computation's organisation, which parts interact and what they keep, can be learned rather than declared.\nA reasoning architecture can contain more than a set of capable parts. Which parts interact, what experience they retain and how their roles change can themselves become learned computation. The attraction is an organisation shaped by the task: local specialisation, useful memory and information reaching the place where it changes an answer.\nMUON investigates the adaptive local unit; HENA investigates the collective. Their ambition reaches beyond placing stock models in a fixed workflow. A hierarchy could arise through local interaction and evolve with what the system is trying to do. An early cue reaching a later request is one concrete demand on that organisation, not its whole purpose.\nQ-MUON and Q-HENA ask the same two questions on a quantum substrate, so two essays here compare a quantum unit with its classical counterpart and ask what recovering a saved swarm can preserve once its resources have changed.\nMessages still consume capacity, memory choices lose things and local improvements can conflict. Following the interaction into its consequences reveals whether the learned organisation helped: what relation survived, which route carried it, what the group could answer and what the full computation cost.\n","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"hasPart":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/changing-the-rule/#article","identifier":"content:causality-change-the-rule-not-the-judge","name":"Change the rule without moving the finish line","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/changing-the-rule/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/what-should-a-surprise-change/#article","identifier":"content:muon-surprise-is-a-question-not-a-verdict","name":"What should a surprise change?","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/what-should-a-surprise-change/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/recovery-across-a-changing-world/#article","identifier":"content:q-hena-recovery-is-a-claim","name":"Recovery across a changing world","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/recovery-across-a-changing-world/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/quantum-comparison/#article","identifier":"content:q-muon-quantum-comparison","name":"Comparing a quantum unit with its classical counterpart","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/quantum-comparison/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/","identifier":"journey:causality-from-unit-to-swarm","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From unit to swarm","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/changing-the-rule/#article","identifier":"content:causality-change-the-rule-not-the-judge","@type":"Article","name":"Change the rule without moving the finish line","headline":"Change the rule without moving the finish line","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["evolutionary rule search","local update rules","quality-diversity niches","reward hacking","candidate ancestry"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/changing-the-rule/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Change the rule without moving the finish line","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/changing-the-rule/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Composition and learning","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Change the rule without moving the finish line","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/changing-the-rule/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/emergence-needs-a-comparison/","https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/four-questions-for-an-evaluation/"]},"description":"What happens when an evolving routing rule can improve its score by changing what counts as success?","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-10-21","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Change the rule without moving the finish line\nImagine a search discovering a routing rule with a better score. Looking closer, the improvement came from no longer counting missed deadlines. The rule has changed the answer to an easier question, while the original jobs still wait. What kind of variation would let a system improve its way of working without losing the problem it was meant to solve?\nConsider a toy search for a rule that routes jobs between two workers. One worker is faster for short jobs; the other handles long jobs more reliably. The objective combines completed work with a penalty for missed deadlines. A candidate which changes the routing threshold is a legitimate variation. One which drops missed jobs from the denominator changes the meaning of success. Both edits might increase the visible score, but only one addresses the declared problem. Protecting the denominator and the measurement procedure from anything the candidate may edit keeps that difference observable.\nA fixed workload passes through an editable routing candidate to a protected evaluator, with run feedback updating only permitted state.\nThe routing rule can change; the workload and the way success is measured stay outside the candidate's control.\nThe threshold may change which worker receives a job. Queue estimates may change within the state budget, and messages within the communication ceiling. The generator cannot supply easier jobs to a favoured candidate, and the evaluator continues counting every missed deadline. These boundaries make the candidate's extra expressiveness testable.\nA local rule has a collective expression\nOne way to make the search concrete is to let a candidate specify a local update rule: how a unit responds to its inputs, which messages it sends and which bounded internal state it retains. The candidate is the rule; the pattern produced by many units interacting is its collective expression. Changing the rule may change traffic concentration, specialisation or recovery after a unit disappears, even when every unit runs the same code. A topology drawn in advance and a topology shaped by those interactions therefore answer different questions.\nFor the two-worker example, a richer candidate could use a small local state for recent queue pressure and send a bounded message when its own deadline risk rises. The behaviour emerges over a workload rather than existing entirely in the threshold's source text. It could reduce missed deadlines, oscillate between workers or turn communication into the bottleneck. Evaluating only the local rule on isolated jobs would miss those effects; evaluating its collective behaviour under one workload would miss its sensitivity to another.\nOne illustrative rule keeps an exponentially smoothed queue estimate q_i at worker i:\n\nq_i(t+1)=(1-\\alpha)q_i(t)+\\alpha n_i(t),\\qquad 0\\leq\\alpha\\leq1,\n\nwhere n_i is its current queue length. The worker sends a pressure alert when q_i crosses a threshold \\kappa. A short job normally goes to the faster worker, but a pressure difference exceeding \\tau sends it to the other one, subject to that worker's availability. The candidate (\\alpha,\\kappa,\\tau) changes the local response without changing what counts as a completed job. Queue state, alert size and alert frequency still consume the declared budgets. A stale neighbour estimate can make the rule oscillate, so message timing is part of its expression, not an incidental transport detail.\nThe genome in this small construction is the update and its parameters. Its phenotype includes the resulting routes, queues, alerts and deadline failures across a workload. A threshold that works on isolated arrivals may produce a burst of redirects when both workers receive congestion alerts together. That failure gives the next mutation a specific object to change: smoothing, alert timing or rerouting, rather than an undifferentiated demand for a higher score.\nThe research proposal limits what a candidate may edit. A mutation can vary a local update, a message decision or a parameter the experiment exposes, while the workload generator, resource accounting and outcome definition stay out of its reach. The first question is whether such variation discovers useful collective behaviour at all. Allowing the search to rewrite its own mutation machinery is another level of change, with another evaluator problem, rather than a free extension of the first experiment.\nImproving one niche and selecting across niches are different operations\nLocal improvement asks whether a small edit improves a particular rule under its existing conditions. Population selection asks which rules deserve further attention across different conditions. A diverse archive can preserve a rule that is excellent for long jobs even if another dominates the common short-job workload, but keeping specialists also changes what a leaderboard can mean. Incomparable niche scales make global ranking arbitrary; a single global score can instead erase the specialists the archive was meant to preserve. Local improvement and population selection therefore need different comparisons.\nCausality proposes separating validation-gated edits inside a niche from selection across a population. The experiment would start with frozen mutation and evaluation machinery, candidates held within fixed limits and recorded ancestry, making it possible to compare local improvement with the population’s ability to keep useful specialists.\nThis mechanism has a place in the architectural programme. MUON supplies the unit boundary against which a local rule is defined; HENA would execute the rules and population dynamics. The aim is to discover useful organisation, including specialisation and routes that a designer did not prescribe for every case. What the search edits is the local rule inside a fixed experiment, not a live model’s architecture.\nThere is a nearby practical comparison in changing an agent's procedure while keeping its model fixed. SkillOpt uses trajectory feedback and validation to search in that text space. Its target helps locate the difference here: the proposed routing experiment varies a collective update rule, not just a procedure executed by one agent. A validation-gated search can supply a useful pattern without transferring its results to the new candidate or workload.\nThe safety control is also a research variable\nA frozen evaluator and an editable one create different experiments. In the routing example, allowing a candidate to drop missed jobs from its score exposes the temptation to improve the mark without improving the work. An independent count of completed jobs and missed deadlines can then reveal the exploit. That count must stay out of the candidate's reach, or the comparison loses the means to distinguish improvement from tampering.\nLocal selection adds another question. It can preserve useful specialists that a global average would discard, but a niche can also develop a locally convenient deception. The relation between locality and reward hacking is therefore an experimental variable, not a safety property conferred by distributing the population.\nWhat a rejected change contributes\nA candidate's ancestry explains how it arrived, including revisions that made performance worse. A rejected routing rule can make a later search less wasteful when its workload, evaluator and failure remain identifiable. Its value is the specific contrast: this routing rule, under this evaluator and workload, performed worse. Remembering only that it lost says little about the next candidate, while an explanation of why it failed remains another hypothesis. Retaining both the result and that distinction lets a later search reuse the evidence without turning one failed proposal into a universal prohibition.\nAn ancestry record would distinguish a child that lowers \\tau from one that changes the alert policy, and keep the parent, edit, niche and comparison attached to each. If the first child improves short-job throughput but worsens long-job deadlines, it may be rejected in one niche and worth testing in another. If a later child also changes \\alpha, the earlier result cannot be attributed to that new pair of edits. Rejected siblings are consequently part of the search history, not missing rungs on a straight ladder of winners. Search cost includes their trials too.\nSearch and use have different boundaries\nA sandbox can let candidates vary aggressively without allowing each variation to replace a live component. The protected evaluator and its separate evaluation coordinates give those variations something stable to answer to. A live change also needs the operator's decision; generating it is a different operation from using it.\nThe routing example makes the boundary concrete. A better completion rate can come from allocating work more effectively or from dropping missed jobs out of the denominator. Only the first improves the work. Letting the rule evolve is useful precisely because the independent count can still tell those outcomes apart.\n","image":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/changing-the-rule/what-can-change.svg","description":"A fixed workload passes through an editable routing candidate to a protected evaluator, with run feedback updating only permitted state.","caption":"The routing rule can change; the workload and the way success is measured stay outside the candidate's control.\n"}],"url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/changing-the-rule/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/","identifier":"collection:causality-composition-and-learning","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Composition and learning","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/","identifier":"journey:causality-from-unit-to-swarm","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From unit to swarm","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Composition and learning","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"From unit to swarm","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/"}}]}],"description":"Compare a unit's contract, a composition's joint constraints and the contribution of its organisation.","text":"How much of a collective answer belongs to its parts, and how much to their arrangement? We need a usable component boundary before that question can be tested. Even then, individually adequate units can lose a joint fact or compete for the same capacity.\nThe progression therefore builds from substitution to composition before asking what learned organisation contributes. Otherwise a swarm's apparent gain could come from a stronger model hidden in one slot, or its failure from a broken component rather than the routing being studied. Keeping those alternatives visible gives the organisational experiment a particular problem to solve.\n","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/","identifier":"collection:causality-composition-and-learning","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Composition and learning","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/"}],"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/three-tests-for-a-reasoning-unit/#article","identifier":"content:muon-a-shared-interface-is-not-an-equal-model","name":"Three tests for a reasoning unit","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/three-tests-for-a-reasoning-unit/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/when-pairs-agree-and-the-whole-differs/#article","identifier":"content:hena-pairs-do-not-contain-the-whole","name":"Every pair can agree while the whole differs","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/when-pairs-agree-and-the-whole-differs/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/emergence-needs-a-comparison/#article","identifier":"content:hena-emergence-needs-a-comparison","name":"What would count as emergent hierarchy?","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/emergence-needs-a-comparison/"}}]}},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/emergence-needs-a-comparison/#article","identifier":"content:hena-emergence-needs-a-comparison","@type":"Article","name":"What would count as emergent hierarchy?","headline":"What would count as emergent hierarchy?","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["emergent hierarchy","local state updates","sparse learned routing","distributed records","communication rounds"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/emergence-needs-a-comparison/","@type":"WebPage","name":"What would count as emergent hierarchy?","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/emergence-needs-a-comparison/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Composition and learning","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"From unit to swarm","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"What would count as emergent hierarchy?","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/emergence-needs-a-comparison/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/when-pairs-agree-and-the-whole-differs/","https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/three-tests-for-a-reasoning-unit/"]},"description":"An early cue and a later request reach different units. Can their learned organisation carry the relation into an answer?","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-11-02","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"What would count as emergent hierarchy?\nAn early cue reaches one unit; a much later request reaches another. Neither sees the whole stream. Somewhere between them, the cue has to survive, find a route and become part of the answer. Can the units learn an organisation that makes that happen, rather than relying on a designer to place the right connection in advance?\nThat is the constructive question in HENA. Sparse communication, limited memory and local rules give the units a way to interact; useful hierarchy would have to emerge through what those interactions contribute. A nested arrangement is easy to draw. The harder problem is discovering an arrangement whose changes help the task, and understanding why it helps more than a simpler one.\nFrom an explicit organisation to a learned one\nPale makes an organisation explicit enough to operate: responsibilities, capabilities, context and coordination are described and compiled. HENA’s architectural direction goes further. Could more of that organisation arise and evolve from intent through interactions among local units? The intended result is a collective model whose learned organisation carries part of its intelligence, rather than a fixed workflow arranging otherwise unchanged models.\nThis is why hierarchy matters beyond an attractive arrangement of nodes. Specialisation, memory and routes would have to become useful responses to what the collective is trying to do. A future HENA could stand alone; a surrounding harness could retain its responsibilities for external effects and resources while less of the internal coordination needed to be specified by hand. The cue example gives one small demand on that larger architecture.\nThe work happens between units\nA concrete candidate gives each unit a local slice of input \\tilde x_i, state h_i, neighbour messages m_i and reads r_i from a limited record store:\n\nh_i(t+1)=f_\\theta\\bigl(h_i(t),\\tilde x_i(t),m_i(t),r_i(t)\\bigr),\n\\qquad\nm_i(t)=\\sum_{j\\in\\mathcal N_i(t)}a_{ij}(t)g_\\theta\\bigl(h_j(t)\\bigr).\n\nThe neighbourhood \\mathcal N_i(t) and weights a_{ij}(t) are subject to a routing policy. These are candidate update rules, not a trained HENA model. Their interest is that each unit's access is local while the task may require a relation distributed across several units.\nImagine a long stream containing a machine's maintenance cue near its beginning and a repair request much later. One unit processes the cue; another receives the request. The first has to preserve an interpretable record, the second has to reach it, and the answer has to use the relation between them. Sending every input to every unit avoids part of the problem by abandoning locality. Having a powerful central unit read the full stream can solve it by abandoning the many-unit explanation. A useful sparse composition would have to carry the relevant distinction through its actual messages and memory operations.\nEach round can read records, exchange messages, update local state and write selected records before a subset produces an answer. The proposed objective combines task loss with communication and latency costs, and a declared term controlling routing entropy. The direction of that last term matters: discouraging uncertainty can collapse traffic onto one route, while encouraging dispersion can send irrelevant messages everywhere. A sparse graph is not a substitute for specifying what routing is meant to learn.\nConsider an illustrative three-unit trace with only two retained-record slots. Unit A receives “machine 7 requires inspection” and writes a record carrying the machine identity and requirement. Intervening events compete for the second slot; retaining them must not silently evict the first. When B later receives a request to repair machine 7, it needs more than a generic “maintenance happened” message. A route through C can carry the key needed to find A's record, after which B can combine that record with the current request. Sending a key, reading its record and applying the inspection rule are three separate operations, each observable in the trace.\nThis trace suggests distinct ablations. Erase the machine identity while retaining the same-sized record and the matching relation disappears. Keep the record but disable the learned route, and the information survives without reaching B. Deliver the record but remove its influence on the answer, and retrieval succeeds while the task fails. These are constructed failure modes, not HENA results. They give a collective experiment something more precise to explain than an attractive cluster diagram.\nThere is an established constructive precedent for local learned rules. In Growing Neural Cellular Automata, cells perceive a local neighbourhood and share a learned state-update rule; training shapes the resulting pattern, and different training regimes affect persistence and regeneration. That experiment shows how a local update can participate in a global process. HENA asks a different task question about reasoning, adaptive communication and limited records. The analogy suggests mechanisms to study, not a transfer of the cellular automaton's result.\nSparse edges do not make the whole computation cheap\nSuppose N units each send at most k messages per round and interact for R rounds. Then the send count obeys\n\nM\\leq NkR.\n\nFor 64 units, four outgoing messages and eight rounds, the ceiling is 2,048 sends. All-to-all communication without self-messages over the same eight rounds would make 64\\times63\\times8=32{,}256 sends. The comparison holds the round count fixed; it does not silently compare eight sparse rounds with one dense round. Nor is a send count a runtime measurement: payload size, routing, memory access and inference remain part of the cost. If the rounds grow with N, an apparent scaling benefit can disappear.\nThe intended experiment must measure the whole answer, not only the sparsity of one adjacency matrix. A system can save communication while spending more compute searching for routes. It can save memory by forgetting the very relation the task requires. Joint facts are a simple way to make that failure visible.\nA per-unit store with K vectors of width d has Kd scalar positions; across N units the raw vector state has NKd, before keys, provenance and indexing. Sparse selected edges do not make edge selection sparse: a router that scores all N destinations per unit still performs a dense search before sending its few messages. Record lookup can have the same hidden cost. Measuring destination candidates examined, payload bytes, reads, writes and rounds alongside inference exposes where a proposed saving actually occurs.\nOrganisation has to change the answer\nAn imposed hierarchy gives the adaptive organisation something concrete to improve on. At the same task and budgets, the designer's best fixed hierarchy may already carry the needed relation efficiently; a flat organisation or a single unit may do so with less coordination. Those comparisons can reveal a contribution that a changing graph alone leaves invisible: the organisation has learnt a useful route, rather than merely learnt to redraw itself.\nOne possible question is whether units specialise into stable roles and route information to the right place under a changing workload. A topology can look orderly while contributing nothing to the answer. Removing its learned routing while preserving compute supplies a sharper contrast: if performance remains unchanged, the apparent organisation may be incidental. If performance falls, the task, routing decisions and communication cost reveal what the organisation contributed. Removing a useful unit asks a different question about how that contribution survives disruption.\nThe criterion cannot be invented after seeing the prettiest run. Nor should every successful cluster count as a new level of description. A nested coarse-graining simply forgets distinctions at successive levels unless a separate argument establishes what new question became expressible.\nWhen the swarm becomes a costly single model\nSuppose most requests eventually reach one strong unit. The other units might be useful filters, or they might merely add delay before the same model answers. The distinction appears when the strong unit runs alone at the same total budget. Similarly, a sparse swarm that needs many rounds can spend its communication saving on repeated inference, while shared memory can circulate a mistaken premise until the whole group agrees with it. Each case changes the account of where the computation happens: filtering may help the answer, repeated inference may consume its saving, and shared agreement may preserve an error rather than useful knowledge.\nHENA's proposed first stock-model swarm would make those comparisons possible as an orchestration experiment. MUON's shared slot would allow its units to vary while the task and resource boundary stayed fixed. A later differentiable architecture would ask how the units can learn routing and organisation together, with the stock-model comparison supplying a baseline. Useful specialisation, information that reaches the right place, and the cost of making that happen are the contributions that would give the learned organisation its meaning.\n","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/emergence-needs-a-comparison/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/","identifier":"journey:causality-from-unit-to-swarm","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From unit to swarm","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/three-tests-for-a-reasoning-unit/#article","identifier":"content:muon-a-shared-interface-is-not-an-equal-model","@type":"Article","name":"Three tests for a reasoning unit","headline":"Three tests for a reasoning unit","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["reasoning-unit contracts","task adequacy","model substitution","swarm cost accounting","four-arm comparison"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/three-tests-for-a-reasoning-unit/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Three tests for a reasoning unit","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/three-tests-for-a-reasoning-unit/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Composition and learning","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"From unit to swarm","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Three tests for a reasoning unit","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/three-tests-for-a-reasoning-unit/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/emergence-needs-a-comparison/","https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/quantum-comparison/"]},"description":"A replacement model returns a valid reply but fails the caller's task; the shared boundary gives three different things to investigate.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-10-28","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Three tests for a reasoning unit\nReplace the model behind an interface and the caller still receives a valid reply. On a harder task, the answer fails; under load, the successful answer arrives too late. What did the shared interface let us preserve? It made the replacement possible, but a valid reply is only part of what the caller needs. The new model also has to do the task within the available time and resources.\nMUON starts with a unit slot: inference, declared state, ordered records, resource behaviour and lifecycle operations. Behind that boundary, its longer-term micro-transformer design asks how temporal processing, routed computation and distributed records could form a small reasoning unit. The interface lets those internals change without making their differences disappear.\nThe native unit is intended to be a local building block of an intelligence, not only a wrapper around a larger model. Its smallness makes organisation among units necessary: useful computation, experience and specialisation must combine through HENA rather than be assumed to exist inside every part. The contract is meant to let units work together while each unit's architecture develops.\nThe state a unit carries from earlier inputs can contribute in two quite different ways. It can choose which of the unit's routed computations, its experts, runs, or it can carry information into the prediction itself. When a cue arrives well before the request that needs it, those two paths need separate tests: knowing which computation to select is not the same as preserving the cue that computation needs. A direct path from that state to the output, compared with a control in which the state only routes, would expose the distinction; MUON's guide follows that comparison through a road notice. Whether several dependent transformations share one routing choice is another design decision, separate from the question of whether the final unit fits its caller.\nOne request through MUON's unit slot\nThe proposed slot makes a request inspectable before the model runs. The caller declares its requirements: input shape, streaming, deadline, state and replay needs. A capability report identifies the exact model, tokenizer and backend and describes what the filler supports. Its estimate of this request's demand is then compared with fixed capacity and current availability. Those are five different things: a requirement is not a capability, an estimated demand is not capacity, and reported availability is not a reservation.\nOnly an atomic reservation gives the request a live allocation. Two callers may both see enough free capacity; admitting both on that observation alone would overspend it. A lease binds the allocation and its lifetime. Expiry or a failed reservation leads to a typed refusal, rather than an inference that silently consumes another caller's budget.\nAfter admission, inference produces an output and may change the declared state. Ordered records connect input, output and resource events within an episode, step and sequence. A checkpoint captures the state according to its opaque, summary or typed descriptor; restoration must honour that descriptor's promise. Shutdown also belongs to the lifecycle and leaves a final record. This is the proposed dataflow that a stock filler would have to implement before its benchmark result could be interpreted as a MUON-slot result.\nThe MUON slot compares caller requirements and filler capabilities, estimates demand, reserves a lease or refuses, then records inference and lifecycle events in a host-owned store.\nRequirements, capabilities, estimated demand, capacity and availability remain distinct; the reservation is the step that allocates resources. Checkpoint, restore and shutdown contribute records alongside inference.\nThe record protocol and the store are separate again. A host can retain its own unit records using shared semantics without calling a research evaluator. That permits different local memory designs to be compared through a common record boundary, rather than requiring every unit to surrender its history to one central service.\nThree claims, three questions\nConformance asks whether the component honours the contract. Does it really stream when it says it does? Does its checkpoint restore the declared state? Does it refuse work it cannot reserve resources for, rather than silently truncating it?\nTask adequacy asks whether it meets a floor for a particular workload. Correct message shape does not answer that question. Nor does an accurate model identifier.\nSubstitution asks whether one component can replace another in a named context, under specified observations, tasks and tolerances. Two units might both exceed an accuracy floor but differ so much in latency or error distribution that only one fits a caller's needs. Opacity hides internals; it does not hide capability or erase differences.\nCount the work around the model\nConsider a hypothetical workload with a total compute allowance of 100 units. Ten small agents use six units each. Their router uses fifteen, memory and verification use ten, and failed attempts consume another fifteen. That uses the full hundred. Reporting only the sixty units spent inside agents makes the swarm look cheaper by excluding forty units of its own work. The same accounting has to apply to the single stronger model, which also needs orchestration and verification. Count rejected proposals in both systems.\nWork in the hypothetical composition\tCompute units\t\nTen units, six each\t60\t\nRouting\t15\t\nMemory and verification\t10\t\nFailed attempts\t15\t\nWhole answer\t100\t\nThe intended experiment has four arms: many identical units, one stronger unit, a mixture of units and a no-swarm control. Compare them on the same tasks, with the same capability floor and resource ceilings. Compute, latency, energy and communication may still move in different directions; keeping those costs visible would show what one arrangement saves and what it spends in exchange.\nA losing thesis can leave a useful architecture\nIf the many-small-unit arm loses after coordination is charged, the strongest admissible filler can still serve the slot. The interface remains useful even if the swarm thesis fails, because its contract lets the experiment change implementations without rewriting success criteria to protect a preferred result. Stock models would provide the first working controls; a proposed micro-transformer unit would have to earn its place later. Conversely, a swarm victory on one task would establish a reason to use that composition there, not a universal replacement for larger models.\nThe same discipline applies to a quantum counterpart. Preserving the outer contract establishes a common comparison boundary, not equal behaviour or quantum advantage. An interface is the beginning of a fair test, not its conclusion.\n","image":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/three-tests-for-a-reasoning-unit/unit-slot-and-dataflow.svg","description":"The MUON slot compares caller requirements and filler capabilities, estimates demand, reserves a lease or refuses, then records inference and lifecycle events in a host-owned store.","caption":"Requirements, capabilities, estimated demand, capacity and availability remain distinct; the reservation is the step that allocates resources. Checkpoint, restore and shutdown contribute records alongside inference.\n"}],"url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/three-tests-for-a-reasoning-unit/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/","identifier":"journey:causality-from-unit-to-swarm","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From unit to swarm","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/when-pairs-agree-and-the-whole-differs/#article","identifier":"content:hena-pairs-do-not-contain-the-whole","@type":"Article","name":"Every pair can agree while the whole differs","headline":"Every pair can agree while the whole differs","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["pairwise marginals","joint parity","shared resource capacity","n-ary relation encoding","swarm composition"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/when-pairs-agree-and-the-whole-differs/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Every pair can agree while the whole differs","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/when-pairs-agree-and-the-whole-differs/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Composition and learning","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"From unit to swarm","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Every pair can agree while the whole differs","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/when-pairs-agree-and-the-whole-differs/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/emergence-needs-a-comparison/"]},"description":"Matching pairs can leave incompatible wholes possible; parity and simultaneous reservations reveal different missing relations.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-10-29","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Every pair can agree while the whole differs\nEvery pair of reports agrees. Taken together, the reports still leave two incompatible worlds possible. How much of the whole did those pairwise agreements actually preserve? A tiny distribution gives the question an exact answer; a shared pool of resources reveals a different way locally satisfactory reports can fail to describe a working arrangement.\nThe statistic that every pair loses\nTwo laws on three bits differ only in which parity they admit:\nThe even law puts probability one quarter on 000, 011, 101 and 110, and the odd law on 001, 010, 100 and 111, yet every two-bit marginal is uniform under both.\nThe upper panel shows disjoint joint supports; every omitted probability is zero. The lower panel shows what survives projection: each two-bit outcome has probability one quarter under both laws.\nLook only at the first two positions. In either law, 00, 01, 10 and 11 each occur with probability 1/4. For example, the first law's 00 pair comes from 000; the second law's comes from 001. Dropping the third coordinate removes their difference. The same happens for either other pair. Every pairwise marginal agrees; the joint parity always differs.\nReconstructing the joint distribution from these marginals has no unique answer. In the relational version, joining the three pair projections admits all eight tuples, including the four the original relation excluded. The projections lost a constraint.\nThis is not the stronger claim that no global model exists. Both distributions are valid global models. Nor is it true that all labelled pairs of one concrete tuple lose that tuple: knowing its coordinate pairs determines its coordinates. Projecting a distribution or relation is a different operation from redundantly encoding one occurrence.\nThe request that every pair permits\nThree jobs each require four units from a pool of ten. Any pair requires eight and fits. All three require twelve and do not. A matrix of successful pair tests cannot authorise the aggregate allocation.\nHere the missing object is not a higher-order probability law. It is a simultaneous reservation. Reading the same availability snapshot three times and approving each request separately does not create twelve units. The allocator must account for accepted reservations together.\nThe two examples have a shared lesson but different mechanisms. Statistical projection loses a joint constraint; separate capacity decisions fail to account for a shared resource. Treating both as generic “emergence” conceals what needs fixing.\nA binary graph can still represent a many-way fact\nThe issue is not a competition between graph and hypergraph names. A graph can introduce an occurrence node and link it to its participants through typed roles. Its identity keeps the participants attached to one event instead of allowing arbitrary recombination. The W3C's n-ary relation patterns describe this established approach.\nThe encoding must preserve the constraint its user needs. Adding a node is not automatically sufficient: if its roles, time or occurrence identity disappear in an export, the export may again permit combinations the original excluded.\nWhat this asks of a swarm\nIn HENA, a useful unit is not evidence of a useful composition. Units can make correlated errors, interpret messages differently, contend for memory or jointly exceed a budget. A task may depend on a joint relation none of their isolated summaries retained.\nThese two failures give a proposed HENA experiment concrete tasks: concurrent requests that contend for shared capacity, and an answer that depends on a joint fact its messages must retain. Comparing the swarm with simpler alternatives at matched resources would reveal whether its organisation helps preserve those relations. A learned hierarchy would matter if its routing and memory carried those joint relations more effectively, rather than merely arranging units into nested groups.\n","image":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/when-pairs-agree-and-the-whole-differs/joint-and-marginal.svg","description":"The even law puts probability one quarter on 000, 011, 101 and 110, and the odd law on 001, 010, 100 and 111, yet every two-bit marginal is uniform under both.","caption":"The upper panel shows disjoint joint supports; every omitted probability is zero. The lower panel shows what survives projection: each two-bit outcome has probability one quarter under both laws.\n"}],"url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/when-pairs-agree-and-the-whole-differs/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/","identifier":"journey:causality-from-unit-to-swarm","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From unit to swarm","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/quantum-comparison/#article","identifier":"content:q-muon-quantum-comparison","@type":"Article","name":"Comparing a quantum unit with its classical counterpart","headline":"Comparing a quantum unit with its classical counterpart","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["quantum-classical comparison","quantum advantage","data preparation","measurement shots","barren plateaus","parameterised quantum circuits"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/quantum-comparison/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Comparing a quantum unit with its classical counterpart","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/quantum-comparison/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Composition and learning","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Comparing a quantum unit with its classical counterpart","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/quantum-comparison/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/three-tests-for-a-reasoning-unit/","https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/recovery-across-a-changing-world/"]},"description":"Where does a quantum candidate's useful contribution survive preparation, measurement and comparison with classical methods?","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-11-04","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Comparing a quantum unit with its classical counterpart\nSuppose the central step of a computation becomes dramatically shorter. Preparing its input and obtaining a sufficiently precise answer take longer than before. Has the computation become cheaper? A quantum candidate makes that familiar boundary question especially consequential: the circuit is part of a workload whose preparation, measurement and repetition may decide whether the change helps.\nQ-MUON proposes a quantum unit behind MUON's unit contract; Q-HENA proposes a corresponding swarm adaptation. A common outer boundary would make the change of substrate easier to examine: what task can the candidate perform, at which tolerated error, and for what complete cost?\nThe same interface supplies a question, not an answer\nIf both units accept a common task and return a common result shape, they can be compared. That establishes a boundary for observations; it does not establish equal accuracy, equal state or interchangeable checkpoints. The three tests for a reasoning unit separate these questions: conformance establishes whether each unit honours the contract, task adequacy tests whether it meets the workload's floor, and substitution concerns replacing one with the other in a specified context. Those answers give an advantage claim a meaningful object.\nPreparation, measurement and repetition\nThere are constructive questions worth putting inside that boundary. A quantum feature map could transform classical input before a kernel comparison; a parameterised circuit could learn a measurement-based response; a composite design could ask what correlations its units can establish and use. Each proposes a different place where the substrate might matter. None becomes a Q-MUON result merely by being a recognised research direction.\nCandidate mechanism\tQuestion it makes concrete\tWork the comparison must include\t\nQuantum feature map and kernel\tDoes this representation separate the chosen examples usefully?\tEncoding, kernel estimation, sample needs and classical kernel alternatives\t\nParameterised circuit\tCan its parameters learn the task response?\tOptimisation, repeated measurements and classical simulation where applicable\t\nComposition of quantum units\tDoes their interaction contribute to the collective answer?\tCommunication, usable correlations, unit preparation and collective evaluation\t\nChoosing a task would narrow one of these into an experiment. Keeping them distinct prevents “quantum reasoning” from standing in for an unspecified mechanism.\nSuppose a candidate circuit uses few internal operations but requires expensive data preparation and repeated shots to estimate its answer. Counting only the circuit's central step would compare different workloads. The classical system's preprocessing and verification must also be charged at the same boundary.\nThe task size and tolerated error decide how much repetition is needed before the circuit produces a usable answer. A simulation can expose how that cost behaves in the chosen model; device noise and calibration introduce another experiment. Wall-clock time also includes access arrangements, queues and classical work around the circuit. A shorter run through one provider cannot, by itself, isolate a substrate-level advantage.\nTrainability is itself a question\nThe barren-plateau result of McClean and colleagues identifies exponentially vanishing gradients for an important class of randomly initialised parameterised circuits. It is a reason to examine a proposed ansatz, not a theorem that every quantum learning design fails. Escaping that obstacle would make a construction easier to train, but would not establish an advantage over classical methods: the same construction may admit an effective classical simulation. The relevant comparison therefore includes appropriate classical methods, rather than a deliberately weak baseline or a convenient library default.\nWhether many quantum units can form a useful collective\nMUON and Q-MUON investigate the local building block; HENA and Q-HENA investigate how many units can form a useful intelligence. Q-HENA's target is a distributed quantum compute substrate with quantum communication, entanglement-mediated memory and hierarchy from quantum local rules. That is an architectural aspiration, not just a test harness around an isolated circuit. The comparisons locate what would make it worthwhile, while the exact unit and collective mechanisms remain research.\nKeeping the classical and quantum paths apart makes a negative result usable\nKeeping classical and quantum paths distinct avoids forcing every result into a succession story. A quantum component may lose on the intended task. It may win only within a narrow scale or error regime. It may remain inconclusive because the total comparison is too costly. Those are informative outcomes if the claim was fixed beforehand.\nCompatibility makes such findings portable only to the extent actually shown. Repeating a probability distribution, restoring a declared internal representation and recovering a live swarm are different promises. A component might preserve one while requiring the next run to start afresh. The useful comparison identifies which task benefit survives that change, and which preparation or recovery cost must be paid again.\n","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/quantum-comparison/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/","identifier":"collection:causality-composition-and-learning","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Composition and learning","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/recovery-across-a-changing-world/#article","identifier":"content:q-hena-recovery-is-a-claim","@type":"Article","name":"Recovery across a changing world","headline":"Recovery across a changing world","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["swarm recovery","checkpoint semantics","expired resource leases","observational equivalence","mixed restore modes"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/recovery-across-a-changing-world/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Recovery across a changing world","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/recovery-across-a-changing-world/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Composition and learning","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Recovery across a changing world","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/recovery-across-a-changing-world/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/quantum-comparison/"]},"description":"A saved swarm returns to a world whose leases and components have changed; what can its recovery actually preserve?","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-11-06","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Recovery across a changing world\nA saved process can remember what it believed. It cannot ensure that its resources, device or permissions still exist. Recovery has to reconcile the saved state with the current world, not simply deserialize the most convenient file.\nConsider a hypothetical swarm that reserved eight units from a pool of ten and then crashed. Its checkpoint records the reservation. During the interruption the lease expires and those eight units are reassigned. Loading the file cannot revive the old lease: doing so would let two holders claim the same capacity. Resuming the work requires a new reservation from the current allocator. If none is available, the process cannot resume even though its saved state loaded correctly. The checkpoint preserves a belief about capacity, while the current allocator determines whether that capacity can be used.\nThree restoration promises\nA restore may aim at the same declared state, a state equivalent under specified observations, or merely a fresh start. These are different promises. Even exact restoration of one component does not establish exact recovery of the surrounding system.\nFor a quantum unit, compatibility also depends on the backend, circuit representation, topology, calibration or noise model and measurement procedure. A classical checkpoint does not acquire quantum meaning because the new unit preserves the old API. Q-MUON's proposed profile separates exact, semantic and restart-only modes so that each candidate would declare which state and observations its restoration preserves.\nIf a swarm contains one restart-only unit, its saved internal state is not restored. A semantic unit needs its equivalence criterion checked. Combining these with exact units cannot produce an unqualified claim of exact whole-swarm recovery.\nProposed mode\tWhat carries across the interruption\tWhat the task must tolerate\t\nExact\tThe declared internal state\tChanged external resources may still prevent resumption\t\nSemantic\tA possibly different state with the declared observable behaviour\tDifferences outside the declared observations\t\nRestart only\tNo retained internal state\tRepeating preparation and any lost work\t\nSemantic recovery needs declared observations\nSuppose two restored components behave alike on three selected probes but differently on a fourth. Whether they are equivalent depends on whether that fourth probe belongs to the declared family. “Same enough” requires a named family and tolerance, for the same reason that a summary preserves answers to particular questions rather than every possible future question. A changed calibration or capability profile can invalidate the promised comparison even when the checkpoint bytes themselves remain unchanged.\nFor a finite illustration, compare restored states 000 and 001. Reading the first bit gives zero in both; reading the second does too. A profile that declares only those reads can treat the states as equivalent. Declaring the third read separates them completely. For probabilistic probes, one possible comparison is\n\n\\sup_{p\\in\\mathcal P}\\operatorname{TV}\\bigl(L_p(x),L_p(y)\\bigr)\\leq\\varepsilon,\n\nwhere L_p(x) is the output law of probe p on state x. This illustrative comparison makes equivalence depend on the probe family \\mathcal P and a tolerance \\varepsilon. A task using a probe outside \\mathcal P would ask for a guarantee the comparison never made.\nRecovery of the collective task\nQ-HENA proposes adapting HENA's swarm responsibilities on top of quantum units. A recovery experiment would have to follow the restored units into the collective task, identifying the candidate, profiles, budgets, repetitions and coverage. That makes it possible to ask whether the differences each unit's restore mode allows change the result the swarm was meant to produce.\nIn the reservation example, the bytes can restore exactly while the work still cannot resume. In a mixed swarm, one unit may resume from its exact state, another from a state that agrees only on selected observations, and another from a fresh start. The collective result depends on which of those changes the task can tolerate, not on the number of files that loaded successfully.\nA useful recovery report tells us what was restored, what was restarted, what changed and what was refused. That makes partial recovery a visible state rather than a silent weakening of the promise. It also keeps rollback honest: returning to older bytes is not permission to return to older authority.\n","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/recovery-across-a-changing-world/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/","identifier":"collection:causality-composition-and-learning","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Composition and learning","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/what-should-a-surprise-change/#article","identifier":"content:muon-surprise-is-a-question-not-a-verdict","@type":"Article","name":"What should a surprise change?","headline":"What should a surprise change?","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["surprise-driven learning","neural memory","learning rate limits","signal calibration","structural adaptation"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/what-should-a-surprise-change/","@type":"WebPage","name":"What should a surprise change?","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/what-should-a-surprise-change/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Composition and learning","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"What should a surprise change?","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/what-should-a-surprise-change/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/compression-is-a-promise/","https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/three-tests-for-a-reasoning-unit/"]},"description":"A traffic prediction fails. Should the unit remember the event, adjust a parameter or propose another account of the road?","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-10-26","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"What should a surprise change?\nA predictor expects a quiet road and observes heavy traffic. The mismatch deserves attention, but it does not tell us whether a sensor is broken, the model is poor, the distribution changed or an omitted event blocked the road. Surprise identifies a failure of expectation, not the mechanism behind it. That distinction matters when a system uses surprise to change itself: a signal that deserves a memory write does not automatically deserve a parameter update, and neither automatically warrants a new variable or a structural edit.\nKeeping an event is not learning a rule\nThree operations can follow the same residual: retaining an event for later inspection, adjusting a learned parameter using evidence across episodes, or proposing a change to the model's structure. Keeping the event preserves a particular observation; learning attempts to improve predictions beyond that observation; a structural proposal changes what the model can represent. Their risks and time scales differ because they alter different parts of the unit.\nThe Titans paper gives a technical example of learning a neural memory at test time using a memory-loss gradient as a surprise signal. Its mechanism connects surprise to a particular memory objective. A useful adaptation would need to specify what the unit can change, which evidence triggers that change and how the change affects subsequent tasks.\nMUON proposes studying surprise-driven learning inside a reasoning unit. The candidate controller would need to use the unit's actual state, measurements and update operations. A retention rule and a parameter-learning rule can then be compared on their own terms, rather than treating every response to the same residual as one learning mechanism.\nFor a local construction, let h_t be temporal state and M_\\phi a candidate adaptive memory. It predicts a target v_t from a key k_t, using a memory loss\n\nL_t=\\tfrac12\\lVert M_\\phi(k_t)-v_t\\rVert^2.\n\nThe gradient \\nabla_\\phi L_t has a specific object to update. A candidate step \\phi_{t+1}=\\phi_t-\\eta_t\\nabla_\\phi L_t attempts to improve that key-to-target association. It is not a gradient of a global causal graph, and its norm measures sensitivity of this loss, not the event's importance to every later task. This illustrates a mechanism to compare, rather than an implemented MUON memory.\nA prediction head would have to consume h_t, a memory read, or both for the stored distinction to reach the output. If a closure cue changes only which of the unit's routed computations, its experts, is selected, the unit can still forget which road is closed. A matched routing-only control keeps that choice while removing the direct state contribution; a frozen-memory control keeps the architecture while removing adaptation. Their errors reveal different parts of what the candidate memory does.\nScale can masquerade as importance\nSuppose one proposed signal has two components: gradient magnitude and distributional divergence. Adding their raw values is arbitrary. Multiplying the loss by a constant can enlarge the gradient term without supplying stronger evidence that anything important changed.\nA bounded controller can instead calibrate each component against a fixed reference, transform it into a finite dimensionless value between zero and one, and combine those values using non-negative weights summing to one. If the transformed values are 0.8 and 0.2, equal weights give 0.5; weights 0.25 and 0.75 give 0.35. The weights express a policy, not a discovered truth about the event.\nCalibration also needs an answer for inputs outside the reference distribution. Clipping limits numerical damage; it does not make an unfamiliar input understood. Rejecting non-finite values and bounding an update prevents one class of instability without proving the chosen update is useful.\nThree responses to one signal\nSuppose the calibrated aggregate is S=0.5. A candidate learning-rate rule is\n\n\\eta(S)=\\operatorname{clamp}\\!\\left(\\eta_{\\mathrm{base}}[1+\\alpha\\tanh(\\beta S)],\\eta_{\\min},\\eta_{\\max}\\right).\n\nWith \\eta_{\\mathrm{base}}=0.01, \\alpha=1, \\beta=2 and allowed rates from 0.005 to 0.02, it gives approximately 0.0176. This is a larger step within a specified range. The parameter update still needs a loss, a gradient and a choice of parameters allowed to adapt. Saturation limits the rate; a sufficiently large or misdirected gradient can still make the update harmful.\nA memory response answers another question. A clipped logit followed by a sigmoid can assign a bounded priority to the event using surprise, estimated usefulness and age. If the score passes a retention threshold, the owner of the record store may keep it. A road closure may be worth remembering for the next route even if one episode is insufficient evidence for changing a general traffic model. Forgetting the event and declining to learn a new rule are therefore different decisions.\nA structural response is slower again. Persistent error might suggest a missing event variable, a different expert connection or a richer representation. Crossing a threshold nominates such a hypothesis for comparison; it does not make the edit true. These three time scales create a useful design: attention now, adaptation across observations, and structural search when the existing account keeps failing. They also give an experiment something to separate. Retention alone, bounded parameter adaptation and separately evaluated structural proposals can be tested against a fixed unit on the same changing stream.\nResponse\tWhat changes\tWhat later observations can test\t\nRetain the closure report\tWhich event remains available\tWhether another route decision uses it\t\nUpdate traffic-model parameters\tPredictions within the existing representation\tWhether held-out traffic predictions improve\t\nPropose a closure variable\tWhat the model can express\tWhether the richer account explains cases the old one cannot\t\nThe same road event can justify examining all three without making them interchangeable updates.\nDifferent explanations predict different failures\nA sensor fault should leave evidence in the consistency of the measurements, while a model fitted on newer data may respond differently to a changing traffic distribution. A residual that persists across matched conditions poses a different problem from one tied to a particular setting. An omitted event variable becomes a useful candidate when adding it improves the account on held-out cases, rather than merely explaining the surprising observation after the fact. These comparisons gather different evidence; the initial residual establishes none of their conclusions.\nA structure-changing system therefore needs a proposal boundary. Crossing a surprise threshold may nominate a candidate edit. An independent comparison decides whether the edit improves the task without violating protected behaviour. The signal cannot both propose the explanation and certify it.\nThe sharper question is whether the failure arose inside an adequate representation or because the representation cannot answer the required question. Noise, model error, drift and lost information demand different repairs. Surprise keeps the inquiry open long enough to discover which one we have.\n","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/what-should-a-surprise-change/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/","identifier":"collection:causality-composition-and-learning","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Composition and learning","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/","identifier":"collection:causality-evidence-and-evaluation","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"What does the measurement establish?","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"description":"A record gets its chance to be useful through the policy that selects it. How can its contribution be estimated?","text":"This collection asks what a measurement establishes about a change, and why a candidate must not choose the test that admits it.\nThe decision to keep a record changes what an agent can later retrieve. Retrieval changes what it does, and what it does changes the outcomes available for scoring that record. Usefulness is entangled with the policy that gives a record its chance to be useful.\nA faster request after a cache rollout raises a similar difficulty. The cache may have helped, or the traffic may have changed. A comparison that separates those explanations is more informative than the before-and-after number alone. In memory, the comparison becomes harder still: an early cue may matter only after many intervening decisions, and the record with the newest reward may not deserve the credit.\nEstimating a contribution and deciding how much confidence to place in that estimate are different problems. Repeated runs can be stable while sharing one mistaken premise. Independent-looking reports can all descend from the same observation. Coverage can omit the very regime in which a rule fails. These distinctions affect the result itself, not only the presentation of an evaluation.\nSmall exact examples make some of the difficulty visible. An influence can cancel along a path, and a moving support can change a detection rate. Their assumptions differ, but both make a tempting shortcut answerable to a constructed case. The aim is a useful estimate whose interpretation survives closer examination, so that the next decision is informed by what was actually learned.\n","url":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"hasPart":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/","identifier":"journey:causality-from-observation-to-evaluation","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From observation to evaluation","url":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/","identifier":"journey:causality-from-observation-to-evaluation","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From observation to evaluation","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"What does the measurement establish?","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"From observation to evaluation","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/"}}]}],"description":"Follow an engineering intervention into the question of how a memory score earns trust.","text":"A score cannot repair the attribution it was fed. Before estimating a memory record's usefulness, we need a comparison that gives its contribution a meaning. Before trusting the resulting estimate, we need to understand how it behaves and what the evaluation left unexamined.\nThat dependency supplies the order here: an intervention and its alternatives, an update from attributed experience, then scrutiny of the resulting comparison and the completed experiments that give it an outcome and a price. A smooth or reproducible score can still track the wrong contribution. Separating those stages lets a failure reach the stage capable of explaining it, instead of demanding a more confident number from the last one.\n","url":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/","identifier":"collection:causality-evidence-and-evaluation","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"What does the measurement establish?","url":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/"}],"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":4,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/causal-inference-primer/#article","identifier":"content:causality-causal-inference-primer","name":"Did the cache make it faster?","url":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/causal-inference-primer/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/ema-vs-bayesian-updates/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-ema-vs-bayesian-updates","name":"EMA and Bayesian updates for online scoring","url":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/ema-vs-bayesian-updates/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/four-questions-for-an-evaluation/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-four-questions-for-an-evaluation","name":"Four questions behind a benchmark result","url":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/four-questions-for-an-evaluation/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":4,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/what-did-selective-memory-buy/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-memory-comparison","name":"What did selective memory buy?","url":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/what-did-selective-memory-buy/"}}]}},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/causal-inference-primer/#article","identifier":"content:causality-causal-inference-primer","@type":"Article","name":"Did the cache make it faster?","headline":"Did the cache make it faster?","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["cache rollout","causal attribution","random assignment","shared-cache interference","negative controls"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/causal-inference-primer/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Did the cache make it faster?","url":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/causal-inference-primer/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"What does the measurement establish?","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"From observation to evaluation","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Did the cache make it faster?","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/causal-inference-primer/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/ema-vs-bayesian-updates/"]},"description":"Latency falls after a cache rollout. Which comparison could separate the cache's contribution from the changing traffic?","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-03-02","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Did the cache make it faster?\nSuppose a cache rollout coincides with lower request latency. The graph establishes a change in the measured requests. It does not yet establish how much the cache caused, because traffic, request mix, upstream behaviour and other releases may also have changed. Crediting the wrong mechanism has a practical consequence: the next rollout may repeat the change and fail to repeat the result.\nThe comparison behind the metric\nFor an eligible population over a stated period, the question is what would change if requests used the cache rather than the previous path. The assignment unit and outcome give that comparison its meaning. Average latency, tail latency, error rate and resource cost answer different questions; an improvement in one can hide a loss in another.\nFor a request i, write Y_i(1) for latency with the cache and Y_i(0) for latency without it. The average treatment effect is\n\n\\tau=\\mathbb E\\bigl[Y_i(1)-Y_i(0)\\bigr].\n\nA negative value means lower average latency. We cannot serve the same request both ways in the same circumstances and observe both outcomes. The experiment therefore needs a defensible way to use different requests to estimate that missing comparison.\nA before-and-after comparison leaves time and treatment tangled. Random assignment can make the comparison stronger, but only if assignment is followed, measurement is comparable and interference is understood. A shared cache can let one group's traffic change the other group's experience. Randomisation does not erase that mechanism.\nMechanism\tWhat changes in the experiment\t\nRequest mix changes\tCompare eligible requests with comparable difficulty, not whichever traffic happens to arrive\t\nCache warm-up crosses groups\tAssignment to independent cache pools may be needed instead of request-level assignment\t\nA release changes both paths\tHold that release common across groups or model its separate effect\t\nThe cache changes resource contention\tInclude the shared load in the treatment definition rather than treating it as unrelated noise\t\nWith interference, latency is better written Y_i(z_i,s_i): it depends on the request's assignment z_i and the cache state s_i created by other traffic. A pool-level comparison then asks about the effect of a cache policy, not an isolated request switch.\nAn experiment with paired cache pools\nOne possible design uses paired, isolated cache pools and a common release. Within each time block, randomly assign one pool to the new cache policy and the other to the old path. Keep eligibility and traffic allocation fixed, and declare whether warm-up belongs inside the measurement window. Pool assignment prevents the same cache entries being warmed by both arms. Shared upstream capacity can still connect them; isolation has to concern the actual interference, not only two names in a configuration.\nThe causal picture explains why. Time and request difficulty can both affect latency; a rollout scheduled at a particular time can become associated with either. Assignment changes the cache policy, which changes cache state and upstream reads, which affect latency. Shared upstream load can also carry a path from one pool's policy into the other pool's result. Random assignment addresses the first association, while independent pools address one interference path. Neither automatically removes the shared-load path.\nSuppose four paired blocks produce the following invented mean latencies. Each block has two newly prepared pools and the same stipulated eligible workload; these are arithmetic examples, not measurements of a deployment.\nPaired block\tNew-policy pool, ms\tOld-path pool, ms\tPaired difference, ms\t\n1\t90\t100\t-10\t\n2\t104\t110\t-6\t\n3\t85\t95\t-10\t\n4\t114\t120\t-6\t\nThe average paired difference is -8 ms. Averaging all requests without retaining their blocks would discard part of the assignment design. Treating every request as an independent randomised unit would also overstate how many independent treatment allocations occurred: the policy was assigned to pools. Reusing a warm pool in another block would introduce carryover that this stipulated construction excludes.\nA report of this experiment would keep the block-level differences and allocations, request counts and difficulty mix, warm-up interval, upstream-read counts, errors, tail latency and resource cost together. Four blocks are a small comparison, not grounds for a precise production guarantee. If average latency improves while timeouts rise, the experiment has found a trade-off rather than an unqualified victory. If an uncached negative-control workload improves similarly, shared load becomes a concrete competing explanation to examine.\nA useful explanation includes its strongest alternative\nThe proposed mechanism might be fewer upstream reads. A competing explanation might be lighter traffic. Their predictions differ: the cache hypothesis should relate improvement to the requests it can actually serve, while a general load reduction may affect uncached requests too. A negative control can expose an explanation that fits every movement too conveniently.\nRepeated trials help distinguish a stable result from noise. They do not repair a biased assignment, a leaking comparison or a metric changed halfway through. Uncertainty belongs beside the effect and its assumptions, not in place of them.\nRetaining a useful record is another question\nReckon studies which records an agent should keep under a budget. Removing a record and observing a performance drop is an informative contrast, but it still needs a design and assumptions before receiving a causal interpretation. A retention score can be useful without being causal proof.\nDelayed contribution makes the memory problem harder. A record can matter well after its arrival, and other records may have intervened before the successful decision. An updated estimate has to absorb some account of that credit. The update can faithfully process the observations while their attribution still needs the same care as the cache rollout.\n","url":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/causal-inference-primer/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/","identifier":"journey:causality-from-observation-to-evaluation","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From observation to evaluation","url":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/ema-vs-bayesian-updates/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-ema-vs-bayesian-updates","@type":"TechArticle","name":"EMA and Bayesian updates for online scoring","headline":"EMA and Bayesian updates for online scoring","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["online utility scoring","exponential moving average","delayed credit assignment","Beta-Bernoulli posterior","memory retention budgets"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/ema-vs-bayesian-updates/","@type":"WebPage","name":"EMA and Bayesian updates for online scoring","url":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/ema-vs-bayesian-updates/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"What does the measurement establish?","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"From observation to evaluation","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"EMA and Bayesian updates for online scoring","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/ema-vs-bayesian-updates/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/causal-inference-primer/"]},"description":"Follow an implemented EMA update into delayed-cue retention, then compare what a candidate Bayesian estimate would add.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-11-17","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"EMA and Bayesian updates for online scoring\nA cue arrives before it is useful. Many observations later, it helps an agent choose the right door. The memory budget was too small to keep everything in between, so a retention policy had to make its choice before that outcome arrived. How should experience change the score attached to the cue, and how quickly should an old score lose its hold?\nReckon's laboratory uses an exponential moving average in particular utility-update paths. One scalar absorbs each credited observation with little work or storage. Following that update into the delayed-cue problem lets us examine both its attraction and the decisions hidden inside the credit it receives.\nAn EMA decides how quickly the past fades\nThe implemented form is\n\nu_{t+1}=(1-\\eta)u_t+\\eta c_t o_t,\n\nwhere u is the stored utility estimate, \\eta the learning rate, c the assigned credit and o the outcome. The implementation clamps the resulting utility to configured lower and upper bounds; the first arithmetic illustration below remains inside zero and one, so clipping does not change its arithmetic. For a fixed scalar, this update uses constant work and storage. The laboratory’s default is \\eta=0.1, with utility clamped to [0,1]. For the first arithmetic illustration, deliberately choose u_0=0.4 and the faster rate \\eta=0.25:\nCredited observation co\tOld score\tNew score\t\n1\t0.4\t0.75(0.4)+0.25=0.55\t\n0\t0.55\t0.75(0.55)=0.4125\t\nThe arithmetic makes the estimate's response concrete, while leaving the origin of its credit unresolved: it does not show that the record caused the outcome.\nCredit matters as much as decay. Giving a record credit in proportion to its existing score feeds the estimate back into itself. A smooth transformation may improve numerical behaviour without removing that circularity. The useful test is a matched comparison against alternatives, not the elegance of the recurrence.\nDelayed usefulness makes credit difficult\nConsider a constructed key-and-door task. An early observation says which of two doors will open later. Several irrelevant observations arrive between the cue and the door. The agent can retain only one record. Keeping the cue can improve the eventual choice even though nothing immediately rewards the write; retaining the latest vivid observation can destroy the only useful distinction. First-in-first-out and recency are informative controls precisely because delayed usefulness can disagree with both.\nCredit assigned after success is still ambiguous. Suppose the agent retrieved both a key cue and a general navigation hint. Equal credit gives each half the outcome. Weighting credit in proportion to existing utility gives the already favoured record more evidence for being favoured. If utilities are 0.9 and 0.1, the successful episode reinforces a nine-to-one allocation before the episode has shown which record mattered. The current laboratory’s SHAPED rule instead normalises \\log(1+e^{u_i/s}) across retrieved records, with default scale s=1 and utility 0.5 when a record’s utility is missing. For utilities 0.9 and 0.1, this gives shares of about 0.625 and 0.375, rather than nine to one. It softens the preference without establishing that either record caused the success. The credit and memory-manager guide explains the implemented rules and update path. Matched omission or substitution runs can supply a more informative contrast, provided they keep the task and alternative information access comparable.\nThe completed Tutor, Continual, Conversation and TextWorld comparisons put the current policies beside their controls and full probe price. They test retention outcomes; the softplus credit transformation still needs its own matched comparison against equal credit.\nThe task also separates storing, retrieving and using. A cue kept in memory but never reached cannot help the answer. A cue retrieved and quoted may still be ignored by the policy. A successful answer might come from guessing. Measuring those events separately lets the outcome challenge the score rather than merely reward activity around the record.\nA calculator result keeps receiving credit\nAn actual simulated conversation makes the distinction visible. In a recorded Reckon episode, the agent calculates 81 \\times 35 and stores the result, 2835. The conversation moves on to a length requirement, a tone requirement and searches for information. The calculator result remains among the retrieved records, so it continues to receive a share of each turn’s reward.\nThis episode used first-in-first-out retention with 100 slots and finished with thirteen records, without pruning. It lets us examine scoring under ample memory; it does not establish that a selective policy kept the right things. The saved notebook follows one record through all seven updates and checks the episode’s full set of seventeen score updates against the recorded reads and rewards.\nStep\tRecords retrieved\tRecorded reward\tEqual credit\tOld utility\tNew utility\t\n1\t1\t0\t1\t1.500000\t1.000000\t\n2\t1\t0\t1\t1.000000\t0.900000\t\n3\t1\t0.5\t1\t0.900000\t0.860000\t\n4\t2\t0.5\t1/2\t0.860000\t0.799000\t\n5\t3\t0.5\t1/3\t0.799000\t0.735767\t\n6\t4\t0.5\t1/4\t0.735767\t0.674690\t\n7\t5\t3\t1/5\t0.674690\t0.667221\t\nThe retrieved IDs, rewards and old and new utilities are saved observations. Equal credit is calculated from the source’s rule of dividing one among the retrieved records; the table rounds utilities to six decimal places. The notebook keeps the original precision and recomputes each update with \\eta=0.1 and bounds [0,1].\nThe first row shows the clamp at work. This adapter writes tool results with an initial utility of 1.5. With a zero reward, the next value before clamping is 0.9(1.5)=1.35, which becomes 1 at the upper bound. The following rows carry that new value forward.\nThe final row is more surprising. The turn earns a reward of 3, but five retrieved records share it. This record’s target is therefore 3/5=0.6, below its previous utility of 0.67469. Its score falls to 0.667221 even after a positive reward. The simulator’s reward is a task score, not a probability of success.\nThe arithmetic is doing what the rule asks. What it has not established is whether the old calculation helped satisfy the later tone or length requirement. Retrieval membership gave it credit. A causal comparison would have to ask what changed when that record was unavailable, under a suitable alternative. The trace makes the scoring mechanism inspectable without resolving that harder question.\nA posterior adds a model, not automatic certainty\nA Bayesian alternative would specify what is being estimated, how observations arise and what prior assumptions apply, then update a posterior. A suitable model can carry uncertainty that a single moving average does not. A misspecified model can also produce confident answers about the wrong quantity.\nFor an elementary candidate, suppose p is the probability of success when a particular cue is available under a fixed task policy. A \\operatorname{Beta}(1,1) prior and conditionally independent Bernoulli observations give\n\np\\mid s,f\\sim\\operatorname{Beta}(s+1,f+1),\n\\qquad\\mathbb E[p\\mid s,f]=\\frac{s+1}{s+f+2}.\n\nThree successes and one failure give a posterior mean of 4/6, rather than the raw fraction 3/4.\nNote\nThis Bayesian comparison model is not an implemented scorer. The model’s uncertainty concerns that availability-conditioned success probability, not the cue's causal contribution. Policy selection and an easier set of trials can bias the observations without violating the posterior arithmetic.\nWe can follow the same four binary observations through simple estimates. Let the sequence be 1,1,0,1, take u_0=0.5, \\eta=0.25 and full credit c_t=1 for the EMA, and retain the uniform Beta prior. A running mean supplies a baseline that uses no decay or prior:\nObservation\tEMA\tRunning mean\tBeta posterior mean\t\n1\t0.625\t1\t2/3\t\n1\t0.71875\t1\t3/4\t\n0\t0.5390625\t2/3\t3/5\t\n1\t0.654296875\t3/4\t4/6\t\nThese methods are processing an availability-conditioned success trace here, not receiving causal credit. The comparison isolates their arithmetic before introducing a retention decision. Two records can have the same posterior mean and different numbers of observations, which changes posterior concentration; an EMA's current scalar does not supply that count. Conversely, a late zero immediately changes the EMA's weighting of all earlier observations. Neither behaviour establishes which estimate will keep the useful cue under a memory ceiling.\nThat simple model also treats the success probability as stationary. An EMA deliberately fades old observations; an undiscounted posterior accumulates them. When a hidden task mode changes, a score can remain confident in a cue that belonged to the old mode. A fair comparison must therefore declare whether adaptation comes from a fixed decay, a change model or another reset rule, and count the observations each needs to recognise the change.\nThe budget belongs inside the test\nThe value of a retention policy depends on what it keeps within the memory and compute budget, including the cost of probing a record's usefulness. First-in-first-out, least recently used, random retention and simple importance rules are meaningful baselines. A delayed cue tests whether a record survives until needed; an occasionally observed hidden mode tests detection lag; a bounded replay buffer tests forgetting across tasks. A small tutoring simulation can ask whether retaining a student's specific misconception helps a later hint more than retaining a generic summary. These are different uses of memory, not interchangeable points on one leaderboard.\nStorage cost changes the decision too. Ranking utility per byte can favour a small cue over a large history record, but a ratio-based greedy rule is only a heuristic when useful records interact or costs are indivisible. Two fragments might matter only together. And a probe that consumes another trial or model call spends part of the same budget whose allocation it is supposed to improve. A better estimate earns its place only when the decisions it supports repay that expense.\nA removal contrast depends on assignment, alternatives and measurement for its causal interpretation. If a run without the cue also changes the task or gives the agent another source of the answer, the contrast has changed more than the retained record. A responsive score cannot disentangle those changes on its own.\nReckon's completed retention comparisons now connect the implemented policies to measured outcomes and their probe price. Comparing shaped credit with equal credit, or a moving average with a separately specified posterior-based retention rule, remains another experiment. Those comparisons would ask whether a better account of a record's usefulness and uncertainty changes what the agent keeps enough to repay the work of estimating it.\nReckon, context-control episode recorded 15 November 2026, run 045229537dbf, source 2f74405, seed 42, simulated backend. The attached notebook retains the scoring fields from the first complete episode in context_controls.jsonl, with its source hash and the same-record joins. The notebook guide explains the distinction between context controls and policy comparisons.\n","url":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/ema-vs-bayesian-updates/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/","identifier":"journey:causality-from-observation-to-evaluation","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From observation to evaluation","url":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/four-questions-for-an-evaluation/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-four-questions-for-an-evaluation","@type":"Article","name":"Four questions behind a benchmark result","headline":"Four questions behind a benchmark result","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["benchmark repeatability","evaluator independence","causal grounding","test coverage","multiple comparisons"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/four-questions-for-an-evaluation/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Four questions behind a benchmark result","url":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/four-questions-for-an-evaluation/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"What does the measurement establish?","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"From observation to evaluation","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Four questions behind a benchmark result","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/four-questions-for-an-evaluation/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/causal-inference-primer/","https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/ema-vs-bayesian-updates/"]},"description":"A retention policy beats learned utility and loses to no memory: four questions examine what the completed benchmark supports.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-11-17","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Four questions behind a benchmark result\nMeasured retention improved a classifier's accuracy by 2.448 percentage points over learned utility. It also lost 1.580 points against no memory. Measured retention is the policy that ranks a memory record partly by what happens when that record is removed; learned utility ranks it by a score updated from credited outcomes. Both comparisons came from the same completed Reckon experiment, and both declared intervals excluded zero. Calling the candidate “better” without naming its comparator would hide the more useful result: measurement improved one policy while a simpler control still did better.\nReckon separates four coordinates in its intended evaluation contract: repeatability, independence, grounding and coverage. They are not ingredients to average into a prettier score. Each asks a question the others cannot answer.\nCan the result be repeated?\nA single favourable run cannot show how an improvement varies. Reckon's Tutor confirmation fixed 256 fresh seeds before inspecting their outcomes, with each seed contributing a mean over five carried-memory episodes. Measured retention beat utility at two slots in fifty seeds, tied in 180 and lost in 26. Its average gain was 0.339 percentage points, with a declared 97.5% interval of 0.037–0.640 points. The average and paired trace carry different parts of the result.\nRepetitions and their variability become informative alongside the changing seed, runtime, model and configuration. Replaying the same seed checks reproduction; fresh seeds test variation within the declared generator. Even deterministic reproduction answers only part of the question: a leaking test can reproduce its mistake perfectly. Repeatability characterises the measurement procedure without establishing the validity of its interpretation. The complete memory comparison gives the protocols, other controls, uncertainty and measurement cost.\nWho made the answer and who judged it?\nIf the same model generated a candidate, produced its preferred rubric and judged its output, agreement may reflect shared assumptions. Separate code, data, models or human review can reduce particular dependencies, but the actual overlap determines which independence matters. A second evaluator sharing the training examples may not supply an independent test of generalisation; a separate model with the same source assumptions may repeat the same blind spot. Opening another chat changes the session without necessarily changing either dependency. Independence has to concern the particular failure the comparison is meant to expose.\nThe distinction also applies when an agent improves its own procedure. Feedback can help propose an edit, validation can select it, and a held-out comparison can examine the selected result. Reusing the last comparison as search feedback changes its role. Pale's procedure-and-model example develops that separation around SkillOpt and a four-arm comparison; the procedure that wins validation still has to face cases it did not help select.\nWhat supports the interpretation?\nRemoving a memory record changes an outcome. That is an intervention contrast within the tested system. Calling it causal requires the treatment and control, experimental unit, estimand, identification assumptions and contamination checks to be specified. Ablation alone does not provide all of them.\nA measured contrast and a proxy such as gradient magnitude contribute different information. The proxy can help choose what to examine; normalising it into the same numeric range does not make it evidence of the mechanism.\nWhat was not tested?\nA candidate may improve the declared workload while omitting refusal cases, long inputs, resource exhaustion or recovery after interruption. An omitted case supplies no result. Tests cut short by a budget limit are particularly revealing: the comparison's coverage has become entangled with the resources needed to complete it.\nMany comparisons also change the statistical problem. At a nominal five-percent false-positive rate, one hundred independent tests under the null would produce five false positives in expectation. Declaring a multiple-testing policy is part of the experiment; choosing only the favourable pairs afterwards changes the meaning of the reported evidence.\nA worked report for the memory comparison\nThe Tutor result makes the four coordinates concrete. It presents six questions about six initially misunderstood concepts, with two- and four-slot memory conditions. Each seed's five episodes carry memory forward; the independent analysis unit is the seed mean, not each of the 1,280 main episodes in a policy row and certainly not every probe.\nRepeatability names the fixed generator, retention formula, source and runtime, all 256 seeds, and the complete paired differences. At four slots, measured retention gains 0.755 percentage points over utility, with a 97.5% interval of 0.421–1.089. Both slot comparisons use the declared two-test correction. Their intervals are positive but lie below the five-point effect used for sample planning. That planning assumption is not an acceptance threshold for a useful system.\nIndependence distinguishes the confirmation's fresh seeds from the exploratory cases used to develop the comparison. Starting students and response randomness are paired independently of action choices, so a changed hint decision does not silently give one policy a different random stream. The outcome is scored by the simulation's cleared misconceptions, rather than by a language model judging its own teaching. These choices address particular dependencies; they do not make a small simulation representative of every learner.\nGrounding keeps the question narrow: does this implemented retention mixture improve the declared Tutor outcome over utility? Its record-removal probes copy memory into fresh FIFO-reference episodes. They do not continue the live student's state, and the policy-level gain does not establish the causal contribution of every record that received credit. The ranking uses utility and positive measured effects together, so the experiment also does not isolate which ingredient produced the gain.\nCoverage includes all nine policies and both limited budgets, including null and adverse outcomes. It does not include arbitrary students, larger curricula, different continuation rules or a common total-compute allowance. The measured arms consume 5,790,720 extra probe episodes. At two slots, measured retention's inclusive time is about 2,087 seconds against utility's 0.772; at four, about 4,254 against 0.846. These are the recorded machine times, with probe time already inside the totals. An accuracy comparison that omits that price has left out part of what the candidate did.\nEach coordinate points to a different next question. Fresh cases examine variation; a different reference intervention examines attribution; a changed curriculum examines transfer; a common compute allowance examines practical value. Combining the four into one quality score would make those distinctions harder to see.\nWhat the difference supports\nThe laboratory's memory update is itself a subject for this kind of comparison. Its score may be cheap and responsive; whether that response helps retention is another question. Repeatability, independence, grounding and coverage describe different ways the answer can remain uncertain.\nA small positive difference can survive this examination while its adoption case remains weak. A negative comparison can reveal a simpler way to do the task. An evaluation earns its value by keeping those answers visible, with the question and cost that give each one meaning.\n","url":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/four-questions-for-an-evaluation/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/","identifier":"journey:causality-from-observation-to-evaluation","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From observation to evaluation","url":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/what-did-selective-memory-buy/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-memory-comparison","@type":"TechArticle","name":"What did selective memory buy?","headline":"What did selective memory buy?","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["memory retention experiments","probe cost","paired comparisons","continual learning","literal recall","TextWorld"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/what-did-selective-memory-buy/","@type":"WebPage","name":"What did selective memory buy?","url":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/what-did-selective-memory-buy/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"What does the measurement establish?","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"From observation to evaluation","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"What did selective memory buy?","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/what-did-selective-memory-buy/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://causality.in/reckon/compare-replay-budgets/","https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/ema-vs-bayesian-updates/","https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/four-questions-for-an-evaluation/"]},"description":"Reckon's completed memory experiments find small gains, a loss against no memory, and a large price for measuring which records to retain.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"datePublished":{"@value":"2026-11-18","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"What did selective memory buy?\nA memory policy can spend more time discovering which record to keep than the task spends using it. That is what makes the first completed Reckon comparisons interesting. Measuring record removal sometimes improved the outcome over learned utility. In one classification workload it still lost to using no memory. In a text-game diagnostic it matched the simpler first-in-first-out policy's wins while paying for thousands of extra replays.\nThe research question is whether a limited memory can retain what measurably changes an answer, at a price worth paying. A positive difference answers only part of that question. The outcome, the comparator, the measurement process and its cost have to travel together.\nWhat the policies were allowed to remember\nThese experiments compare nine policies. No memory discards each new admission before it can be retrieved; full memory has enough capacity to avoid pruning in the declared workload. The limited policies choose which record to discard by age (FIFO), last use (LRU), seeded randomness, an explicitly supplied importance score, learned utility, Diversity (which favours coverage across record types), or measured retention. Full capacity is a reference, not a claim that storing more always improves the answer.\nLearned utility updates a record's score from credited outcomes. Measured retention mixes that utility with a bonus for a positive record-removal estimate, per byte. The experiments retain negative and zero estimates even though the ranking gives only positive estimates that bonus. A comparison between these policies therefore tests the implemented mixture; it does not isolate measurement from utility or establish that every useful record received the right score.\nTutor and Continual copy the current memory into fresh task episodes, then compare keeping it with removing a named record under a FIFO reference policy. Their probes do not continue the live student's or classifier's hidden state. Conversation and TextWorld instead reconstruct the actual episode prefix and earlier removals, compare FIFO at the pending write with removing a candidate first, then continue both under FIFO. The FIFO baseline may itself remove that candidate. These are different interventions, not interchangeable estimates of a record's universal value.\nSmall tutoring gains had a large price\nThe Tutor workload starts with six misunderstood concepts and asks six questions in a session. Each of 256 fresh outer seeds supplies five episodes with carried memory. The outcome is the fraction of those misconceptions cleared, averaged within the seed. At two slots, measured retention clears 33.320% on average, versus utility's 32.982%; at four, the figures are 36.823% and 36.068%.\nThe paired gains are 0.339 and 0.755 percentage points. Their declared 97.5% intervals are 0.037–0.640 and 0.421–1.089 points. Both exclude zero under the two-comparison correction, but lie wholly below the five-point effect used to plan the sample. That planning value was an analyst's detectability target, not an accepted threshold for adoption.\nAt two slots, fifty seeds improve, 180 tie and 26 worsen. At four, 74 improve, 157 tie and 25 worsen. The average preserves a small positive effect while the paired outcomes show how unevenly it occurs. Acquiring those measurements used 5,790,720 additional probe episodes across the two measured arms. A small gain can be statistically distinguishable and still leave its practical value unanswered. Four questions behind a benchmark result reads this Tutor confirmation for what can be repeated, who judged it, what supports its interpretation and what it left untested.\nClassification improved against utility and lost against no memory\nThe Continual workload trains a small classifier across three tasks, with replay opportunities for retained examples. The confirmation uses 64 fresh seeds, each averaged over three carried episodes. At two slots, measured retention reaches 85.556% accuracy, utility 83.108%, and no memory 87.135%.\nMeasured retention gains 2.448 percentage points over utility, with a corrected interval of 1.739–3.156. Against no memory it loses 1.580 points, with interval −2.193 to −0.967. Both declared contrasts exclude zero, in opposite directions. Reporting only the first would turn an improvement over one weak comparator into a misleading account of the whole experiment.\nAll one-slot limited policies produce identical seed outcomes because a new admission leaves only one retained candidate to remove. The measured policy nevertheless pays for probing that sole candidate. At two slots it has a real choice, and its probes reach carried records, yet it takes about 1,408 times utility's inclusive elapsed time on this machine. The result points towards the representation, replay rule and comparison workload as objects to investigate, rather than making selective memory valuable by definition.\nLiteral recall shows a gain, but the other controls still matter\nConversation uses an eight-step scripted controller whose final answer is scored for literal constraint recall. Across 256 fresh outer seeds, measured retention improves over utility by 6.185 points at two slots and 4.883 at four. The corresponding corrected intervals are 4.045–8.325 and 3.016–6.749 points.\nAt two slots, however, LRU recalls 24.349% on average, compared with measured retention's 16.927%. At four, measured retention reaches 77.604%, utility 72.721%, and LRU 71.745%. Full memory recalls everything; no memory recalls nothing. The declared tests establish the two comparisons against utility. The other means describe the tested alternatives without supplying additional significance tests or a universal ranking.\nThe measured arms add 26,972 replay episodes and take about 74 and 117 times utility's inclusive time. The controller is scripted, and the endpoint is literal recall. A gain here does not measure the reasoning quality of an arbitrary language model.\nA text-game diagnostic exposes another limit\nThe later TextWorld diagnostic uses sixteen fresh generated games and a repaired controller that follows visible instructions and retrieved action history. Full memory wins fifteen of sixteen. Measured retention wins one at two slots and three at four, matching FIFO's complete score vectors. Random retention wins four games at four slots; utility wins zero and two.\nThese are descriptive outcomes, with no confirmation test or confidence interval. The measured policy improves over utility by one seed at each budget, adds 10,672 replays, and takes about 266 and 469 times utility's recorded inclusive time. Early timings overlapped other local work, so those ratios are observations of that run rather than an isolated hardware benchmark.\nCapacity and language both matter. The normal third instruction needs the goal and two earlier action records; two slots cannot hold all three. Four slots can, but repeated descriptions and inventory compete for that allowance. Two generated quests need only two actions despite the three-command generation setting. In this diagnostic, even full memory failed one case because the controller misread an insertion instruction. Another full-memory win took seventeen commands after an earlier instruction-grounding failure. The parser now separates the requested action from clauses describing completed prerequisites. Regression checks cover those two parsing repairs.\nOf 5,336 candidate-removal pairs, five have positive signed effects, three negative and 5,328 zero. The negative cases retain an especially useful distinction: removing a candidate lets the FIFO continuation win, but the main ranking removes a different record. The recorded effects expose something the default scoring rule does not exploit. A zero final-score effect also need not mean that intermediate actions were unchanged or that the record is irrelevant under every continuation.\nA subsequent four-game development used the repaired parser to compare that default rule with an optional rule that selects a removal when its measured continuation improves on FIFO. At four slots the optional rule won one game; default measured retention, FIFO and utility won none. Seeded random won the same game. Every limited policy scored zero at two slots, while full memory won all four games in three actions each. The two measured rules together added 5,408 replays. The optional rule's one improving selection kept the goal for the next action, then lost it before the third; its eventual win came through a six-action fallback. This development compares selection rules under the repaired controller. It does not isolate the benefit of repairing the parser, and four games with the same wins as random do not confirm a general retention gain.\nPut measurement on the same bill\nThe counts below belong to the measured arms. Each row's elapsed time already includes its probes or replays. Adding the two time columns would charge that work twice.\nWorkload\tSlots\tMain episodes\tExtra probe or replay episodes\tInclusive seconds\tProbe or replay seconds\t\nTutor\t2\t1,280\t2,096,128\t2,087.329\t2,086.370\t\nTutor\t4\t1,280\t3,694,592\t4,253.775\t4,252.780\t\nContinual\t1\t192\t113,792\t301.467\t301.022\t\nContinual\t2\t192\t246,528\t618.282\t617.819\t\nConversation\t2\t256\t10,356\t17.000\t16.673\t\nConversation\t4\t256\t16,616\t30.840\t30.464\t\nTextWorld\t2\t16\t3,912\t1,028.318\t1,024.377\t\nTextWorld\t4\t16\t6,760\t1,803.517\t1,799.584\t\nThose are episode-execution timings. Saving and checking the result files adds work outside them; TextWorld's game preparation is also separate. Store byte and slot counts describe retained record payloads, not process memory or every probe's working storage. The full policy outcomes, seed spreads and costs are in the notes; the saved notebook recomputes the reported gain and cost comparisons from the retained summaries.\nWhat a useful next comparison has to preserve\nThese findings give the laboratory a real answer to build on. Tutor and Conversation establish gains against their declared utility comparators. Continual establishes a gain against utility and a loss against no memory. TextWorld provides diagnostic cases where a simpler policy gets the same wins for far less work. Changing the workload, controller or retention rule can be worthwhile precisely because those differences now have identifiable causes to investigate.\nA revised method needs a separately declared comparison on fresh cases, with the old adverse and null outcomes still visible. The three-seed FIFO replay-budget example asks a separate question about capacity; it is not a confirmation of these policy results. Nor do memory-path controls whose policies all retain the current cue establish useful ranking among those policies.\nThe stronger question is whether a policy improves the answer under a common total resource allowance, including the work spent deciding what to remember. Until that comparison is made useful, an expensive measurement is evidence to understand, not a reason to deploy its winner.\nReckon, Tutor confirmation completed 15 November 2026, result eef872b88357, source 3d3aea6. The benchmark manual identifies the fixed protocol, all 256 seeds and retained results. The analysis unit is a seed's mean over five episodes; the inner probes use fresh FIFO-reference tasks.\nReckon, Continual confirmation completed 16 November 2026, result 7e2728490cc1, source adad858. The benchmark manual preserves both primary contrasts, 64 seed means over three episodes, and full measurement cost.\nReckon, Conversation confirmation completed 16 November 2026, result df3d0053c39f, protocol source e60a4a2. The notebook guide distinguishes the 256-seed confirmation from calibration and earlier read-boundary ablations. Each candidate has one deterministic replay pair; inferential intervals concern outer-seed policy differences.\nReckon, TextWorld retention diagnostic completed 16 November 2026, result 6676e6a9ecd8, source dc90275. The notebook guide preserves the sixteen cases, controller limits and complete costs. These games use live environments and a scripted controller, with no model call or hidden solution.\n","url":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/what-did-selective-memory-buy/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/","identifier":"journey:causality-from-observation-to-evaluation","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From observation to evaluation","url":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/","identifier":"content:causality-project-hena","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"description":"A research project on how small reasoning units learn to solve a task together, the collective that the Causality programme works towards.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"text":"HENA\nCan a warning read by one small unit reach another unit’s later decision? HENA asks whether a collective can learn which messages to send, what experience to keep and how to organise around a task.\nFollow a connector warning through the design. One unit reads that a replacement part has a different connector. Another gets the repair request much later. The experiment asks whether that warning survives the gap and changes the repair decision, through learned communication and memory rather than a route declared for every case.\nFrom arranging a system to learning its arrangement\nPale coordinates work through declared responsibilities and capabilities, while HENA asks how a division of work and a hierarchy could arise as units learn to solve tasks together, as Causality’s About page sets out.\nA whole answer from local views\nA long input can exceed what any one unit holds. In HENA’s design, each sees part of the input. Messages and memory carry a useful detail beyond the unit that first saw it. The design has to work within limits on compute, communication and memory.\nGive each unit a local view\nThe input is streamed or encoded in parts. One unit receives the maintenance note while another sees the later repair request. Each begins with its own state and a partial view of the task.\nConnect what the task needs\nOver several rounds, the units send messages, read from fixed-size memory and update their own state. The connector warning has to reach the right unit before it answers.\nForm the collective answer\nA subset of units or the shared memory supplies the answer. Training judges its quality and counts the messages and time it took to produce it. The question is whether useful routes and a hierarchy emerge under those conditions.\nQuestions about a learned organisation\nBroadcasting the connector warning to every unit might get the right answer while spending the communication HENA hopes to save. Keeping it in memory helps only if the repair unit retrieves it in time and uses it correctly. The experiment needs to follow the warning all the way into the decision.\nCompare the learned arrangement with a flat collective, a central coordinator, an imposed hierarchy and a single stronger unit under matched resources. A useful hierarchy should improve the complete task after communication and memory costs are counted. What would count as emergent hierarchy? develops that comparison, including the possibility that the organisation adds no benefit.\nQuestions about HENA\nWhat makes HENA different from orchestrating agents?\nOrchestration scripts who does what; HENA tests whether the units can learn that organisation themselves, deciding whom to message, sharing limited memory and forming a hierarchy that helps them solve a task. The first swarm coordinates existing open-weight models as its baseline.\nWould HENA replace Pale?\nHENA is Pale’s research successor in one limited sense: its learned organisation could take over some of the coordination Pale describes explicitly. Pale sits around the tools people already use; should HENA prove itself, Pale would stand to it as a harness stands to the model it runs, more guardrail than organiser, still governing permissions and resources and observing its work. In Harsh’s account HENA is an evolving swarm of MUONs that reasons about what could have happened, the third rung of the ACI framework in Beyond the Mirage; today’s products come first because they let agents see what a change would affect before they make it. HENA is a hypothesis under study, and how that would work remains open. HENA also stands on its own. Causality’s wider question explains that direction.\nCould it serve as a model behind a coding harness?\nA coding harness such as Claude Code or Codex could use a HENA-backed model as its model provider. The harness still decides which actions to allow and carries them out.\n","url":"https://causality.in/hena/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:hena","name":"HENA","description":"A research project on how small reasoning units learn to solve a task together, the collective that the Causality programme works towards.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/","identifier":"content:causality-project-hena","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA","url":"https://causality.in/hena/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/#article","identifier":"content:hena-docs","name":"How units form a collective","url":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/","identifier":"content:hena-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA mentions","url":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","name":"HENA: Hierarchically Evolving Neural Automata","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","name":"HENA"}]}],"mentions":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:muon","name":"MUON","description":"Research into how a small reasoning unit uses past observations, learns and works with other units.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-muon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MUON","url":"https://causality.in/muon/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/computation-state-and-learning/#article","identifier":"content:muon-docs","name":"Computation, state and learning","url":"https://causality.in/muon/computation-state-and-learning/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/mentions/","identifier":"content:muon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MUON mentions","url":"https://causality.in/muon/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/paper/MUON.tex","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/paper/MUON.tex","name":"MUON: Micro-Unit Operating Network","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/muon/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/muon/overview","name":"MUON"}]},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:pale","name":"Pale","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux","description":"See which review instructions apply in a repository and what would change before you install them. Pale compiles those instructions and permissions from one workspace.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#brand"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale","targetProduct":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/","identifier":"content:pale-project","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Pale","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/understand-your-world/#article","identifier":"content:pale-docs","name":"Introduction to Pale","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/understand-your-world/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/mentions/","identifier":"content:pale-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Pale mentions","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.rawthoughts.in/pale/overview"}]}],"hasPart":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/communication-and-memory/#article","identifier":"content:hena-communication-and-memory","name":"Why communication and memory are computation","url":"https://causality.in/hena/communication-and-memory/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/#article","identifier":"content:hena-docs","name":"How units form a collective","url":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/communication-and-memory/#article","identifier":"content:hena-communication-and-memory","@type":"TechArticle","name":"Why communication and memory are computation","headline":"Why communication and memory are computation","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["local views","message routing","collective memory","communication budgets"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/communication-and-memory/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Why communication and memory are computation","url":"https://causality.in/hena/communication-and-memory/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"HENA","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Why communication and memory are computation","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/communication-and-memory/"}}]}]},"description":"Follow how local views, sparse messages and limited collective memory could contribute to HENA’s answer.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"text":"Why communication and memory are computation\nOne unit reads about a changed connector. Another later gets a request to repair the machine. HENA would give each unit part of the input, messages from a few neighbours and reads from limited memory. The proposed swarm would have to get the warning to the unit that answers.\nA local view can miss a joint fact\nEach unit's information can be accurate and still be insufficient. The relation between the part, the machine and the repair must reach the unit that forms the answer.\nBroadcasting everything might solve the information gap, but spends the communication HENA seeks to save. Sending every question through a central unit creates a different bottleneck. The research asks whether learned routes can deliver the relevant contribution without every unit reconstructing the complete problem.\nMessages change who can contribute\nIn each round, the proposed incoming message combines contributions from a set of neighbouring units:\nm_i^t=\\sum_{j\\in N_i^t}a_{ij}^t\\,g(h_j^t).\nN_i^t determines which units can contribute in that round. The weights a_{ij}^t determine how their messages combine; g(h_j^t) produces a message from a neighbour's state. Changing those routes or weights changes what the next update can use, even with the same external input.\nMore rounds can carry the cue further, but add latency and communication. A denser route may preserve the connector relation or bury it among redundant messages. The useful route must help the task within compute, communication and memory limits together.\nMemory limits make time part of the choice\nThe early note must survive until the repair question arrives. In a fixed-size store, keeping it can displace something else. Retrieval must then put it where it can affect the answer. A saved note or a retrieval hit alone does not show that the answer used it correctly.\nA text store could support the first stock-model swarm. The longer-term trainable memory target would let task errors guide memory access itself. HENA would keep this store and its history; Reckon's proposed record interface would let an evaluator examine them. The developer manual in the HENA repository explains these store responsibilities.\nLearned hierarchy needs a useful collective effect\nRepeated complementary routes could produce specialisation and larger structures. Test whether those structures improve what the collective can answer, what it remembers and the complete cost of its work. Compare a single stronger unit, a flat collective, an imposed hierarchy and a central coordinator.\nThe result might show that a route helps, that communication costs consume the gain or that the apparent hierarchy adds nothing beyond stronger units. The emergence inquiry follows those comparisons through an example.\nHow units form a collective connects these choices to local unit learning, evolution and HENA's broader aim.\n","url":"https://causality.in/hena/communication-and-memory/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"copyrightHolder":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}],"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:hena","name":"HENA","description":"A research project on how small reasoning units learn to solve a task together, the collective that the Causality programme works towards.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/","identifier":"content:causality-project-hena","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA","url":"https://causality.in/hena/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/#article","identifier":"content:hena-docs","name":"How units form a collective","url":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/","identifier":"content:hena-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA mentions","url":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","name":"HENA: Hierarchically Evolving Neural Automata","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","name":"HENA"}]}],"mentions":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:reckon","name":"Reckon","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux","description":"Measure what memory changes for its cost, and compare proposed improvements before deciding to use them.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon","targetProduct":{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-reckon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Reckon","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/understand-the-memory-laboratory/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-docs","name":"Understand the memory laboratory","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/understand-the-memory-laboratory/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/mentions/","identifier":"content:reckon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Reckon mentions","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/reckon/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/reckon/overview","name":"Reckon"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/","identifier":"content:causality-project-hena","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA","url":"https://causality.in/hena/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/#article","identifier":"content:hena-docs","@type":"TechArticle","name":"How units form a collective","headline":"How units form a collective","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["collective computation","sparse communication","distributed memory","emergent hierarchy","swarm of MUONs"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/","@type":"WebPage","name":"How units form a collective","url":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"HENA","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"How units form a collective","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/"}}]}]},"description":"Follow a repair example to see how local reasoning, a few messages and limited memory could help units solve a task together.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"text":"How units form a collective\nImagine a maintenance note warning that a replacement part has a different connector. Much later, a repair request arrives. One small reasoning unit reads the warning; another gets the request. Can the first unit's experience change the second unit's answer?\nHENA is a proposed architecture for learning that coordination. Instead of one large model attending to the whole input, many small units would each see part of it, exchange a few messages and share limited memory.\nMemory and communication are the architecture\nThe connector warning has to survive the intervening input, stay associated with the right machine and reach the unit choosing the repair. Saving the note is only the beginning. A retrieved note might concern another machine; a correct message might arrive too late. The answer needs to account for the connector when deciding which work can proceed.\nThis gives us a path to investigate: where the cue enters, what keeps it available, how it reaches the later question and whether it changes the answer. Fixed-size memory means that keeping one item can displace another. Sparse communication means that every unit cannot receive every update. Learning what to keep and where to send it together could produce specialisation: some units perform particular computations, and recurring routes connect complementary work.\nHow a unit updates from its view, messages and memory\nEach unit would combine its local input with its current state, incoming messages and reads from memory. A candidate update is\nh_i^{t+1}=f_\\theta(h_i^t,\\tilde x_i^t,m_i^t,r_i^t).\nHere h_i^t is the unit's current state, \\tilde x_i^t its local input, m_i^t the messages it receives and r_i^t what it reads from memory. The learned update f_\\theta produces its next state. The repair unit could therefore respond to the connector warning in a message or memory read even if the repair request itself says nothing about it.\nRepeated rounds carry information beyond the first local view. A subset of units or the collective memory then supplies the answer. MUON studies the individual unit; HENA studies how those units work together.\nThe target is to handle long sequences at less than quadratic cost while keeping or improving accuracy, context retention, compositional reasoning and adaptability. Compute, communication and memory each have a limit. Linear or near-linear processing and memory that does not grow with the whole input are targets to test.\nMemory can learn from the task\nIf the answer misses the connector warning, training should be able to improve how that relation survives and reaches the later decision. The longer-term target is differentiable memory: reads and writes that can learn from task errors alongside routing and unit updates. Saving and retrieving text alone does not provide that training path.\nDifferentiable-memory research demonstrates the general idea. HENA has not chosen a mechanism. An error at the answer may depend on several earlier messages, updates and memory operations; working out how it should change them remains open. Communication and memory follows the cue through those choices in more detail.\nEvolving local rules\nThe programme also asks whether changing local rules can produce better collective behaviour. Here a candidate is a set of local rules, kept as a rule document, edited and tested in separate trials. Its proposed evolution mechanism groups these candidates into niches according to a behaviour characteristic. Small edits improve candidates within a niche, while quality-diversity selection keeps useful alternatives across niches rather than reducing the whole population to one winner. Ancestry records retain the rejected candidates too.\nMutation and evaluation operators start fixed. That lets an experiment change a candidate without changing how it is judged at the same time. Changing the rule develops this distinction.\nWhat would establish useful hierarchy?\nThe connector example needs a correct answer, delivered at an acceptable cost. A diagram of nested groups cannot tell us whether those groups learned to organise or whether they helped. Compare the learned arrangement with a single stronger unit, a flat collective, a central coordinator and an imposed hierarchy at matched resources.\nHENA would keep its own swarm store and run history. Reckon would provide ways to inspect and evaluate records from that store.\nThe swarm might make correlated errors, lose a joint fact, route everything through one bottleneck, fill memory with noise, communicate too much or fail to train reliably. Pairwise agreement shows how units can agree in pairs while missing something that matters to the whole. The emergence inquiry asks which interactions actually help.\nFrom a working baseline to the native architecture\nThe first planned swarm would coordinate stock open-weight models through MUON's unit interface. A unit built for MUON could later replace one of those models through the same interface. This gives the research a baseline against which to test learning from surprises, memory that learns from task errors and learned ways to divide the work.\nThe longer-term design would train the units, routes and memory together and test whether a useful hierarchy emerges. Q-HENA explores the corresponding quantum architecture.\nHow HENA relates to Pale's explicit coordination\nPale describes an organisation's responsibilities and coordination explicitly, and HENA asks how more of that organisation could be learned through local interactions; Causality's About page sets out how the two relate. HENA would need MUON's unit and MONAD's account of how a description can change, and that need grows as the system organises itself.\nInspect the source\nWith source access, developers can check the kernel crate in the HENA repository, which re-exports the MONAD, MUON and Reckon kernels because swarm coordination is not implemented. They can also inspect what the kernel exposes; its developer manual covers the resource and recovery decisions an implementation would need to make.\n","url":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"copyrightHolder":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}],"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:hena","name":"HENA","description":"A research project on how small reasoning units learn to solve a task together, the collective that the Causality programme works towards.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/","identifier":"content:causality-project-hena","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA","url":"https://causality.in/hena/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/#article","identifier":"content:hena-docs","name":"How units form a collective","url":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/","identifier":"content:hena-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA mentions","url":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","name":"HENA: Hierarchically Evolving Neural Automata","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","name":"HENA"}]}],"mentions":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:monad","name":"MONAD","description":"A calculus for preserving, compressing and changing what a description can express.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/","identifier":"content:causality-project-monad","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MONAD","url":"https://causality.in/monad/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/descriptions-and-repair/#article","identifier":"content:monad-docs","name":"Descriptions and repair","url":"https://causality.in/monad/descriptions-and-repair/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/mentions/","identifier":"content:monad-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MONAD mentions","url":"https://causality.in/monad/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD/releases/download/v0.1.0/MONAD.pdf","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD/releases/download/v0.1.0/MONAD.pdf","name":"MONAD: Minimal Ontological Notions for Axiomatic Distinction","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/monad/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/monad/overview","name":"MONAD"}]},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:muon","name":"MUON","description":"Research into how a small reasoning unit uses past observations, learns and works with other units.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-muon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MUON","url":"https://causality.in/muon/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/computation-state-and-learning/#article","identifier":"content:muon-docs","name":"Computation, state and learning","url":"https://causality.in/muon/computation-state-and-learning/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/mentions/","identifier":"content:muon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MUON mentions","url":"https://causality.in/muon/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/paper/MUON.tex","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/paper/MUON.tex","name":"MUON: Micro-Unit Operating Network","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/muon/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/muon/overview","name":"MUON"}]},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:pale","name":"Pale","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux","description":"See which review instructions apply in a repository and what would change before you install them. Pale compiles those instructions and permissions from one workspace.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#brand"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale","targetProduct":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/","identifier":"content:pale-project","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Pale","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/understand-your-world/#article","identifier":"content:pale-docs","name":"Introduction to Pale","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/understand-your-world/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/mentions/","identifier":"content:pale-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Pale mentions","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.rawthoughts.in/pale/overview"}]},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:q-hena","name":"Q-HENA","description":"Research into how quantum units share work, communicate and recover together.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/","identifier":"content:causality-project-q-hena","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-HENA","url":"https://causality.in/q-hena/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/quantum-collective-computation/#article","identifier":"content:q-hena-docs","name":"Quantum collective computation","url":"https://causality.in/q-hena/quantum-collective-computation/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/mentions/","identifier":"content:q-hena-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-HENA mentions","url":"https://causality.in/q-hena/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/q-hena/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/q-hena/overview","name":"Q-HENA"}]},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:reckon","name":"Reckon","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux","description":"Measure what memory changes for its cost, and compare proposed improvements before deciding to use them.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon","targetProduct":{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-reckon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Reckon","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/understand-the-memory-laboratory/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-docs","name":"Understand the memory laboratory","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/understand-the-memory-laboratory/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/mentions/","identifier":"content:reckon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Reckon mentions","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/reckon/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/reckon/overview","name":"Reckon"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/","identifier":"content:causality-project-hena","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA","url":"https://causality.in/hena/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/","identifier":"content:hena-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA mentions","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":11,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/recovery-across-a-changing-world/#article","identifier":"content:q-hena-recovery-is-a-claim","name":"Recovery across a changing world","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/recovery-across-a-changing-world/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/quantum-comparison/#article","identifier":"content:q-muon-quantum-comparison","name":"Comparing a quantum unit with its classical counterpart","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/quantum-comparison/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/emergence-needs-a-comparison/#article","identifier":"content:hena-emergence-needs-a-comparison","name":"What would count as emergent hierarchy?","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/emergence-needs-a-comparison/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":4,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/when-pairs-agree-and-the-whole-differs/#article","identifier":"content:hena-pairs-do-not-contain-the-whole","name":"Every pair can agree while the whole differs","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/when-pairs-agree-and-the-whole-differs/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":5,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/three-tests-for-a-reasoning-unit/#article","identifier":"content:muon-a-shared-interface-is-not-an-equal-model","name":"Three tests for a reasoning unit","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/three-tests-for-a-reasoning-unit/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":6,"item":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/collections/systems/learning-from-a-recommendation/learning-from-a-judgement/#article","identifier":"content:counsel-judgement-learning","name":"What a judgment can learn from","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/collections/systems/learning-from-a-recommendation/learning-from-a-judgement/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":7,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/changing-the-rule/#article","identifier":"content:causality-change-the-rule-not-the-judge","name":"Change the rule without moving the finish line","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/changing-the-rule/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":8,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/the-intelligence-between-the-parts/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-the-intelligence-between-the-parts","name":"The intelligence between the parts","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/the-intelligence-between-the-parts/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":9,"item":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/collections/systems/making-a-working-world-intelligible/architecture/#article","identifier":"content:pale-architecture","name":"How Pale fits together","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/collections/systems/making-a-working-world-intelligible/architecture/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":10,"item":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/collections/systems/making-a-working-world-intelligible/the-cost-of-understanding/#article","identifier":"content:pale-understanding-cost","name":"The cost of understanding","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/collections/systems/making-a-working-world-intelligible/the-cost-of-understanding/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":11,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/building-a-place-to-think/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-building-a-place-to-think","name":"Building a place to think","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/building-a-place-to-think/"}}],"itemListOrder":"https://schema.org/ItemListOrderDescending"},"description":"Writing that discusses or references HENA across the connected publications.","text":"","url":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:hena","name":"HENA","description":"A research project on how small reasoning units learn to solve a task together, the collective that the Causality programme works towards.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/","identifier":"content:causality-project-hena","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA","url":"https://causality.in/hena/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/#article","identifier":"content:hena-docs","name":"How units form a collective","url":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/","identifier":"content:hena-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA mentions","url":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","name":"HENA: Hierarchically Evolving Neural Automata","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","name":"HENA"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/","identifier":"content:causality-project-monad","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MONAD","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"description":"A calculus for preserving, compressing and changing what a description can express.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"text":"MONAD\nAn inventory says a part is in stock. Can it tell you whether the part fits the machine? MONAD studies which answers survive a change of description and what you need to recover a missing answer. It asks what would let a fixed frame become something a system can change without losing account of its meaning.\nWhen a better answer needs a different language\nWork through the stock-and-fit example to see which questions an inventory can answer. A Lean library and paper develop the mathematics. A small Rust kernel supplies types and numerical operations for research code.\nFollow what a description can answer\nStart with the answer you need. Try to reconstruct it from the shorter description, then work out which information a new observation could supply.\nState the questions\nDecide which questions you need to answer now and after any changes you plan to make. An inventory for ordering parts and one for repairing a machine can need different information.\nExamine the transformation\nCompare cases that the summary treats alike. In the example below, the stock summary gives the same answer for both parts even though only one fits.\nRepair or enrich the account\nRecover the missing distinction from an available source, or enrich a description whose objects, questions or rules cannot express what is needed. The longer-term calculus also asks how the rules of change can themselves become objects of change.\nQuestions a description must answer\nThe stock summary cannot tell the two parts apart, but a compatibility record might supply the missing answer. Finding that record repairs an omission. If the description has no way to represent machines or their compatibility with parts, it needs a richer language before it can even ask the question. Descriptions and repair follows that distinction from an available observation to a change in what the description can express.\nQuestions about MONAD\nWhat does MONAD let researchers do?\nState which distinctions a representation must preserve, examine a transformation or compression, and reason about the information and cost needed to repair an inadequate account. The Lean library makes parts of that mathematics exact, and the Rust kernel brings its types and numerical operations into research code.\nIs MONAD only about AI?\nMONAD is a pure mathematical programme, and AI is one source of its examples. Adaptive models, changing scientific descriptions and other systems supply examples and tests; they do not define its axioms.\nHow does the stock-and-fit example relate to the larger aim?\nIt shows a summary preserving availability while losing the distinction needed for repair. Moving from that fixed example to descriptions whose objects, questions and rules can change is the broader research task.\nDo I need software to follow MONAD?\nYou can read the worked example without software. Descriptions and repair then follows the same question into the Lean library and the Rust kernel.\n","url":"https://causality.in/monad/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:monad","name":"MONAD","description":"A calculus for preserving, compressing and changing what a description can express.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/","identifier":"content:causality-project-monad","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MONAD","url":"https://causality.in/monad/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/descriptions-and-repair/#article","identifier":"content:monad-docs","name":"Descriptions and repair","url":"https://causality.in/monad/descriptions-and-repair/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/mentions/","identifier":"content:monad-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MONAD mentions","url":"https://causality.in/monad/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD/releases/download/v0.1.0/MONAD.pdf","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD/releases/download/v0.1.0/MONAD.pdf","name":"MONAD: Minimal Ontological Notions for Axiomatic Distinction","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/monad/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/monad/overview","name":"MONAD"}]}],"hasPart":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/descriptions-and-repair/#article","identifier":"content:monad-docs","name":"Descriptions and repair","url":"https://causality.in/monad/descriptions-and-repair/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/what-a-summary-preserves/#article","identifier":"content:monad-what-a-summary-preserves","name":"What a summary preserves","url":"https://causality.in/monad/what-a-summary-preserves/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/descriptions-and-repair/#article","identifier":"content:monad-docs","@type":"Article","name":"Descriptions and repair","headline":"Descriptions and repair","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["endogenous distinguishability","changing descriptions","answer preservation","recoverability"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/descriptions-and-repair/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Descriptions and repair","url":"https://causality.in/monad/descriptions-and-repair/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"MONAD","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Descriptions and repair","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/descriptions-and-repair/"}}]}]},"description":"See which answers survive a shorter description and when a missing distinction needs a richer one.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"text":"Descriptions and repair\nTwo parts are in stock. One fits the machine you need to repair; the other does not. An inventory can be perfectly accurate about availability and still leave you unable to choose between them.\nMONAD starts from this difficulty: which answers does a description keep, and what happens when a later question needs a distinction it left out?\nA small exact example\nWrite the state of a part as (a,b). The first value says whether it is available; the second says whether it fits. Repair requires both. A summary that keeps only a=1 gives the same answer for (1,0) and (1,1), although only the second part permits repair. No amount of calculation on that summary can tell you which part fits.\nWrite the answer you need as f:X\\to Y and the summary as q:X\\to S. The answer survives if there is a rule g:S\\to Y such that\nf=g\\circ q.\nIn other words, you can get the required answer from the summary alone. When two states share a summary but need different answers, no such rule exists. What a summary preserves works through all four states and shows how choosing a different question changes which summary is sufficient.\nThe representation can become the problem\nPerhaps a compatibility record can supply the missing answer. Then the problem is finding and reading that record, at whatever cost the observation requires. But suppose the description has no way to represent machines or the relation between a machine and a part. It needs more than another value: it needs a way to express compatibility.\nSummary and repair follows the first problem, recovering an answer from information we can reach. Changing the description follows the second. MONAD asks how to reason about both, including which earlier answers a richer description should preserve.\nThat is the ambition behind a calculus of endogenous distinguishability: the descriptions, the questions they can express and the rules for changing them can themselves change. The eventual calculus must account for those changes without allowing a rule to justify its own replacement.\nWhat a description can distinguish\nThe four part states form the carrier, the things this description represents. Asking whether a part is available is a probe. A description also declares continuations, the changes it permits. Two states may give the same answer now, yet become distinguishable after one of those changes.\nTo call two states indistinguishable now, every present probe must agree on them. To say they remain indistinguishable, every probe after every permitted continuation must agree too. The choice of questions and continuations therefore matters as much as the current values.\nChanging availability from 0 to 1 leaves the four-state description intact. Adding the compatibility question separates states the availability question grouped together. Adding kinds of objects the carrier could not represent goes further still. These are different changes, with different information to account for.\nWhat the calculus needs to explain\nFour working requirements follow from that question. They guide the research; they are not a finished set of axioms.\nGenerative openness asks how a description can express a distinction it could not previously support. Finding the old compatibility bit is different from introducing machines and compatibility into a language that had neither. Both can change the answers we can give, but they do not change the description in the same way.\nAnticipatory compression asks what a summary must retain for the future changes we choose to allow. Keeping today's answers is not enough when tomorrow's question separates states the summary merged. The permitted changes, approximation and cost must be stated.\nInvariant accounting asks what each transformation preserves, hides, loses or makes newly expressible. Information that can be recovered from an accessible record is in a different position from information no available source can supply.\nTrajectory-sensitive relevance asks how the path taken, and the futures still possible, change which distinctions matter. Two descriptions can agree now without making the same information relevant along every later path.\nThree principles for an honest comparison\nLooking the same through today's questions does not make two states identical under every later question. A system can also be optimal within an inadequate description: the best stock count still cannot decide whether a part fits.\nA persistent error deserves the same care. It might be measurement noise or a poor fit inside the current model; it might also point to a missing distinction. MONAD has to investigate those possibilities, rather than assume every mismatch calls for a new language. Preserving answers, choosing an adequate description and explaining a residual are separate questions.\nWhat the Lean library already makes exact\nThe Lean library makes parts of this question exact. It proves results about grouping states that give the same answers, limits on future-safe compression, residual budgets, repair from available sources and the size of a supplement. It also treats probabilistic comparison and gives counterexamples to treating detectable influence as an order. The wider calculus of changing carriers and transformation rules remains open.\nThe example above requires every declared answer to survive exactly. Preservation under uncertainty asks what can be said when the descriptions instead produce probability laws. Under a stated simulation error, it bounds how much a decision loss can change. A small numerical distance alone does not establish that two descriptions are equivalent or that recovering an answer is affordable.\nTake the example into the Lean library\nTry the four-state example on paper first. In the MONAD repository, you can then read the same example in Lean and inspect the hypotheses behind the theorem. Its formal guide leads through the rest of the proof library.\nFor Rust integration, the repository's kernel guide compares two finite probability distributions.\nNote\nThe kernel's scope. The kernel provides types and a few numerical operations for a fixed-context observer model. It does not implement the changing-description calculus or solve repair problems automatically.\nWhere MONAD sits beside Pale, MUON and HENA\nPale works within an explicit world model. MUON and HENA investigate how adaptive units and their collective organisation might change. Their practical questions can test MONAD's usefulness, while its mathematical definitions remain independent of those applications. Economic transitions pose a related question: when is a changed parameter enough, and when do the actors or relations themselves need to change?\nWorking with the source\nThe Lean library and paper require source access. The existing proofs hold under their stated assumptions. A downstream adaptive system still needs tests of its own behaviour.\n","url":"https://causality.in/monad/descriptions-and-repair/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"copyrightHolder":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}],"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:monad","name":"MONAD","description":"A calculus for preserving, compressing and changing what a description can express.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/","identifier":"content:causality-project-monad","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MONAD","url":"https://causality.in/monad/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/descriptions-and-repair/#article","identifier":"content:monad-docs","name":"Descriptions and repair","url":"https://causality.in/monad/descriptions-and-repair/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/mentions/","identifier":"content:monad-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MONAD mentions","url":"https://causality.in/monad/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD/releases/download/v0.1.0/MONAD.pdf","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD/releases/download/v0.1.0/MONAD.pdf","name":"MONAD: Minimal Ontological Notions for Axiomatic Distinction","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/monad/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/monad/overview","name":"MONAD"}]}],"mentions":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:hena","name":"HENA","description":"A research project on how small reasoning units learn to solve a task together, the collective that the Causality programme works towards.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/","identifier":"content:causality-project-hena","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA","url":"https://causality.in/hena/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/#article","identifier":"content:hena-docs","name":"How units form a collective","url":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/","identifier":"content:hena-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA mentions","url":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","name":"HENA: Hierarchically Evolving Neural Automata","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","name":"HENA"}]},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:muon","name":"MUON","description":"Research into how a small reasoning unit uses past observations, learns and works with other units.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-muon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MUON","url":"https://causality.in/muon/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/computation-state-and-learning/#article","identifier":"content:muon-docs","name":"Computation, state and learning","url":"https://causality.in/muon/computation-state-and-learning/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/mentions/","identifier":"content:muon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MUON mentions","url":"https://causality.in/muon/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/paper/MUON.tex","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/paper/MUON.tex","name":"MUON: Micro-Unit Operating Network","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/muon/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/muon/overview","name":"MUON"}]},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:pale","name":"Pale","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux","description":"See which review instructions apply in a repository and what would change before you install them. Pale compiles those instructions and permissions from one workspace.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#brand"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale","targetProduct":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/","identifier":"content:pale-project","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Pale","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/understand-your-world/#article","identifier":"content:pale-docs","name":"Introduction to Pale","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/understand-your-world/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/mentions/","identifier":"content:pale-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Pale mentions","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.rawthoughts.in/pale/overview"}]},{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:post-agi-economics","name":"Post-AGI Economics","description":"Research into production, scarcity, distribution and the models we use when cognitive work becomes cheaper.","brand":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/#brand"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/hasPart":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/#entity"}],"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/","identifier":"content:post-agi-economics-home","@type":"WebPage","name":"When intelligence gets cheap","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/","identifier":"content:causality-project-monad","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MONAD","url":"https://causality.in/monad/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/mentions/","identifier":"content:monad-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MONAD mentions","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":13,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/future-without-retrocausation/#article","identifier":"content:monad-future-without-retrocausation","name":"The future can constrain a model without sending a message back","url":"https://causality.in/blog/future-without-retrocausation/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/when-the-support-moves/#article","identifier":"content:monad-when-the-support-moves","name":"When the support moves","url":"https://causality.in/blog/when-the-support-moves/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/recovering-a-distinction-from-a-noisy-answer/#article","identifier":"content:monad-a-noisy-answer-is-not-an-exact-repair","name":"A noisy answer is not an exact repair","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/recovering-a-distinction-from-a-noisy-answer/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":4,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/one-experiment-through-another/#article","identifier":"content:monad-one-experiment-through-another","name":"When one experiment can stand in for another","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/one-experiment-through-another/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":5,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/how-tolerances-accumulate/#article","identifier":"content:monad-approximate-is-not-equivalent","name":"How tolerances accumulate","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/how-tolerances-accumulate/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":6,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/the-price-of-another-question/#article","identifier":"content:monad-the-price-of-another-question","name":"The price of another question","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/the-price-of-another-question/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":7,"item":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/collections/systems/making-a-working-world-intelligible/architecture/#article","identifier":"content:pale-architecture","name":"How Pale fits together","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/collections/systems/making-a-working-world-intelligible/architecture/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":8,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/compression-is-a-promise/#article","identifier":"content:monad-compression-is-a-promise","name":"Compression is a promise about questions","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/compression-is-a-promise/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":9,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/budgeted-questions/#article","identifier":"content:monad-budgeted-questions","name":"What can you distinguish with one more move?","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/from-summary-to-repair/budgeted-questions/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":10,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/when-effects-cancel/#article","identifier":"content:monad-influence-is-not-transitive","name":"When effects cancel along a path","url":"https://causality.in/blog/when-effects-cancel/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":11,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/which-factors-did-we-recover/#article","identifier":"content:monad-which-factors-did-we-recover","name":"Which factors did we recover?","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/which-factors-did-we-recover/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":12,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/when-the-description-must-change/#article","identifier":"content:monad-when-the-description-must-change","name":"When the description must change","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/when-the-description-must-change/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":13,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/the-ineffable-geometry/#article","identifier":"content:causality-the-ineffable-geometry","name":"The ineffable geometry","url":"https://causality.in/blog/the-ineffable-geometry/"}}],"itemListOrder":"https://schema.org/ItemListOrderDescending"},"description":"Writing that discusses or references MONAD across the connected publications.","text":"","url":"https://causality.in/monad/mentions/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:monad","name":"MONAD","description":"A calculus for preserving, compressing and changing what a description can express.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/","identifier":"content:causality-project-monad","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MONAD","url":"https://causality.in/monad/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/descriptions-and-repair/#article","identifier":"content:monad-docs","name":"Descriptions and repair","url":"https://causality.in/monad/descriptions-and-repair/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/mentions/","identifier":"content:monad-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MONAD mentions","url":"https://causality.in/monad/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD/releases/download/v0.1.0/MONAD.pdf","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD/releases/download/v0.1.0/MONAD.pdf","name":"MONAD: Minimal Ontological Notions for Axiomatic Distinction","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/monad/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/monad/overview","name":"MONAD"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/what-a-summary-preserves/#article","identifier":"content:monad-what-a-summary-preserves","@type":"TechArticle","name":"What a summary preserves","headline":"What a summary preserves","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["sufficient summaries","observational equivalence","answer-preserving compression","source-dependent repair"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/what-a-summary-preserves/","@type":"WebPage","name":"What a summary preserves","url":"https://causality.in/monad/what-a-summary-preserves/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"MONAD","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"What a summary preserves","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/what-a-summary-preserves/"}}]}]},"description":"Work through answer-preserving compression and the different ways a missing distinction can be repaired.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"text":"What a summary preserves\nA summary can discard details while keeping the answers you need. This four-state example shows how to check that claim, choose a sufficient summary, and identify the information a failed summary would need. No software is required.\nFix the question before judging the summary\nLet a state be (a,b), where a says whether a part is available and b whether it fits the machine. Both take values 0 or 1. The repair question is whether both conditions hold, so its answer is f(a,b)=a\\land b.\nSuppose the summary keeps only availability, so q(a,b)=a. Try to answer the repair question using only the middle column before checking the last one.\nState\tAvailability summary\tRepair possible?\t\n(0,0)\t0\tNo\t\n(0,1)\t0\tNo\t\n(1,0)\t1\tNo\t\n(1,1)\t1\tYes\t\nThe first two states can share a summary for this question because their answers agree. The last two cannot: the same summary, 1, would need to yield both No and Yes. No rule using only that summary can answer correctly for every state.\nIn general, an answer f:X\\to Y survives a summary q:X\\to S when there is a rule g:S\\to Y with f=g\\circ q. This says what has to remain possible after compression; it does not require retaining every detail of the original state.\nKeep the answer rather than every original detail\nFor the repair question alone, a sufficient summary is the repair answer itself: r(a,b)=a\\land b. It has two possible values. Three states share the answer No, and only (1,1) has the answer Yes:\n\\{(0,0),(0,1),(1,0)\\},\\qquad \\{(1,1)\\}.\nThese groups are the observational equivalence classes for this one question: states belong together exactly when the required answer agrees. The quotient is the set of those classes. Reading the class tells us the answer, so compression to these two classes is sufficient. Any sufficient summary must keep (1,1) apart from the other three states, though it may retain additional distinctions.\nNotice that r cannot tell whether a part is available when repair is impossible. That was not part of its contract. If both availability and repair must remain answerable, the coarsest sufficient grouping has three classes:\n\\{(0,0),(0,1)\\},\\qquad \\{(1,0)\\},\\qquad \\{(1,1)\\}.\nThe pair of answers (a,a\\land b) identifies these three classes. It still merges the two unavailable-part states because neither required question separates them. This is semantic minimality: the fewest distinctions needed for the declared answers. It does not yet minimise storage, computation or the cost of obtaining those answers.\nA different question changes the judgement\nIf the only question were whether a part is available, the original summary q=a would be sufficient. Its inadequacy appears when the repair question needs compatibility. A description can therefore be sufficient for one purpose and insufficient for another without either claim being contradictory.\nThat is why preserving every currently asked question is not the same as preserving every question a later language or task might admit. The broader programme asks what happens as those descriptions, questions and change rules themselves grow.\nNow admit the compatibility question b independently of availability. The two coordinate questions a and b together separate all four states. Any summary preserving both must keep all four states apart. A different encoding is possible; a merger is not. The formal obstruction generalises this argument: if a family of questions separates every distinct pair and a compression preserves every question in that family exactly, the compression must be injective, meaning different states never share an output.\nThe assumptions matter. A restricted question family allowed the two- and three-class summaries above. Allowing error, limiting continuations, paying for later observations or revising the summary changes the problem. MONAD studies how to state and account for those choices, rather than treating perfect preservation under unrestricted future questions as a useful compression rule.\nRecover information or enrich the description\nIf an accessible compatibility record exists, a permitted observation can supply what the summary omitted. Whether that repair is possible depends on the actual source and access, not only on the fact that another bit would be helpful.\nStarting from q=a, no further observation is needed when a=0: the repair answer is already No. When a=1, asking for b determines the answer. If that query is permitted and costs one unit, the strategy costs zero on the unavailable-part branch and one on the available-part branch. An average cost would also need a distribution over states. If no source of b is accessible, noticing the collision between (1,0) and (1,1) cannot recover the missing value.\nKeeping b before discarding the original state would be a different operation. A supplement encoder with access to (a,b) can save the missing information; a later procedure with access only to a cannot manufacture it. Revealing this old coordinate also adds nothing to the original four-state carrier. It is not evidence that a new dimension has emerged.\nA description with no objects for machines, parts or compatibility has a different problem. Another value in the old vocabulary cannot express the missing relation. The account needs a richer description before it can ask the repair question properly.\nSummary and repair develops the information-and-access problem, while changing the description follows the need for a different representational frame. The wider calculus remains a research programme.\nThe formal guide in the MONAD repository translates this example into Lean's existing definitions and results. The kernel's finite-distribution example addresses a separate, narrower task: computing a numerical distance between supplied probability laws. It does not implement the quotient or the repair strategy above.\n","url":"https://causality.in/monad/what-a-summary-preserves/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"copyrightHolder":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}],"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:monad","name":"MONAD","description":"A calculus for preserving, compressing and changing what a description can express.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/","identifier":"content:causality-project-monad","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MONAD","url":"https://causality.in/monad/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/descriptions-and-repair/#article","identifier":"content:monad-docs","name":"Descriptions and repair","url":"https://causality.in/monad/descriptions-and-repair/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/mentions/","identifier":"content:monad-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MONAD mentions","url":"https://causality.in/monad/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD/releases/download/v0.1.0/MONAD.pdf","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MONAD/releases/download/v0.1.0/MONAD.pdf","name":"MONAD: Minimal Ontological Notions for Axiomatic Distinction","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/monad/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/monad/overview","name":"MONAD"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/","identifier":"content:causality-project-monad","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MONAD","url":"https://causality.in/monad/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-muon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MUON","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"description":"Research into how a small reasoning unit uses past observations, learns and works with other units.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"text":"MUON\nA road closes just before a route request arrives. Can a small reasoning unit carry the notice into its answer? MUON studies how a unit uses earlier information and learns from experience. Follow the route example.\nA building block that can keep learning\nA common interface lets the caller try an existing model first and test a unit that learns through the same calls. HENA studies how several units work together.\nExperience that changes the next computation\nThe road notice can change the next route, help with a later request or reveal a recurring mistake. Follow what the unit does with each kind of experience.\nTemporal state that reaches the answer\nThe unit combines the current question, earlier state and messages. The route it suggests should change when the road closes.\nExperience worth retaining\nA unit marks the notice as useful. The store decides whether to keep it within its memory budget so a later request can use it.\nLearning across episodes\nRepeated mistakes change how the unit weighs notices in future tasks. Surprise adjusts the rate of learning within a stated limit.\nProposals for a richer structure\nIf the same kind of error persists, researchers can try a change to how the unit routes computation. A separate comparison shows whether the change helps.\nQuestions about MUON\nWhat exists today?\nToday the repository holds the slot contract as a draft and a kernel that builds on MONAD. A conformance suite, then adapters for open-weight models, are in front, and no unit runs yet.\nIs MUON an adapter around an existing model?\nMUON starts with adapters for existing open-weight models. A smaller model designed for MUON later uses the same interface for inference, state, records, resources and lifecycle.\nHow does MUON relate to Counsel and Pale?\nMUON studies a learning unit for the scale of work where Counsel supplies local judgments within Pale’s explicit organisation, and HENA studies how such units learn to organise collectively; Causality’s About page sets out how those directions connect.\nDoes the design require many small units to win?\nNo. The comparison includes many identical units, one stronger unit, a mixture and no swarm, with the full resource cost counted. A negative result can leave a useful unit interface and a stronger baseline.\nWhere should I start?\nStart with the road notice. Computation, state and learning follows it into the next route answer and lays out the comparison between different reasoning units. Three tests for a reasoning unit then explains why meeting an interface, being adequate for a task and replacing another model are separate claims.\n","url":"https://causality.in/muon/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:muon","name":"MUON","description":"Research into how a small reasoning unit uses past observations, learns and works with other units.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-muon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MUON","url":"https://causality.in/muon/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/computation-state-and-learning/#article","identifier":"content:muon-docs","name":"Computation, state and learning","url":"https://causality.in/muon/computation-state-and-learning/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/mentions/","identifier":"content:muon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MUON mentions","url":"https://causality.in/muon/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/paper/MUON.tex","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/paper/MUON.tex","name":"MUON: Micro-Unit Operating Network","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/muon/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/muon/overview","name":"MUON"}]}],"mentions":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/counsel/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:counsel","name":"Counsel","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux","description":"Local judgment where rules leave a choice, with a separate model service to help Scope find useful context.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#brand"},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/counsel/","identifier":"content:counsel-introduction","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Counsel","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/counsel/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/counsel/frame-a-judgment/#article","identifier":"content:counsel-docs","name":"Frame a useful judgment","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/counsel/frame-a-judgment/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/counsel/mentions/","identifier":"content:counsel-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Counsel mentions","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/counsel/mentions/"}]},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:hena","name":"HENA","description":"A research project on how small reasoning units learn to solve a task together, the collective that the Causality programme works towards.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/","identifier":"content:causality-project-hena","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA","url":"https://causality.in/hena/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/#article","identifier":"content:hena-docs","name":"How units form a collective","url":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/","identifier":"content:hena-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA mentions","url":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","name":"HENA: Hierarchically Evolving Neural Automata","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","name":"HENA"}]},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:pale","name":"Pale","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux","description":"See which review instructions apply in a repository and what would change before you install them. Pale compiles those instructions and permissions from one workspace.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#brand"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale","targetProduct":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/","identifier":"content:pale-project","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Pale","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/understand-your-world/#article","identifier":"content:pale-docs","name":"Introduction to Pale","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/understand-your-world/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/mentions/","identifier":"content:pale-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Pale mentions","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.rawthoughts.in/pale/overview"}]},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:q-muon","name":"Q-MUON","description":"Research into a quantum reasoning unit that keeps MUON’s caller interface.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-q-muon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-MUON","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/the-quantum-local-unit/#article","identifier":"content:q-muon-docs","name":"The quantum local unit","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/the-quantum-local-unit/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/mentions/","identifier":"content:q-muon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-MUON mentions","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/q-muon/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/q-muon/overview","name":"Q-MUON"}]}],"hasPart":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/computation-state-and-learning/#article","identifier":"content:muon-docs","name":"Computation, state and learning","url":"https://causality.in/muon/computation-state-and-learning/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/how-experience-changes-a-unit/#article","identifier":"content:muon-how-experience-changes-a-unit","name":"How experience can change a unit","url":"https://causality.in/muon/how-experience-changes-a-unit/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/computation-state-and-learning/#article","identifier":"content:muon-docs","@type":"TechArticle","name":"Computation, state and learning","headline":"Computation, state and learning","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["adaptive reasoning units","temporal state","sparse routing","unit capability contracts"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/computation-state-and-learning/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Computation, state and learning","url":"https://causality.in/muon/computation-state-and-learning/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"MUON","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Computation, state and learning","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/computation-state-and-learning/"}}]}]},"description":"Follow a road notice into the next route answer and see how MUON would compare different reasoning units.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"text":"Computation, state and learning\nA unit learns that a road has closed. The next question asks for a route. Does the closure change the answer? MUON uses questions like this to design how one unit could carry information forward and how a caller could compare two models doing the same work.\nOne unit and a collective ask different questions\nMUON asks what one unit needs to compute, remember and learn. If another unit received the notice, the question becomes how it would reach the unit making the decision. HENA studies that kind of coordination. Test the unit’s answer and the way units work together separately.\nLocal computation with useful temporal state\nThe long-term design is a micro-transformer unit: small enough to run in numbers, working from its own inputs and state. Units would exchange sparse messages rather than send everything through a central hub. Memory would be spread across units and their hosts’ stores, using a shared record protocol; each store would decide what to keep.\nThe road notice must have a path into the later answer. One candidate ingredient, selective state-space processing, updates a compact state as inputs arrive. Another, routed expert computation, chooses which expert computations to run. Combining them leaves a question: does the earlier state only choose an expert, or can it also change what that expert predicts?\nCompare two designs that combine those ingredients. In the first, the state carrying the road notice affects only expert selection. In the second, it also has a direct path to the prediction. Give both the same notice, route question and available routes. If selecting a different expert still sends the traveller down the closed road, test whether the direct path changes that answer. This is a proposed comparison of the path information takes, not a choice between memory and routing.\nRouting also needs a comparison of its own. Several learned transformations may depend on each other. Shared routing chooses experts for that group together; independent routing makes each choice separately. The design tests both rather than assuming that separately chosen parts will cooperate.\nSurprise can change different things\nAn unexpected observation could change the state used for the next answer, deserve storage for later retrieval, or contribute to learning across episodes. Repeated failures might also suggest a different structure. How experience can change a unit follows those possibilities through the road-closure example.\nA surprising route answer tells you where to look. It could come from an old notice, a missed message or a model error. MUON would put its candidate surprise signals on a common finite scale before using them to adjust a learning rate or score an observation for storage. A proposed structural change would need its own comparison. The surprise inquiry works through how to test the cause.\nOne interface, so a better unit can replace a stock model\nThe caller should be able to compare a new unit on the route task without rebuilding the application around that model. Stock open-weight models would fill the role first, through adapters for MLX, GGUF/llama.cpp, Ollama and Hugging Face. The later micro-transformer would answer through the same interface.\nA common inference call is only part of what the caller needs:\nWhat the caller needs to do\tWhat the unit would expose\t\nAsk the route question and receive an answer as it is produced\tInference, including streaming\t\nInspect the state being carried forward or recover a snapshot\tDeclared state and snapshots\t\nFollow the notice, answer and resource use through the run\tOrdered records\t\nCheck whether the work fits and obtain capacity for it\tResource estimates, reservations and refusal when capacity is unavailable\t\nStart the unit, advance it, recover or stop it\tLifecycle operations and checkpoints\t\nThe caller also needs to know which model revision, tokenizer, quantisation and backend produced the answer, and which behaviours that version supports. The developer manual in the MUON repository explains how an adapter would report those choices and expose the interface.\nEstimating the resources a request needs does not reserve them. An allocation example in that manual follows two requests that each fit a shared budget but cannot both spend it. Stock models would remain available as controls and fallbacks when another unit is tested.\nWhy many small units are worth testing\nThe hope is that useful organisation can accomplish work otherwise done by a larger model. The comparison includes many identical units, one stronger unit, a mixture and no swarm, using the same tasks and resource ceilings. Model inference, routing, memory, verification, failed proposals and communication all count toward cost. A cheaper answer cannot win by missing the task’s minimum performance.\nIf the many-unit design loses, the system can keep the strongest single or mixed alternative behind the same interface. Three tests for a reasoning unit explains why meeting an interface, being adequate for a task and replacing another model are separate claims. Q-MUON takes the one-unit question to a quantum substrate.\nA possible place in a working system\nCounsel is designed for local judgment within Pale’s compiled organisation, and a future MUON unit might serve a judgment role if it improves that work; Causality’s About page sets out the architecture MUON and HENA explore at those two scales.\nInspect the source\nWith the source checkouts, developers can compile the kernel crate in the MUON repository. It re-exports MONAD’s kernel, since the unit itself is not implemented yet.\n","url":"https://causality.in/muon/computation-state-and-learning/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"copyrightHolder":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}],"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:muon","name":"MUON","description":"Research into how a small reasoning unit uses past observations, learns and works with other units.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-muon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MUON","url":"https://causality.in/muon/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/computation-state-and-learning/#article","identifier":"content:muon-docs","name":"Computation, state and learning","url":"https://causality.in/muon/computation-state-and-learning/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/mentions/","identifier":"content:muon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MUON mentions","url":"https://causality.in/muon/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/paper/MUON.tex","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/paper/MUON.tex","name":"MUON: Micro-Unit Operating Network","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/muon/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/muon/overview","name":"MUON"}]}],"mentions":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/counsel/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:counsel","name":"Counsel","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux","description":"Local judgment where rules leave a choice, with a separate model service to help Scope find useful context.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#brand"},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/counsel/","identifier":"content:counsel-introduction","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Counsel","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/counsel/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/counsel/frame-a-judgment/#article","identifier":"content:counsel-docs","name":"Frame a useful judgment","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/counsel/frame-a-judgment/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/counsel/mentions/","identifier":"content:counsel-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Counsel mentions","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/counsel/mentions/"}]},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:hena","name":"HENA","description":"A research project on how small reasoning units learn to solve a task together, the collective that the Causality programme works towards.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/","identifier":"content:causality-project-hena","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA","url":"https://causality.in/hena/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/#article","identifier":"content:hena-docs","name":"How units form a collective","url":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/","identifier":"content:hena-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA mentions","url":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","name":"HENA: Hierarchically Evolving Neural Automata","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","name":"HENA"}]},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:pale","name":"Pale","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux","description":"See which review instructions apply in a repository and what would change before you install them. Pale compiles those instructions and permissions from one workspace.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#brand"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale","targetProduct":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/","identifier":"content:pale-project","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Pale","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/understand-your-world/#article","identifier":"content:pale-docs","name":"Introduction to Pale","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/understand-your-world/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/mentions/","identifier":"content:pale-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Pale mentions","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.rawthoughts.in/pale/overview"}]},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:q-muon","name":"Q-MUON","description":"Research into a quantum reasoning unit that keeps MUON’s caller interface.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-q-muon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-MUON","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/the-quantum-local-unit/#article","identifier":"content:q-muon-docs","name":"The quantum local unit","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/the-quantum-local-unit/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/mentions/","identifier":"content:q-muon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-MUON mentions","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/q-muon/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/q-muon/overview","name":"Q-MUON"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-muon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MUON","url":"https://causality.in/muon/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/how-experience-changes-a-unit/#article","identifier":"content:muon-how-experience-changes-a-unit","@type":"Article","name":"How experience can change a unit","headline":"How experience can change a unit","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["surprise-driven learning","temporal updates","parameter learning","structural hypotheses"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/how-experience-changes-a-unit/","@type":"WebPage","name":"How experience can change a unit","url":"https://causality.in/muon/how-experience-changes-a-unit/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"MUON","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"How experience can change a unit","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/how-experience-changes-a-unit/"}}]}]},"description":"Follow a road notice through an immediate answer, later recall, learning and a proposed change to the unit.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"text":"How experience can change a unit\nAt noon a road notice should change the next route. At four the road might reopen. A useful unit would need to use the first notice when it matters and stop relying on it once conditions change. MUON studies four ways a proposed unit might respond, from updating its current state to trying a different structure.\nRemembering a closure is different from learning a rule\nAsk for the same route with and without the noon notice. If the route changes when it should, the earlier observation has affected this answer. Sending the question to a different expert helps only if that expert’s computation can use the notice.\nStoring the observation solves another part of the problem: making it available later. It still needs to be retrieved at the right time and used well. Keeping a closure after the road reopens could make the answer worse. The useful question is what retaining it contributes to later work, including the cost of keeping and retrieving it.\nLearning across episodes changes how the unit behaves on future tasks. Repeated errors might motivate an update to its parameters so it handles similar notices differently later. If the unit’s structure cannot connect a notice to the affected route, changing parameters may be insufficient. A different structure becomes a candidate to test.\nChange\tWhat changes\tHow to tell whether it helps\t\nTemporal update\tThe state used for the current computation\tCheck whether an earlier cue changes the later answer appropriately\t\nRetention choice\tWhich observations remain available\tCompare later performance and storage or retrieval cost\t\nParameter learning\tLearned behaviour across episodes\tCheck lasting improvement and effects on existing capabilities\t\nStructural hypothesis\tThe unit’s proposed sparse organisation\tCompare the candidate with the existing structure on the failure it should address\t\nUse a separate comparison for each change. Saving the noon notice can help an afternoon request even if the model’s parameters stay the same. To test learning, try new cases after the update and check whether the unit still handles the ones it already got right.\nSurprise can direct attention without explaining the cause\nAn observation that differs from a prediction may deserve attention. The disagreement could come from noise, changed conditions, too few resources or a poor representation. Its size alone cannot distinguish those causes.\nMUON gives surprise three possible jobs: adjust a learning rate, contribute to a retention score, or trigger a structural hypothesis for separate evaluation. Learning rates and scores would have finite limits. The store would choose what to retain, and a proposed structure would not replace the current one until it had been tested.\nThe signals need interpretation before they can do any of this. A gradient describes how an error score changes when a component’s representation changes; its magnitude is one possible measure of sensitivity. A distributional divergence measures a difference between probability distributions, such as predicted activity with and without earlier context. The two measures answer different questions and can have very different numerical scales. Adding their raw values could let the larger-numbered one dominate for no useful reason.\nThe design therefore calls for calibrating each signal to a finite value on a common scale between zero and one. The calibration must specify its reference data, weights and limits, including what happens outside the conditions used to set it. Otherwise changing numerical representation could look like changing importance.\nThe surprise inquiry develops these mechanisms and their limits. Computation, state and learning places them within the broader unit design; the developer manual in the MUON repository explains the interface that would let a surrounding system use such a unit.\n","url":"https://causality.in/muon/how-experience-changes-a-unit/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"copyrightHolder":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}],"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:muon","name":"MUON","description":"Research into how a small reasoning unit uses past observations, learns and works with other units.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-muon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MUON","url":"https://causality.in/muon/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/computation-state-and-learning/#article","identifier":"content:muon-docs","name":"Computation, state and learning","url":"https://causality.in/muon/computation-state-and-learning/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/mentions/","identifier":"content:muon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MUON mentions","url":"https://causality.in/muon/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/paper/MUON.tex","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/paper/MUON.tex","name":"MUON: Micro-Unit Operating Network","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/muon/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/muon/overview","name":"MUON"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-muon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MUON","url":"https://causality.in/muon/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/mentions/","identifier":"content:muon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MUON mentions","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":7,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/quantum-comparison/#article","identifier":"content:q-muon-quantum-comparison","name":"Comparing a quantum unit with its classical counterpart","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/quantum-comparison/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/three-tests-for-a-reasoning-unit/#article","identifier":"content:muon-a-shared-interface-is-not-an-equal-model","name":"Three tests for a reasoning unit","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/three-tests-for-a-reasoning-unit/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/what-should-a-surprise-change/#article","identifier":"content:muon-surprise-is-a-question-not-a-verdict","name":"What should a surprise change?","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/what-should-a-surprise-change/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":4,"item":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/collections/systems/learning-from-a-recommendation/learning-from-a-judgement/#article","identifier":"content:counsel-judgement-learning","name":"What a judgment can learn from","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/collections/systems/learning-from-a-recommendation/learning-from-a-judgement/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":5,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/changing-the-rule/#article","identifier":"content:causality-change-the-rule-not-the-judge","name":"Change the rule without moving the finish line","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/changing-the-rule/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":6,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/the-intelligence-between-the-parts/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-the-intelligence-between-the-parts","name":"The intelligence between the parts","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/the-intelligence-between-the-parts/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":7,"item":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/collections/systems/making-a-working-world-intelligible/architecture/#article","identifier":"content:pale-architecture","name":"How Pale fits together","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/collections/systems/making-a-working-world-intelligible/architecture/"}}],"itemListOrder":"https://schema.org/ItemListOrderDescending"},"description":"Writing that discusses or references MUON across the connected publications.","text":"","url":"https://causality.in/muon/mentions/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:muon","name":"MUON","description":"Research into how a small reasoning unit uses past observations, learns and works with other units.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-muon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MUON","url":"https://causality.in/muon/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/computation-state-and-learning/#article","identifier":"content:muon-docs","name":"Computation, state and learning","url":"https://causality.in/muon/computation-state-and-learning/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/mentions/","identifier":"content:muon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MUON mentions","url":"https://causality.in/muon/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/paper/MUON.tex","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/paper/MUON.tex","name":"MUON: Micro-Unit Operating Network","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/muon/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/muon/overview","name":"MUON"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/privacy/","identifier":"content:causality-privacy","@type":"WebPage","name":"Privacy notice","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"description":"Website hosting, email contact, reading preferences and external reading assistance for RTEPL's editorial publications.","text":"Privacy notice\nRawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited (RTEPL) is responsible for deciding how personal data is used on rawthoughts.in, causality.in, sleepwalker.in and post-agi-economics.org, and on their subdomains, including the documentation at docs.rawthoughts.in and docs.causality.in. RTEPL publishes and operates these websites. This notice explains the information handled when you read these websites or use their contact and reading tools.\nVisiting the website\nGitHub Pages hosts these publications. Serving a page involves connection information, including your IP address and the page requested. GitHub logs visitors’ IP addresses for security, including when a visitor is not signed in. GitHub handles that information under its privacy statement and may process it outside India.\nMintlify hosts the documentation at docs.rawthoughts.in and docs.causality.in. Serving those pages involves the same connection information, which Mintlify handles under its privacy policy and may process outside India. Mintlify's own visitor telemetry is turned off for these sites. Mintlify also provides those two sites' search, page tools and display settings, under the same privacy policy; the in-browser search, assistant tools, display settings and passage links described below belong to the other websites.\nOn the other websites, search runs in your browser against each website's own index, so what you type is not sent to a search service. Your search is kept in the search page's address, so like any address it can reach GitHub with a page request, appear in your browser history and go with the address if you share it.\nIf your browser has an assistant that supports it, each page offers that assistant two read-only tools: the text of the page you are reading and the same in-browser search. They work on what the page already shows and send nothing to us; what the assistant does with what it reads follows its provider's terms.\nContacting us\nA contact link opens a new message in your own email application. The website does not receive or store the message as a form submission. If you choose to send it, your email service and the recipient's mail service handle the correspondence. Any professional work that follows has its own correspondence and agreement.\nFor privacy questions, a grievance or a request concerning your personal data, write to info@rtepl.co.in. Include enough information to identify the request, but do not send passwords or unnecessary identity documents. We respond within 30 days and may ask for proportionate verification before disclosing or changing personal data.\nReading preferences\nThe display settings remember your colour and motion choices in your browser on this device. You can change them in Settings or clear the website's stored data through your browser. These preferences are used to display the page; they stay in your browser and are not sent to us.\nAfter a page loads, your browser may request a few linked pages from the same site and keep copies on this device. These are ordinary page requests to the site's host. The browser can discard the copies, and clearing the site's stored data removes them.\nAsking about a passage\nWhen you select text and choose a reading assistant, the link opens that provider with the selected passage, nearby context, the section and article titles, the publication's name, and the article and publication addresses. The passage is limited to 4,096 characters and the nearby context to 1,024. Selecting text alone prepares the link in your browser; choosing the provider sends it there.\nThe assistant is an external service. The request is included in its link, so it can also appear in your browser history. The provider's own terms, privacy policy and your account settings govern what it does with the request and any conversation that follows. Reading the article does not require using an assistant.\nHow long information is kept\nWe keep no record of visits on our own systems; GitHub and Mintlify keep their connection logs for the periods their policies set. Correspondence you send is kept for as long as the matter it concerns needs it and as the law requires.\nSecurity and incidents\nThese websites hold no accounts and receive no form submissions. If a personal-data breach affects you, we inform you and the authorities as applicable law requires.\nYour choices\nFollowing another external link takes you to that service under its own data practices.\nYou may ask us about your data, request correction or deletion, withdraw consent or raise a concern using the contact above. Requests are handled under applicable law; information needed to comply with a legal obligation or resolve a dispute may need to be kept. Withdrawing consent does not undo processing that has already occurred lawfully.\nChanges to the website's data practices will be reflected in this notice. A new optional use that requires consent will ask for that consent rather than treating continued reading as agreement.\nLast reviewed on 22 November 2026 by RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited.\n","url":"https://causality.in/privacy/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]}},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/","identifier":"content:causality-project-q-hena","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-HENA","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"description":"Research into how quantum units share work, communicate and recover together.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"text":"Q-HENA\nWhat happens to a shared computation when one quantum unit has to start again? Q-HENA investigates how quantum units communicate, share memory and organise their work. Follow two units through recovery.\nQuantum units learning to work as a whole\nQ-MUON studies what one quantum unit can do. Q-HENA asks how several of those units route contributions, share memory through entanglement and learn how to divide a task. It carries forward HENA’s work on classical coordination and recovery while exploring different ways for units to interact.\nQuantum interaction carrying a collective task\nImagine a task that needs one joint operation from two units. The design has to account for what each unit prepares, when both can act and which measurements help answer the task.\nPrepare local contributions\nEach Q-MUON unit prepares its local input and state. A joint operation also needs enough capacity for both units at the same time.\nConnect the quantum units\nThe collective chooses a route and specifies how the units interact. If one unit has to restart, the collective needs to determine which preparation or interaction to repeat.\nRead the collective result\nThe measurements have to contribute to the answer. A comparison counts preparation, communication, measurement and repeated work alongside local computation.\nQuestions about a quantum collective\nAn improved answer could come from the quantum computation, from a better division of work, or from both. The comparison needs to distinguish them:\nQuestion\tKeep the same\tCompare\t\nWhat does quantum computation contribute?\tTask and organisation\tRelevant classical and quantum units\t\nWhat does the organisation contribute?\tTask and substrate\tLearned routes, an imposed hierarchy and simpler arrangements\t\nFollow each contribution through the units, channels and memory, counting preparation, measurement and repeated work. The collective-computation guide explains these comparisons alongside recovery. The classical architecture remains part of the programme.\nQuestions about Q-HENA\nHow do Q-MUON and Q-HENA differ?\nQ-MUON’s design concerns one quantum unit. Q-HENA’s design concerns how a group routes work, communicates, remembers and organises itself.\nWhy retain both HENA and Q-HENA?\nThey investigate collective intelligence on different substrates. HENA remains the classical architecture; Q-HENA explores what quantum channels, state and memory change without discarding the classical work. Q-HENA has not demonstrated a quantum advantage.\nWhy does recovery matter to the architecture?\nUnits may restore an exact state, return to behaviour equivalent under declared observations, or restart. A collective has to preserve the relationships its task needs across those differences, including preparation and work that must be repeated.\nWhere should I start?\nQuantum collective computation follows a joint operation between two units and what changes when one must restart. Developers can check the Rust crate in the Q-HENA repository, which re-exports its dependencies because quantum swarm coordination is not implemented yet.\n","url":"https://causality.in/q-hena/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:q-hena","name":"Q-HENA","description":"Research into how quantum units share work, communicate and recover together.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/","identifier":"content:causality-project-q-hena","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-HENA","url":"https://causality.in/q-hena/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/quantum-collective-computation/#article","identifier":"content:q-hena-docs","name":"Quantum collective computation","url":"https://causality.in/q-hena/quantum-collective-computation/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/mentions/","identifier":"content:q-hena-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-HENA mentions","url":"https://causality.in/q-hena/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/q-hena/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/q-hena/overview","name":"Q-HENA"}]}],"mentions":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:hena","name":"HENA","description":"A research project on how small reasoning units learn to solve a task together, the collective that the Causality programme works towards.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/","identifier":"content:causality-project-hena","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA","url":"https://causality.in/hena/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/#article","identifier":"content:hena-docs","name":"How units form a collective","url":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/","identifier":"content:hena-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA mentions","url":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","name":"HENA: Hierarchically Evolving Neural Automata","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","name":"HENA"}]},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:q-muon","name":"Q-MUON","description":"Research into a quantum reasoning unit that keeps MUON’s caller interface.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-q-muon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-MUON","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/the-quantum-local-unit/#article","identifier":"content:q-muon-docs","name":"The quantum local unit","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/the-quantum-local-unit/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/mentions/","identifier":"content:q-muon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-MUON mentions","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/q-muon/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/q-muon/overview","name":"Q-MUON"}]}],"hasPart":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/quantum-collective-computation/#article","identifier":"content:q-hena-docs","name":"Quantum collective computation","url":"https://causality.in/q-hena/quantum-collective-computation/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/mentions/","identifier":"content:q-hena-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-HENA mentions","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/recovery-across-a-changing-world/#article","identifier":"content:q-hena-recovery-is-a-claim","name":"Recovery across a changing world","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/recovery-across-a-changing-world/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/quantum-comparison/#article","identifier":"content:q-muon-quantum-comparison","name":"Comparing a quantum unit with its classical counterpart","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/quantum-comparison/"}}],"itemListOrder":"https://schema.org/ItemListOrderDescending"},"description":"Writing that discusses or references Q-HENA across the connected publications.","text":"","url":"https://causality.in/q-hena/mentions/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:q-hena","name":"Q-HENA","description":"Research into how quantum units share work, communicate and recover together.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/","identifier":"content:causality-project-q-hena","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-HENA","url":"https://causality.in/q-hena/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/quantum-collective-computation/#article","identifier":"content:q-hena-docs","name":"Quantum collective computation","url":"https://causality.in/q-hena/quantum-collective-computation/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/mentions/","identifier":"content:q-hena-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-HENA mentions","url":"https://causality.in/q-hena/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/q-hena/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/q-hena/overview","name":"Q-HENA"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/quantum-collective-computation/#article","identifier":"content:q-hena-docs","@type":"TechArticle","name":"Quantum collective computation","headline":"Quantum collective computation","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["quantum collective computation","quantum routing","entanglement-mediated memory","emergent hierarchy","swarm recovery"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/quantum-collective-computation/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Quantum collective computation","url":"https://causality.in/q-hena/quantum-collective-computation/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Q-HENA","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Quantum collective computation","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/quantum-collective-computation/"}}]}]},"description":"Follow a proposed joint operation between two quantum units and see what changes when one must restart.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"text":"Quantum collective computation\nSuppose two Q-MUON units need to perform one joint operation. Both would need capacity at the same time. If one unit had to start again, the collective would have to determine what work to repeat and which earlier observations it could still use. This design case illustrates Q-HENA, the proposal to explore HENA’s swarm question with quantum units.\nQ-HENA asks about the group, not one quantum unit\nQ-MUON concerns one unit’s computation. Q-HENA concerns which contributions reach each other, what the group remembers and how it organises a response. The joint operation illustrates why the group needs its own way to allocate resources and recover work.\nPale declares an organisation’s coordination explicitly where HENA asks how more of it could arise from intent, and Q-HENA asks what changes when that interaction involves quantum state, correlations and measurement; Causality’s About page sets out the comparison.\nQuantum communication and collective memory\nQ-HENA proposes quantum routing and communication, with memory mediated by entanglement. To turn that proposal into a mechanism, it must specify how state is prepared, which interaction occurs, what another unit can receive or measure and how the result contributes to an answer. Entanglement alone does not specify communication or storage.\nThe swarm would also keep operational records through Reckon’s record protocol. Those records would describe work and observations. They would not be copies of quantum state or proof that the state could be restored.\nTwo contributions to examine\nA comparison must separate the contribution of quantum computation from the contribution of organisation:\nQuestion\tHold fixed\tVary\t\nWhat does the substrate contribute?\tTask and organisation\tRelevant classical and quantum unit candidates\t\nWhat does the organisation contribute?\tTask and substrate\tLearned routing, an imposed hierarchy and simpler arrangements\t\nCount preparation, communication, measurement, memory, repeated interaction and failed attempts alongside local computation. Adding units is useful only if their interaction helps the task after those costs are counted. No quantum advantage has been demonstrated. The emergence inquiry develops that question.\nRecovery is collective too\nIn the joint operation, suppose A can resume from a checkpoint and B must start again. Recovering A’s local state would still leave an open question about the relationship between A and B. They might need to repeat their joint preparation or interaction. A change in the hardware’s calibration could also make earlier measurements unsuitable for the resumed run.\nQ-MUON’s proposed recovery modes distinguish restoring the declared state and results within stated tolerances, recovering equivalent behaviour under stated observations, and starting again. Their exact meanings and schema are still to be published. Q-HENA would need to establish which combinations can continue a collective task, and repeat preparation or interaction where necessary. Expired resource allocations must be obtained again.\nThe developer manual in the Q-HENA repository follows this problem through a proposed two-unit interaction. Recovery across a changing world explores why saved state alone is insufficient.\nQ-HENA stays alongside HENA\nQ-HENA would receive HENA’s classical progress and adapt it alongside Q-MUON. Both paths remain part of the programme. A quantum mechanism might help a particular task, lose its benefit once its full cost is counted, or leave the comparison inconclusive. Each result would inform the architecture without replacing the classical question.\nInspect the source\nThe repository contains a Rust crate that re-exports the MONAD, Q-MUON and Reckon kernels, and a draft paper; quantum swarm coordination is not implemented yet. Developers with the required source checkouts can check the crate in the Q-HENA repository.\n","url":"https://causality.in/q-hena/quantum-collective-computation/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"copyrightHolder":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}],"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:q-hena","name":"Q-HENA","description":"Research into how quantum units share work, communicate and recover together.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/","identifier":"content:causality-project-q-hena","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-HENA","url":"https://causality.in/q-hena/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/quantum-collective-computation/#article","identifier":"content:q-hena-docs","name":"Quantum collective computation","url":"https://causality.in/q-hena/quantum-collective-computation/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/mentions/","identifier":"content:q-hena-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-HENA mentions","url":"https://causality.in/q-hena/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/q-hena/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/q-hena/overview","name":"Q-HENA"}]}],"mentions":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:hena","name":"HENA","description":"A research project on how small reasoning units learn to solve a task together, the collective that the Causality programme works towards.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/","identifier":"content:causality-project-hena","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA","url":"https://causality.in/hena/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/#article","identifier":"content:hena-docs","name":"How units form a collective","url":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/","identifier":"content:hena-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA mentions","url":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","name":"HENA: Hierarchically Evolving Neural Automata","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","name":"HENA"}]},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:pale","name":"Pale","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux","description":"See which review instructions apply in a repository and what would change before you install them. Pale compiles those instructions and permissions from one workspace.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#brand"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale","targetProduct":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/","identifier":"content:pale-project","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Pale","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/understand-your-world/#article","identifier":"content:pale-docs","name":"Introduction to Pale","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/understand-your-world/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/mentions/","identifier":"content:pale-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Pale mentions","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.rawthoughts.in/pale/overview"}]},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:q-muon","name":"Q-MUON","description":"Research into a quantum reasoning unit that keeps MUON’s caller interface.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-q-muon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-MUON","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/the-quantum-local-unit/#article","identifier":"content:q-muon-docs","name":"The quantum local unit","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/the-quantum-local-unit/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/mentions/","identifier":"content:q-muon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-MUON mentions","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/q-muon/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/q-muon/overview","name":"Q-MUON"}]},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:reckon","name":"Reckon","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux","description":"Measure what memory changes for its cost, and compare proposed improvements before deciding to use them.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon","targetProduct":{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-reckon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Reckon","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/understand-the-memory-laboratory/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-docs","name":"Understand the memory laboratory","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/understand-the-memory-laboratory/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/mentions/","identifier":"content:reckon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Reckon mentions","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/reckon/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/reckon/overview","name":"Reckon"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/","identifier":"content:causality-project-q-hena","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-HENA","url":"https://causality.in/q-hena/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-q-muon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-MUON","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"description":"Research into a quantum reasoning unit that keeps MUON’s caller interface.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"text":"Q-MUON\nWhat could a quantum state let one reasoning unit do differently? Q-MUON investigates gates, entanglement and communication behind the same external interface as MUON, so preparation, computation and measurement can be compared as one complete task.\nThe same architectural question on another substrate\nThe state-overlap example follows two sensor readings from encoding to a similarity score. It shows which costs a comparison with a classical unit has to count. Q-HENA asks how units of this kind work together.\nFrom quantum state to a useful response\nA unit has to prepare an input, apply a circuit and measure an answer. All three steps count toward the cost of answering a task.\nPrepare the input\nThe candidate encodes a task input as a quantum state. The encoding determines which differences between inputs the unit can use.\nTransform the quantum state\nA circuit applies quantum gates using the chosen parameters. A comparison tests which gates or interactions improve the result.\nReturn an observable answer\nThe unit measures the circuit and returns the result through MUON’s interface. If an answer requires repeated preparations and measurements, each repetition adds to its cost.\nQuestions about Q-MUON\nWhat exists today?\nThe repository holds the fork of MUON, a kernel that builds on MONAD and a paper with an abstract. It has no quantum code; a classical candidate to mirror is the first problem in front.\nIs Q-MUON meant to replace MUON?\nBoth paths are permanent. MUON develops the classical unit and owns the interface. Q-MUON carries that work into the fork and adapts what runs behind it.\nWhere could quantum structure enter?\nCandidate directions include a quantum feature map used by a kernel, a parameterised circuit that learns an output law, and quantum communication between units.\nDoes one interface imply one kind of state?\nNo. Quantum state, measurement and recovery depend on the backend and calibration. Each unit declares what can be restored or repeated rather than inheriting a classical checkpoint promise.\nHow can I explore the design?\nThe guide follows a similarity calculation through the quantum unit and shows what a comparison with a classical unit has to measure. The substrate comparison develops those alternatives.\n","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:q-muon","name":"Q-MUON","description":"Research into a quantum reasoning unit that keeps MUON’s caller interface.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-q-muon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-MUON","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/the-quantum-local-unit/#article","identifier":"content:q-muon-docs","name":"The quantum local unit","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/the-quantum-local-unit/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/mentions/","identifier":"content:q-muon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-MUON mentions","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/q-muon/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/q-muon/overview","name":"Q-MUON"}]}],"mentions":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:hena","name":"HENA","description":"A research project on how small reasoning units learn to solve a task together, the collective that the Causality programme works towards.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/","identifier":"content:causality-project-hena","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA","url":"https://causality.in/hena/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/#article","identifier":"content:hena-docs","name":"How units form a collective","url":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/","identifier":"content:hena-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA mentions","url":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","name":"HENA: Hierarchically Evolving Neural Automata","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","name":"HENA"}]},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:muon","name":"MUON","description":"Research into how a small reasoning unit uses past observations, learns and works with other units.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-muon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MUON","url":"https://causality.in/muon/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/computation-state-and-learning/#article","identifier":"content:muon-docs","name":"Computation, state and learning","url":"https://causality.in/muon/computation-state-and-learning/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/mentions/","identifier":"content:muon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MUON mentions","url":"https://causality.in/muon/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/paper/MUON.tex","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/paper/MUON.tex","name":"MUON: Micro-Unit Operating Network","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/muon/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/muon/overview","name":"MUON"}]},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:q-hena","name":"Q-HENA","description":"Research into how quantum units share work, communicate and recover together.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/","identifier":"content:causality-project-q-hena","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-HENA","url":"https://causality.in/q-hena/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/quantum-collective-computation/#article","identifier":"content:q-hena-docs","name":"Quantum collective computation","url":"https://causality.in/q-hena/quantum-collective-computation/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/mentions/","identifier":"content:q-hena-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-HENA mentions","url":"https://causality.in/q-hena/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/q-hena/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/q-hena/overview","name":"Q-HENA"}]}],"hasPart":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/the-quantum-local-unit/#article","identifier":"content:q-muon-docs","name":"The quantum local unit","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/the-quantum-local-unit/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/mentions/","identifier":"content:q-muon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-MUON mentions","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/recovery-across-a-changing-world/#article","identifier":"content:q-hena-recovery-is-a-claim","name":"Recovery across a changing world","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/recovery-across-a-changing-world/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/quantum-comparison/#article","identifier":"content:q-muon-quantum-comparison","name":"Comparing a quantum unit with its classical counterpart","url":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/quantum-comparison/"}}],"itemListOrder":"https://schema.org/ItemListOrderDescending"},"description":"Writing that discusses or references Q-MUON across the connected publications.","text":"","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/mentions/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:q-muon","name":"Q-MUON","description":"Research into a quantum reasoning unit that keeps MUON’s caller interface.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-q-muon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-MUON","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/the-quantum-local-unit/#article","identifier":"content:q-muon-docs","name":"The quantum local unit","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/the-quantum-local-unit/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/mentions/","identifier":"content:q-muon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-MUON mentions","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/q-muon/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/q-muon/overview","name":"Q-MUON"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/the-quantum-local-unit/#article","identifier":"content:q-muon-docs","@type":"TechArticle","name":"The quantum local unit","headline":"The quantum local unit","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["quantum local units","state preparation and readout","quantum checkpoint compatibility","complete quantum resource costs"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/the-quantum-local-unit/","@type":"WebPage","name":"The quantum local unit","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/the-quantum-local-unit/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Q-MUON","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"The quantum local unit","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/the-quantum-local-unit/"}}]}]},"description":"Follow a proposed quantum similarity calculation and see what a comparison with a classical unit would need to measure.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"text":"The quantum local unit\nGive a reasoning unit two sensor readings and ask how similar they are. Q-MUON asks whether a quantum processor could do a task like this behind the same interface as a classical MUON unit. Q-HENA would connect multiple units into a quantum swarm.\nA local unit on a quantum substrate\nFor the sensor task, a quantum feature map could encode each reading as a state. A circuit could estimate the overlap between those states through repeated measurements, giving a classical learner a similarity score. A fair comparison counts preparing both states, running the circuit and repeating the measurements.\nThat example uses a kernel method. Other tasks could use a circuit with adjustable gates. Training would tune those gates against an objective measured from the circuit’s outputs. Quantum communication could let units exchange quantum states. Each is a candidate for research.\nWhy a quantum unit keeps MUON's interface\nMUON’s draft interface covers inference, state, ordered records, resources and lifecycle operations. Keeping that interface would let a caller use a quantum unit without managing its gates. The developer manual in the MUON repository explains the five responsibilities.\nA caller could receive the same kind of similarity score from either unit. It still needs to know whether that score helps on the task and whether it can swap in the new unit without disrupting its work. Conformance, adequacy and substitution explains those checks.\nWhat would make a quantum unit worth using?\nCompare a quantum candidate with a relevant classical alternative on the same task and at the same error tolerance. Count preparation, training, repeated measurement, noise, communication and recovery. A faster circuit can still produce a slower or more expensive answer.\nFor the sensor example, test whether the measured similarity helps the learner answer its task. If the candidate instead uses adjustable gates, first establish whether it can learn from the task objective. Then compare results and costs with a classical implementation. The substrate comparison develops those alternatives.\nState and recovery need their own meaning\nSuppose the sensor comparison stops halfway through. What would restarting the unit let you recover: its state, equivalent behaviour on the questions you can test, or only a new attempt? The proposed modes name those possibilities exact, semantic and restart_only.\nA saved state format cannot answer that question alone. The backend and device topology matter, as do its calibration or noise model. So do the way the circuit was compiled and measured, the seed, the number of repeated measurements (shots) and the kind of replay being promised. A unit’s compatibility profile would identify these choices and the recovery mode it supports.\nNote\nThe recovery modes’ precise guarantees, schema and tests remain undefined. A classical checkpoint cannot become a quantum checkpoint because its API has the same shape. A calibration change or new backend can also invalidate a recovery promise.\nQ-HENA would need to respect each unit’s mode when recovering the swarm. Recovery across a changing world explains that collective problem.\nMUON and Q-MUON stay side by side\nMUON remains the classical path and owns the shared interface. Q-MUON would start from the corresponding MUON work before adapting it to a quantum backend. The programme keeps both paths for comparison.\nInspect the source\nWith source access, a developer can check the Rust crate, which re-exports MONAD because the quantum unit is not implemented yet. The developer manual in the Q-MUON repository explains the check and the proposed interface.\n","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/the-quantum-local-unit/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"copyrightHolder":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}],"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:q-muon","name":"Q-MUON","description":"Research into a quantum reasoning unit that keeps MUON’s caller interface.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-q-muon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-MUON","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/the-quantum-local-unit/#article","identifier":"content:q-muon-docs","name":"The quantum local unit","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/the-quantum-local-unit/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/mentions/","identifier":"content:q-muon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-MUON mentions","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/q-muon/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/q-muon/overview","name":"Q-MUON"}]}],"mentions":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:muon","name":"MUON","description":"Research into how a small reasoning unit uses past observations, learns and works with other units.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-muon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MUON","url":"https://causality.in/muon/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/computation-state-and-learning/#article","identifier":"content:muon-docs","name":"Computation, state and learning","url":"https://causality.in/muon/computation-state-and-learning/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/mentions/","identifier":"content:muon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MUON mentions","url":"https://causality.in/muon/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/paper/MUON.tex","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/MUON/blob/main/paper/MUON.tex","name":"MUON: Micro-Unit Operating Network","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/muon/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/muon/overview","name":"MUON"}]},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:q-hena","name":"Q-HENA","description":"Research into how quantum units share work, communicate and recover together.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/","identifier":"content:causality-project-q-hena","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-HENA","url":"https://causality.in/q-hena/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/quantum-collective-computation/#article","identifier":"content:q-hena-docs","name":"Quantum collective computation","url":"https://causality.in/q-hena/quantum-collective-computation/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/mentions/","identifier":"content:q-hena-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-HENA mentions","url":"https://causality.in/q-hena/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/q-hena/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/q-hena/overview","name":"Q-HENA"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-q-muon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Q-MUON","url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-reckon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Reckon","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"description":"Measure what memory changes for its cost, and compare proposed improvements before deciding to use them.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"text":"Reckon\nWhat should an agent keep when it has room for only a few records? Reckon measures what changes outcomes for what it costs, with the uncertainty shown. The laboratory runs locally for Causality’s research. The problem in front is serving RawThoughts’ agent tools with the same comparison, so a proposed improvement can be compared before anyone decides to use it. The cases for that comparison come from Pale's genealogy, its history of what each agent was given, what it did and what followed; the laboratory here runs without it.\nUse the local laboratory to compare two replay budgets or inspect a saved run.\nKeep useful experience. Understand what it changed.\nAn early clue can matter several steps later. Run the same task with different memory rules or limits, then follow the surviving records into the outcome.\nTo find out whether one record helped, compare runs with that instance present and absent. Report the cost of finding out alongside the outcome difference. The comparison tests one change under stated conditions; a causal claim also needs the assumptions that support that interpretation.\nFollow what a memory changes\nGive the same classifier room for 2 examples or 16. Compare its accuracy on earlier tasks, then investigate which memory choices made a difference.\nFollow the record\nFollow a record from when it was written to when it was read, scored or removed. Use the saved result to see how the task performed.\nChange the memory\nChange the space available, the retention rule or the presence of one record. Keep the other conditions fixed so the comparison answers the question you chose.\nCompare the useful result\nCompare accuracy or reward for each pair. When you test a policy or a record, report the uncertainty and what it cost to measure the difference, as well as the task's memory use.\nQuestions about Reckon\nHow do Reckon’s wider roles relate to the laboratory?\nA correction can become a case for testing a better method. Reckon connects the case, comparison and owner's decision while keeping ordinary evaluation useful on its own. Cases, evaluations and decisions follows that wider use.\nWhat does the laboratory show?\nIt is for finding which retained records mattered and what keeping them cost: the saved result gives accuracy or reward, and the log shows which memory operations wrote, read or dropped records. The laboratory guide explains how to follow those records and test a particular contribution.\nIs every contribution score causal?\nA causal claim needs a test that changes the record as intended, states the assumptions and keeps the other conditions fixed. Use a score to help choose what to keep, and use an outcome comparison to test what a record did. The cache example works through that reasoning.\nDoes Reckon own HENA’s or Scope’s memory?\nHENA keeps its own swarm store and decides what to keep; Scope keeps only context derived from what Pale and Weave hold. Reckon runs a separate lab. Its wider services concern experiment inputs, results and decisions.\nHow do I run the laboratory?\nThe lab runs on your own machine. Start with the laboratory guide, then compare two replay budgets.\n","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:reckon","name":"Reckon","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux","description":"Measure what memory changes for its cost, and compare proposed improvements before deciding to use them.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon","targetProduct":{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-reckon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Reckon","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/understand-the-memory-laboratory/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-docs","name":"Understand the memory laboratory","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/understand-the-memory-laboratory/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/mentions/","identifier":"content:reckon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Reckon mentions","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/reckon/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/reckon/overview","name":"Reckon"}]}],"mentions":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:hena","name":"HENA","description":"A research project on how small reasoning units learn to solve a task together, the collective that the Causality programme works towards.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/","identifier":"content:causality-project-hena","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA","url":"https://causality.in/hena/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/#article","identifier":"content:hena-docs","name":"How units form a collective","url":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/","identifier":"content:hena-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA mentions","url":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","name":"HENA: Hierarchically Evolving Neural Automata","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","name":"HENA"}]},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:scope","name":"Scope","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux","description":"The smallest fresh account of a task, with its sources, decisions, people and open questions intact.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#brand"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/scope","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/scope","targetProduct":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/scope/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/scope/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/","identifier":"content:scope-introduction","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Scope","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/context-for-a-task/#article","identifier":"content:scope-docs","name":"Context for a task","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/context-for-a-task/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/mentions/","identifier":"content:scope-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Scope mentions","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.rawthoughts.in/scope/overview"}]}],"hasPart":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/cases-evaluations-and-decisions/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-cases-evaluations-and-decisions","name":"Cases, evaluations and decisions","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/cases-evaluations-and-decisions/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/compare-replay-budgets/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-compare-replay-budgets","name":"Compare two replay budgets","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/compare-replay-budgets/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/understand-the-memory-laboratory/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-docs","name":"Understand the memory laboratory","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/understand-the-memory-laboratory/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/inspect-an-exported-run/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-inspect-an-exported-run","name":"Inspect an exported run","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/inspect-an-exported-run/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/cases-evaluations-and-decisions/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-cases-evaluations-and-decisions","@type":"Article","name":"Cases, evaluations and decisions","headline":"Cases, evaluations and decisions","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["curated experiment cases","reproducible evaluation","decision records","experiment reuse"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/cases-evaluations-and-decisions/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Cases, evaluations and decisions","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/cases-evaluations-and-decisions/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Reckon","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Cases, evaluations and decisions","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/cases-evaluations-and-decisions/"}}]}]},"description":"Follow two teams comparing a memory rule, from a shared test case to deciding whether to use it.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"text":"Cases, evaluations and decisions\nImagine two teams testing whether a memory rule helps an agent remember an early clue. They need the same task inputs, results they can compare and a record of who decided whether to use the rule. The laboratory runs the comparison today; the repository holds no service yet for sharing cases, evaluations or decisions between teams, and Reckon's design gives each of those jobs its own service. For a worked comparison, compare two replay budgets in the lab.\nReuse the same case\nIn the design, the two teams name one version of the clue sequence and its expected outcomes. The curator keeps where it came from and how it may be used. A correction gets a new version, leaving the earlier one available to explain past results. HENA keeps its own live records and decides how to use them; Scope's context stays derived from what Pale and Weave hold, and a curated case changes none of it.\nEvaluate a specified comparison\nThe experiment owner asks which memory rule works better on the delayed-cue task, with the task, inputs and budget specified. The evaluator reports what changed, the uncertainty, the cost and what another team needs to repeat the comparison.\nUse that result to judge the tested rule in that task. If the reward changes during a bandit task, test the rule on that task separately. If the evaluator changes between runs, account for that before comparing the numbers. The experiment owner decides whether to use the candidate.\nPreserve a decision\nIf the owner accepts, rejects or defers the candidate, the record identifies the version they decided on, confirms who decided and preserves the results they used. The artifact's owner still controls release, and the system running it still decides whether to activate it.\nAn ordinary evaluation can stand on its own. Promoting a learned change needs the case, comparison and owner's decision together, followed by the artifact owner's release checks and the caller's choice to use it. Applying a correction to today's work does not have to wait for that learning process.\nA correction from everyday work can supply a case: what information was available, what the agent did, what the person changed and what happened later. Permission to reuse those sources matters. The correction tells us what someone wanted; the later outcome helps test whether it worked. The same cases can inform comparisons of tools, skills, policies or a successor to Counsel's local judge.\nThe developer manual covers the implemented lab.\n","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/cases-evaluations-and-decisions/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"copyrightHolder":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}],"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:reckon","name":"Reckon","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux","description":"Measure what memory changes for its cost, and compare proposed improvements before deciding to use them.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon","targetProduct":{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-reckon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Reckon","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/understand-the-memory-laboratory/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-docs","name":"Understand the memory laboratory","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/understand-the-memory-laboratory/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/mentions/","identifier":"content:reckon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Reckon mentions","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/reckon/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/reckon/overview","name":"Reckon"}]}],"mentions":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:hena","name":"HENA","description":"A research project on how small reasoning units learn to solve a task together, the collective that the Causality programme works towards.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/","identifier":"content:causality-project-hena","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA","url":"https://causality.in/hena/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/#article","identifier":"content:hena-docs","name":"How units form a collective","url":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/","identifier":"content:hena-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA mentions","url":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","name":"HENA: Hierarchically Evolving Neural Automata","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","name":"HENA"}]},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:scope","name":"Scope","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux","description":"The smallest fresh account of a task, with its sources, decisions, people and open questions intact.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#brand"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/scope","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/scope","targetProduct":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/scope/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/scope/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/","identifier":"content:scope-introduction","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Scope","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/context-for-a-task/#article","identifier":"content:scope-docs","name":"Context for a task","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/context-for-a-task/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/mentions/","identifier":"content:scope-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Scope mentions","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.rawthoughts.in/scope/overview"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-reckon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Reckon","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/compare-replay-budgets/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-compare-replay-budgets","@type":"TechArticle","name":"Compare two replay budgets","headline":"Compare two replay budgets","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["replay buffer budgets","continual classification","paired seeds","accuracy matrices","memory event logs"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/compare-replay-budgets/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Compare two replay budgets","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/compare-replay-budgets/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Reckon","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Compare two replay budgets","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/compare-replay-budgets/"}}]}]},"description":"Follow a local continual-classification comparison from matched inputs through saved accuracy matrices and event logs to their interpretation.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"text":"Compare two replay budgets\nDoes a classifier remember earlier tasks better when it can keep 16 examples for replay instead of 2? Run both allowances on the same starting data for each of three seeds. The script prints an accuracy difference for each pair and saves the results and memory activity from all six runs.\nImportant\nPrepare the local environment. Use the reckon checkout and a Python environment with the Rust extension installed. If you need one, follow Set up the environment. You can run the comparison without a model service, notebook runner or daemon.\nSet up the comparison\nThe classifier learns three two-dimensional problems in sequence. The line that divides the classes rotates from task to task. The classifier stores examples it gets wrong and sometimes uses a stored example for an extra update as training continues.\nSetting\tValue\t\nTraining and test data\t60 training and 60 test samples per task\t\nLearner\tTwo features; learning rate 0.1; replay probability 0.2\t\nMemory\t2 or 16 slots, both with 50,000 bytes\t\nSeeds\t41, 42 and 43, paired across allowances\t\nRun length\tOne episode per saved run\t\nRetention\tExplicit FIFO in both runs of each pair\t\nThe task generates training and test data before learning starts. That makes the data match within each seed. Later replay can differ because the buffers hold different examples. FIFO removes the oldest retained examples when either allowance is exceeded. Keep that policy and the byte limit fixed to compare slot allowances.\nOne episode here means one complete sequence of three classification tasks. Reckon can carry records, scores and retained events into later episodes; this small example uses one sequence so each accuracy matrix describes the same three problems. The larger memory-policy comparison also varies the retention rule and reports the cost of measuring record effects.\nSave and run the script\nWith the Python environment active, save this as compare_replay_budgets.py in a folder for your results. Run it from that folder. If replay-budget-comparison already exists there, use a new folder or change the output name in the script.\nimport csv\nfrom dataclasses import asdict\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\nimport hashlib\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom time import perf_counter\n\nfrom reckon import CausalMemoryManager, MemoryBudget\nfrom reckon.memory.policies import FIFOPolicy\nfrom reckon.tasks.task_c_continual import ContinualConfig, ContinualTask\n\noutput = Path(\"replay-budget-comparison\")\noutput.mkdir()\ntask_config = ContinualConfig(\n    num_tasks=3,\n    samples_per_task=60,\n    test_samples_per_task=60,\n    feature_dim=2,\n    replay_fraction=0.2,\n    learning_rate=0.1,\n)\nrows = []\n\nfor seed in (41, 42, 43):\n    accuracy_by_slots = {}\n    for slots in (2, 16):\n        run = output / f\"replay-s{slots}-seed{seed}\"\n        (run / \"logs\").mkdir(parents=True)\n        budget = MemoryBudget(max_slots=slots, max_bytes=50_000)\n        memory = CausalMemoryManager(budget, retention_policy=FIFOPolicy())\n        config = {\n            \"task_name\": \"continual\",\n            \"task\": asdict(task_config),\n            \"budget\": asdict(budget),\n            \"policy_name\": type(memory.retention_policy).__name__,\n            \"seed\": seed,\n            \"num_episodes\": 1,\n            \"reset_memory\": False,\n            \"learn_utility\": True,\n        }\n        config_text = json.dumps(config, sort_keys=True, indent=2)\n        (run / \"config.json\").write_text(config_text + \"\\n\")\n        started_at = datetime.now(timezone.utc)\n        started = perf_counter()\n        result = ContinualTask(task_config).run(\n            memory, num_episodes=1, seed=seed,\n            reset_memory=False, learn_utility=True,\n        )\n        wall_time_ms = (perf_counter() - started) * 1000\n        result_data = result.to_dict()\n        (run / \"result.json\").write_text(json.dumps(result_data, indent=2) + \"\\n\")\n        (run / \"logs\" / \"events.jsonl\").write_text(memory.get_event_log_jsonl())\n        episode = result_data[\"raw_data\"][\"episodes\"][0]\n        metrics = {\n            \"mean_accuracy\": result_data[\"performance\"][\"mean_accuracy\"],\n            \"forgetting\": result_data[\"performance\"][\"forgetting\"],\n            \"total_writes\": episode[\"total_writes\"],\n            \"total_reads\": episode[\"total_reads\"],\n        }\n        summary = {\n            \"config_id\": hashlib.sha256(config_text.encode()).hexdigest(),\n            \"notebook_name\": \"replay-budget-comparison\",\n            \"task_name\": \"continual\",\n            \"started_at_ms\": int(started_at.timestamp() * 1000),\n            \"policy_name\": config[\"policy_name\"],\n            \"num_episodes\": 1,\n            \"budget_slots\": slots,\n            \"config\": config,\n            \"metrics\": metrics,\n            \"wall_time_ms\": wall_time_ms,\n        }\n        (run / \"summary.json\").write_text(json.dumps(summary, indent=2) + \"\\n\")\n        rows.append({\"seed\": seed, \"slots\": slots, **metrics})\n        accuracy_by_slots[slots] = metrics[\"mean_accuracy\"]\n        print(run)\n    difference = accuracy_by_slots[16] - accuracy_by_slots[2]\n    print(f\"Seed {seed}: 16 minus 2 slots = {100 * difference:+.2f} percentage points\")\n\nwith (output / \"comparison.csv\").open(\"w\", newline=\"\") as handle:\n    writer = csv.DictWriter(handle, fieldnames=list(rows[0]))\n    writer.writeheader()\n    writer.writerows(rows)\n\nRun the script:\npython compare_replay_budgets.py\n\nLook for six run directories, three paired differences and comparison.csv, which has one row per run. Open any run to find:\nFile\tContents\t\nconfig.json\tTask settings, budget, policy and seed\t\nresult.json\tAccuracy matrix and task results\t\nlogs/events.jsonl\tRecorded memory operations\t\nsummary.json\tMetadata used by the debugger importer\t\nIf the script stops early, treat the comparison as incomplete and start again in a fresh folder. A missing row does not count as zero accuracy.\nTo repeat a run later, record the Reckon revision, dependency lockfiles, local edits, Python packages, Rust toolchain and extension build with the results. Seeds tell you which data to generate; they do not identify the implementation that uses it. The reproducibility guide covers the rest. wall_time_ms measures the task call, excluding setup, imports and file writes.\nRead the accuracy matrix\nStart with the paired differences. A positive number means 16 slots had higher final mean accuracy for that seed. Running this example on 15 November 2026 with Reckon revision 3d3aea6 gave:\nSeed\t2 slots\t16 slots\tDifference in percentage points\t\n41\t81.11%\t82.78%\t+1.67\t\n42\t82.22%\t80.00%\t−2.22\t\n43\t81.11%\t83.89%\t+2.78\t\nThe current runner uses separate random streams for task data and replay, and gives each training position a replay opportunity. Your results may therefore differ from these saved numbers. Read each pair in your comparison.csv before combining them. In the saved comparison, the larger buffer improved two seeds and worsened one. Three seeds describe this example; they do not establish which allowance generally performs better.\nOpen result.json. In raw_data.episodes[0].accuracy_matrix[i][j], the row tells you which training task just finished and the column tells you which task was tested:\nA diagonal entry shows accuracy just after learning that task.\nThe final row shows accuracy after learning all three tasks.\nZeros above the diagonal mean “not evaluated yet.”\nmean_accuracy averages the final row. Compare the final rows from your two runs to see which tasks gained or lost accuracy. In the saved seed 42 run above, most of the drop came from the first task.\nforgetting compares each earlier task's first score on the diagonal with its score in the final row. Count drops only, treating gains as zero, and leave out the last task. An intermediate score higher than the diagonal score does not enter this calculation.\nTwo fields in the saved example have older names. In those original runs, causal_precision is one minus forgetting and reward_per_slot is final mean accuracy divided by counted writes, with a denominator of at least one. Current runs name these diagnostics.one_minus_mean_forgetting and diagnostics.mean_episode_accuracy_per_write; the latter averages each episode’s accuracy divided by its write count, with the same minimum denominator. This example runs one episode per configuration, so that average has one term. See the metrics reference for definitions. The three seeds give an exploratory comparison of this task and learner. To measure one record's contribution, run a separate comparison that changes whether that record is available.\nFollow the memory activity\nOpen a run's logs/events.jsonl beside its result. Find op_type to see what happened. Copy a record ID from a write and look for it in a later read's details or in a pruning entry. For a write, bytes_after shows how many stored bytes counted toward the budget after that write. Use the result matrix for accuracy and the log for when those operations happened.\nThe log lacks full record payloads, classifier state and accuracy at each step.\nCaution\nA full log buffer discards earlier events. Its buffer holds at most 10,000 events. Carried episodes keep the retained events; an explicit memory reset starts a new buffer. For a longer study, consume the manager's event subscription while it runs so a full buffer does not discard the beginning. Those diagnostic copies consume memory outside the task's allowance.\nOpen a saved run in the debugger\nYou can read the results without a debugger. To view the activity there, follow Inspect an exported run to start the two servers. Run these commands from the folder that contains replay-budget-comparison:\nreckon viz import \"replay-budget-comparison/replay-s2-seed41\"\nreckon viz import \"replay-budget-comparison/replay-s16-seed41\"\n\nThe importer checks that the events can be projected for display before saving the run. The timeline preserves numeric event identities and their sequence, so operations at the same logical step remain distinct. Use it to compare writes, reads and removals; the raw log cannot supply full record snapshots or reconstruct the classifier.\nNote\nSuccess unavailable is not zero accuracy. The run list's success_rate is a separate field that this task does not supply. Current imports keep it unavailable (null), and the debugger shows “Success unavailable.” Read mean_accuracy from result.json for this classification task.\nIf import is refused, keep the original files and correct the reported format or identity problem; a missing display does not change the measured result. Stop both servers when you finish.\n","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/compare-replay-budgets/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"copyrightHolder":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}],"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:reckon","name":"Reckon","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux","description":"Measure what memory changes for its cost, and compare proposed improvements before deciding to use them.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon","targetProduct":{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-reckon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Reckon","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/understand-the-memory-laboratory/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-docs","name":"Understand the memory laboratory","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/understand-the-memory-laboratory/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/mentions/","identifier":"content:reckon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Reckon mentions","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/reckon/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/reckon/overview","name":"Reckon"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-reckon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Reckon","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/inspect-an-exported-run/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-inspect-an-exported-run","@type":"TechArticle","name":"Inspect an exported run","headline":"Inspect an exported run","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["event-log import","reckon debugger","memory activity","run inspection"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/inspect-an-exported-run/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Inspect an exported run","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/inspect-an-exported-run/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Reckon","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Inspect an exported run","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/inspect-an-exported-run/"}}]}]},"description":"Follow a record through writes, reads and removals in a saved event log.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"text":"Inspect an exported run\nPick one record in a saved run and see where it was written, read or removed. You can do this from the saved files without running the task again. The debugger can show more when the export contains full record snapshots.\nChoose a run\nFind the run folder that contains logs/events.jsonl. Notebook runs normally live under notebooks/runs/. If you followed Compare two replay budgets, use replay-s2-seed41 from your results. The importer needs the event file, even if you already have figures and scores. It copies the run into the debugger's local storage. Keep the original files for checking what the debugger shows.\nFollow a record\nIn the replay example, pick one stored example. Open logs/events.jsonl in a text editor, find a write and copy its record_id. Search below it for that ID in a later read's details, a score update or a removal.\nUse richer exports to see what a record contained and how it relates to other records. A read tells you that a record was returned. Showing that it helped requires a separate outcome comparison. For a measured connection between two records, supply the probe result for that pair. The debugger displays the result after you run the probe elsewhere.\nThe key-and-door example\nThe built-in key-and-door task gives a small example to follow. Its grid has a start (S), a place to see the key colour (K), two doors (L and R) and a goal (G). The # cells are walls:\nS . . . .\n. . K . .\n. . . . .\n# L # R #\n. . G . .\n\nAt K, the controller writes the observed colour as a cue record. Before choosing a door, it asks for that episode's record by ID.\nThe saved example in notebooks/runs/2026-11-16/keydoor_export_demo/95585cb0799e/ ran three episodes on 16 November 2026 with seed 42, FIFO, one slot and 50,000 bytes. It kept memory between episodes and enabled utility learning. Its result.json records a green-cue failure followed by two blue-cue successes. Each episode wrote and retrieved its cue once. The log contains 11 events: three writes, three reads, three utility updates and two removals.\nFollow the first blue cue, in keydoor_ep1, by searching for record b4b5c6bb-df57-4f6f-ac60-da08406783e8:\nEvent ID\tLogical step\tWhat the log records\t\n4\t24\tThe cue is written\t\n5\t26\tA read returns its ID in details\t\n6\t29\tUtility changes from 0 to 0.1 after the successful episode\t\n7\t33\tFIFO removes it when the next episode writes its cue\t\nThese steps are logical positions, not elapsed times. Read the cue colour and outcome from result.json: the raw log contains neither payloads nor a grid trajectory.\nThe controller chooses right for a retrieved blue cue and otherwise guesses left, without trying a second door. Red opens the left door, so it can succeed without retrieval. Green opens a seeded random door: in this run the right door was open, but remembering green still led to the left-hand guess. A stored and retrieved cue therefore need not produce success; success alone does not show that a cue helped.\nThis task writes one cue and reads it before the next write. With enough bytes for that cue, one slot is sufficient for this controller's memory path. It does not create competing records before the choice, so it cannot by itself show which retention ranking handles that competition better. The task contract explains those limits; the replay comparison exercises a different workload.\nStart the debugger\nThis step is optional. Importing the replay example exposes its recorded writes, reads, removals and score updates on the timeline. Numeric event IDs remain distinct, including operations at the same logical step. A raw log supplies activity rather than full record snapshots; the timeline reference explains what each export can show.\nUse the reckon checkout, a working Python environment with the Rust extension, Node.js 18 or later and pnpm 9. With that environment active, run this from the reckon root to add the Python debugger dependencies:\npython -m pip install \".[viz]\"\n\nStart both servers from the reckon checkout. Keep the project Python environment active in the first and third terminals.\nIn the first terminal, start the backend:\npython -m reckon.viz.server\n\nIn a second terminal, install the interface dependencies, build the shared components and start the interface. If the dependencies and build are already current, run just pnpm dev from viz-ui:\ncd viz-ui\npnpm install\npnpm build:components\npnpm dev\n\nIn a third terminal, import the run using its full path. Use the results folder you actually chose, even if it is outside the checkout:\nreckon viz import \"/absolute/path/to/your-run\"\n\nOpen http://localhost:5173 in your browser and select the imported run. The interface on port 5173 uses the backend on port 8000. If no runs appear, check that both terminals are still running.\nTo follow the key-and-door example in the interface, import its saved directory instead and select that run. The heading shows 3 episodes | 67% success. Start at event 0 and use Next event to reach the blue cue’s four events above; operations sharing a logical step remain separately selectable. Open Source operation to compare each complete log entry with logs/events.jsonl.\nA successful import prints Imported run: and an event count. If it fails:\nMessage or situation\tWhat to check\t\nPath not found\tCheck the full path and folder name. If you used a relative path, check the terminal's working directory too\t\nNo events.jsonl found\tThe producer actually saved an event log\t\nRun identity already exists\tInspect the existing import, or choose a new identity\t\nCannot display run\tRead the reported format or identity error and correct the export; keep its original bytes for diagnosis\t\nThe default run ID is the last part of the directory path. To keep another copy, give it a new ID:\nreckon viz import \"/absolute/path/to/your-run\" --run-id inspected-copy\n\nCompare outcomes, not just activity\nNote\nActivity is not accuracy. The debugger's default performance axis divides writes by writes plus removals and labels that as an operation proxy. It describes memory activity, not task success. Open the saved task result for accuracy or reward. Missing measurements remain unavailable; the event log alone cannot supply probe cost or accuracy.\nWhen measured points are supplied, the frontier retains a point unless another uses no more cost and achieves at least as much performance, with one strict improvement. Exact duplicates remain visible, and overruns expand the budget axis beyond 100%. The plot does not choose a policy or calculate uncertainty across repeated trials. For adding measured points and declaring their units, see the frontier reference.\nThe laboratory guide explains what you can infer from a write, a read or an outcome difference.\nStop the debugger\nPress Ctrl+C in each server terminal when finished. Closing the browser tab does not stop either process. Your saved export and imported copy remain available.\n","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/inspect-an-exported-run/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"copyrightHolder":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}],"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:reckon","name":"Reckon","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux","description":"Measure what memory changes for its cost, and compare proposed improvements before deciding to use them.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon","targetProduct":{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-reckon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Reckon","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/understand-the-memory-laboratory/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-docs","name":"Understand the memory laboratory","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/understand-the-memory-laboratory/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/mentions/","identifier":"content:reckon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Reckon mentions","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/reckon/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/reckon/overview","name":"Reckon"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-reckon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Reckon","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/mentions/","identifier":"content:reckon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Reckon mentions","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":9,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/what-did-selective-memory-buy/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-memory-comparison","name":"What did selective memory buy?","url":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/what-did-selective-memory-buy/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/ema-vs-bayesian-updates/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-ema-vs-bayesian-updates","name":"EMA and Bayesian updates for online scoring","url":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/ema-vs-bayesian-updates/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/four-questions-for-an-evaluation/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-four-questions-for-an-evaluation","name":"Four questions behind a benchmark result","url":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/four-questions-for-an-evaluation/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":4,"item":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/collections/systems/making-a-working-world-intelligible/improving-the-system-between-runs/#article","identifier":"content:pale-learning-comparison","name":"Improving the system between runs","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/collections/systems/making-a-working-world-intelligible/improving-the-system-between-runs/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":5,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/what-i-want-a-memory-to-do/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-what-i-want-a-memory-to-do","name":"What I want a memory to do","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/what-i-want-a-memory-to-do/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":6,"item":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/collections/systems/making-a-working-world-intelligible/architecture/#article","identifier":"content:pale-architecture","name":"How Pale fits together","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/collections/systems/making-a-working-world-intelligible/architecture/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":7,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/the-record-and-the-arrow/#article","identifier":"content:monad-the-record-and-the-arrow","name":"The record and the arrow","url":"https://causality.in/blog/the-record-and-the-arrow/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":8,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/causal-inference-primer/#article","identifier":"content:causality-causal-inference-primer","name":"Did the cache make it faster?","url":"https://causality.in/collections/evidence-and-evaluation/from-observation-to-evaluation/causal-inference-primer/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":9,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/entropy-and-a-learning-mind/#article","identifier":"content:causality-entropy-and-a-learning-mind","name":"Entropy and a learning mind","url":"https://causality.in/blog/entropy-and-a-learning-mind/"}}],"itemListOrder":"https://schema.org/ItemListOrderDescending"},"description":"Writing that discusses or references Reckon across the connected publications.","text":"","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/mentions/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:reckon","name":"Reckon","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux","description":"Measure what memory changes for its cost, and compare proposed improvements before deciding to use them.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon","targetProduct":{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-reckon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Reckon","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/understand-the-memory-laboratory/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-docs","name":"Understand the memory laboratory","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/understand-the-memory-laboratory/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/mentions/","identifier":"content:reckon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Reckon mentions","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/reckon/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/reckon/overview","name":"Reckon"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/understand-the-memory-laboratory/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-docs","@type":"TechArticle","name":"Understand the memory laboratory","headline":"Understand the memory laboratory","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"keywords":["memory retention policies","memory laboratory","resource budgets","record contribution","which memory mattered"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/understand-the-memory-laboratory/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Understand the memory laboratory","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/understand-the-memory-laboratory/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Reckon","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Understand the memory laboratory","item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/understand-the-memory-laboratory/"}}]}]},"description":"Choose a task, compare memory policies and read the saved results.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"text":"Understand the memory laboratory\nA classifier can learn a new task and get worse at an earlier one. Keeping examples to practise again may help, but memory has a cost. How many examples should it keep, and which ones should it give up when it runs out of room?\nReckon lets you compare those choices. Compare two replay budgets starts with one classifier, the same training data and room for either 2 or 16 examples. It takes you from running the comparison to reading the saved accuracy matrices. If you already have a run, follow a record through its event log instead.\nImportant\nLocal setup. These guides use the local reckon source checkout and its Python environment. Follow Set up the environment if you need that prerequisite, then return to the comparison. The laboratory works locally.\nChoose what memory has to do\nRemembering an early clue and practising an old skill put different demands on memory. Choose a task that presents the difficulty you want to study:\nTask\tMemory challenge\t\nKey and door\tRemember an early cue until it becomes useful\t\nHidden-mode bandit\tRespond when the reward-generating mode changes\t\nContinual classification\tReplay earlier examples while learning a new task\t\nTutoring simulation\tRetain information about a simulated learner's misconceptions\t\nYou can change how much memory the task receives, or change the rule that chooses what survives. Keep the other choice fixed when you want to understand its effect. In the replay example, both buffers discard the oldest retained example first, so the comparison asks about room for examples rather than which removal rule is better.\nThe completed retention-policy comparison instead tests several rules on Tutor, Continual, scripted Conversation and TextWorld. It reports the controls and the cost of the extra experiments used to estimate record effects. Read those results when choosing a workload or deciding whether a more expensive rule is worth testing.\nFollow a record\nThe task decides what to remember. In the key-and-door task, it sees a colour near the start and writes that colour as a cue. At the door, it asks for the cue again. Reckon keeps the record's content and ID, its resource cost, where it came from and the scores a retention rule can use.\nThe saved log lets you follow the write and the later read. It can also show score updates and the removal that eventually makes room for another record. Inspect an exported run follows a real blue cue through those four events. The raw log does not contain everything about the record; richer exports can supply record details and relationships for the debugger.\nGetting a record back is only part of the story. The task still has to use it to make a useful choice. To find out whether the record helped, compare the outcome with and without it. A write establishes that something was stored, a read that it was returned, and an outcome comparison tests what difference its availability made.\nWhy a good result can lower a score\nAfter using retrieved records, the task reports an outcome through observe(). Reckon shares credit among those records and blends each share of the outcome into the record's previous utility. With the default update rate, the calculation is:\nnew utility = 0.9 × old utility + 0.1 × credit × outcome\nThe result stays within the configured bounds. Suppose a record's utility was 0.5, its credit is 0.33 and the outcome is 1. Its new utility is 0.483. The episode went well, but the record's assigned share was below its old estimate, so its score fell.\nPython's observe() shares credit equally by default. Shaped credit instead turns the current utilities into positive weights and normalises them. Daemon callers choose their credit policy explicitly. These rules assign a share; they do not measure each record's individual effect. The memory-manager guide explains the alternatives and their inputs.\nAn early record may matter long after its last use, while a recent one contributes nothing. A score matters for survival only if the retention rule uses it. FIFO removes the oldest record and LRU the least recently accessed one. Importance uses a score supplied by the caller; learned utility ranks records by updated utility per byte. That remains a per-byte ranking even when a slot limit forces the removal.\nThe replay tutorial uses FIFO. It still updates utilities, but those updates do not change which examples FIFO removes. Its one episode ends after the utility update. When a run carries memory into later episodes, surviving records can be retrieved and scored again. To compare the removal rules themselves, use the policy comparison with matched tasks, limits and seeds.\nTest what made the difference\nStart with the task's outcome: accuracy for the replay comparison, or reward for a task that reports reward. Keep the settings, results and activity log together. The log helps explain what happened during the run, but counting writes and removals does not tell you how well the task performed. The debugger's default frontier uses writes divided by writes plus removals and labels it as an operation proxy; read task accuracy from the saved result.\nTo find out whether remembering a cue helped the task, compare its outcome with and without that record. Reckon's evaluator waits until the first read that could return the chosen instance. It checks that both runs reached the same memory state and read boundary, then verifies that the record was actually removed in one of them. The task may later write the same fact again, so the comparison tests losing that instance rather than permanently forbidding the information. The evaluation guide explains how to set up the comparison and handle a target it cannot test.\nChoosing what to discard raises another question: is it worth running extra trials to estimate which records matter? The measured retention policy copies the retained memory into fresh task episodes and compares outcomes with and without a candidate under a declared reference policy. Positive removal estimates contribute to the utility ranking; the report keeps the signed estimates, including zero and negative ones. Judge the resulting task performance alongside the extra trials and time needed to make those choices.\nThose fresh episodes do not tell you what a different removal would do at the current write. For that comparison, the Conversation, MiniGrid and TextWorld notebooks can reconstruct the actual episode prefix and earlier removals, then compare removing a candidate with removing FIFO's choice. Both continuations use FIFO afterwards. The notebook guide explains this path and the separate record-removal experiments, including how the controller and continuation rule limit the result.\nWhichever comparison you choose, report the outcome difference, its uncertainty and the measurement cost. To call the difference causal, explain what changed, what stayed fixed and which assumptions make the comparison informative. The cache example works through that reasoning.\nCases, evaluations and decisions beyond the laboratory\nReckon's wider design for cases, evaluations and decisions is the work in front: it preserves an experiment's inputs, compares a proposed change and keeps the decision its owner makes from the result, and none of it is built yet.\nHow Reckon relates to Scope and HENA\nScope asks which permitted context helps a task. A passage being retrieved or quoted does not answer that question. Reckon tests a related one under a memory budget: what changes when a record or retention rule changes? A result on one workload can help Scope design a comparison for its own tasks; Scope still has to test what works there.\nHENA keeps its own swarm store and decides what to keep. It may keep it behind Reckon's record-store interface, designed and not yet published, so that Reckon can inspect and evaluate its records without HENA calling Reckon. Scope keeps only context derived from what Pale and Weave hold, and in the design requests evaluations through Pale.\nWorking with the code\nThe developer manual covers building the Python/Rust library and extending the laboratory. The comparison and event-log guides do not need the daemon.\nCaution\nThe daemon has no caller authentication. If you use it separately, keep it on a trusted local machine. It listens only on loopback and uses plaintext.\nThe daemon's ExportRun returns the latest record snapshots observed by a named run. For an event history in the debugger, use the logs saved by Python or a notebook: ExportRun does not include events, metrics or a complete evaluation bundle.\n","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/understand-the-memory-laboratory/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"copyrightHolder":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}],"about":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:reckon","name":"Reckon","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux","description":"Measure what memory changes for its cost, and compare proposed improvements before deciding to use them.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon","targetProduct":{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/reckon/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-reckon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Reckon","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/understand-the-memory-laboratory/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-docs","name":"Understand the memory laboratory","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/understand-the-memory-laboratory/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/mentions/","identifier":"content:reckon-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Reckon mentions","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/reckon/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/reckon/overview","name":"Reckon"}]}],"mentions":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject","identifier":"project:hena","name":"HENA","description":"A research project on how small reasoning units learn to solve a task together, the collective that the Causality programme works towards.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/#entity"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/#brand"}],"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/","identifier":"content:causality-project-hena","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA","url":"https://causality.in/hena/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/#article","identifier":"content:hena-docs","name":"How units form a collective","url":"https://causality.in/hena/how-units-form-a-collective/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/","identifier":"content:hena-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"HENA mentions","url":"https://causality.in/hena/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","url":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/HENA/blob/main/paper/HENA.tex","name":"HENA: Hierarchically Evolving Neural Automata","about":{"@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity"}},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://docs.causality.in/hena/overview","name":"HENA"}]},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:pale","name":"Pale","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux","description":"See which review instructions apply in a repository and what would change before you install them. Pale compiles those instructions and permissions from one workspace.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#brand"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale","targetProduct":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/pale/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/","identifier":"content:pale-project","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Pale","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/understand-your-world/#article","identifier":"content:pale-docs","name":"Introduction to Pale","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/understand-your-world/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/mentions/","identifier":"content:pale-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Pale mentions","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.rawthoughts.in/pale/overview"}]},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","identifier":"project:scope","name":"Scope","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux","description":"The smallest fresh account of a task, with its sources, decisions, people and open questions intact.","http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#brand"},"http://purl.org/dc/terms/relation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/scope","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","codeRepository":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/scope","targetProduct":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/#entity"},"license":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/scope/blob/main/LICENSE"},"citation":{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/scope/blob/main/CITATION.cff"},"author":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"copyrightHolder":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization"}},"subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/","identifier":"content:scope-introduction","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Scope","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/context-for-a-task/#article","identifier":"content:scope-docs","name":"Context for a task","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/context-for-a-task/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/mentions/","identifier":"content:scope-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Scope mentions","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/scope/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.rawthoughts.in/scope/overview"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/","identifier":"content:causality-project-reckon","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Reckon","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/research/","identifier":"content:causality-projects","@type":"WebPage","name":"Research projects","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"description":"The calculus, local units, collective architectures and measurement tools behind Causality’s research programme.","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://causality.in/collections/"},{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://post-agi-economics.org/"},{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/products/"},{"@type":"ViewAction","target":"https://rawthoughts.in/services/"}],"text":"A dense constellation of points and fine connecting lines.\nMONAD\nAsk which distinctions survive when you compress or change a description. MONAD develops the mathematics in Lean, with a paper and a small Rust kernel.\nA dense constellation of points and fine connecting lines.\nMUON\nDesign a common interface for one reasoning unit, so a stock model and a unit that learns from experience can be compared on the same work.\nA dense constellation of points and fine connecting lines.\nReckon\nRun the same task at two memory budgets and see what changes. The local lab saves outcomes and shows which records were written, read or dropped.\nA dense constellation of points and fine connecting lines.\nHENA\nAsk whether small reasoning units can organise around a task when they can send only a few messages and keep only some of their past work.\nA dense constellation of points and fine connecting lines.\nQ-MUON\nExplore what a quantum implementation could change inside one reasoning unit while keeping MUON’s external interface.\nA dense constellation of points and fine connecting lines.\nQ-HENA\nExplore how quantum units might communicate, route work and share memory through entanglement. The design carries over HENA’s collective interfaces.\nFurther questions\nIntelligence in a working system\nReasoning has to meet the work around it. RawThoughts’ products make one system of that work, with people deciding. Pale is the meta harness through which they act, a harness over the tools people already use; it carries a goal forward through people, agents and applications, with useful context and the decisions that need a person kept in view.\nExplore the systems\nWhat cheaper intelligence changes\nAs cognitive work gets cheaper, constraints can move to energy, ownership and the institutions around production. Post-AGI Economics follows those consequences for firms, households and the models used to understand them.\nExplore the economic inquiry\nThe questions behind the models\nSmall examples can reveal what a description loses, what a collective can learn and what an evaluation actually establishes. Follow these questions through the programme’s connected writing.\nExplore the thoughts\nQuestions about the programme\nHow do these projects fit together?\nCausality asks how a system can change its description and learn its own organisation, and these projects take the question in parts. MONAD asks what a change in description preserves. MUON studies one reasoning unit, HENA studies how units work together, and Reckon tests what memory contributes to a task. Q-MUON and Q-HENA ask related questions about a quantum unit and a quantum collective. Each page says what exists today.\nWhere should I begin?\nFor a small mathematical example, follow a part from stock count to repair: knowing it is available does not tell you whether it fits. To try an experiment, give a classifier two different replay budgets and compare how well it remembers its earlier tasks.\n","image":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://causality.in/assets/content/causality/media/relation-field/dark-0.png","description":"A dense constellation of points and fine connecting lines."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://causality.in/assets/content/causality/media/relation-field/dark-0.png","description":"A dense constellation of points and fine connecting lines."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://causality.in/assets/content/causality/media/relation-field/dark-0.png","description":"A dense constellation of points and fine connecting lines."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://causality.in/assets/content/causality/media/relation-field/dark-0.png","description":"A dense constellation of points and fine connecting lines."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://causality.in/assets/content/causality/media/relation-field/dark-0.png","description":"A dense constellation of points and fine connecting lines."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://causality.in/assets/content/causality/media/relation-field/dark-0.png","description":"A dense constellation of points and fine connecting lines."}],"url":"https://causality.in/research/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"hasPart":[{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":6,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"MONAD","item":{"url":"https://causality.in/monad/","@id":"https://causality.in/monad/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"MUON","item":{"url":"https://causality.in/muon/","@id":"https://causality.in/muon/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Reckon","item":{"url":"https://causality.in/reckon/","@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/#entity","@type":"SoftwareApplication","name":"Reckon","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS, Linux"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":4,"name":"HENA","item":{"url":"https://causality.in/hena/","@id":"https://causality.in/hena/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":5,"name":"Q-MUON","item":{"url":"https://causality.in/q-muon/","@id":"https://causality.in/q-muon/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":6,"name":"Q-HENA","item":{"url":"https://causality.in/q-hena/","@id":"https://causality.in/q-hena/#entity","@type":"ResearchProject"}}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://causality.in/search/","identifier":"content:causality-search","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Search","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://causality.in/terms/"},"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy":{"@type":"http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity","http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline/gazette-theme-lattice"}]},"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":15,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/recovering-a-distinction-from-a-noisy-answer/#article","identifier":"content:monad-a-noisy-answer-is-not-an-exact-repair","name":"A noisy answer is not an exact repair","url":"https://causality.in/collections/changing-descriptions/preservation-under-uncertainty/recovering-a-distinction-from-a-noisy-answer/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/about/","identifier":"content:causality-about","name":"About Causality","url":"https://causality.in/about/","@type":"AboutPage"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/beyond-the-mirage/#article","identifier":"content:causality-beyond-the-mirage","name":"Beyond the Mirage: a framework for AGI classification and controllable progress","url":"https://causality.in/blog/beyond-the-mirage/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":4,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/blog/","identifier":"collection:causality-essays","name":"Blog","url":"https://causality.in/blog/","@type":"CollectionPage"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":5,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/reckon/cases-evaluations-and-decisions/#article","identifier":"content:reckon-cases-evaluations-and-decisions","name":"Cases, evaluations and decisions","url":"https://causality.in/reckon/cases-evaluations-and-decisions/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":6,"item":{"@id":"https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/changing-the-rule/#article","identifier":"content:causality-change-the-rule-not-the-judge","name":"Ch